//! Product requirements, feature contracts and architecture decisions.

#[doc = "# Mealshop\n\nRecipes, informed nutrition, shopping and family meal planning. **Early development:** a\nRust HTTP server includes a curated ingredient catalogue (F02/F23, accepted in PRs #4/#5),\nplus local recipe authoring/persistence (F04/F18) and a native Nextcloud client (F22).\nThe broader product features below remain planned.\n\n[![CI](https://github.com/Th3Link/mealshop/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/Th3Link/mealshop/actions/workflows/ci.yml)\n[![Audit](https://github.com/Th3Link/mealshop/actions/workflows/audit.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/Th3Link/mealshop/actions/workflows/audit.yml)\n[Documentation](https://th3link.github.io/mealshop/) · [Feature specs](../page_specs_readme_md/index.html) ·\n[Requirements](../page_docs_requirements_md/index.html) · [ADRs](../page_docs_adr_index_md/index.html)\n\n## Vision\n- Ingredient database, precise configurable units, scalable recipes, macro- and micronutrients\n  per serving, and evidence-backed explanations of beneficial food components.\n- Shared recipes and later paid recipes/collections; source-link-only sharing for imported recipes.\n- Shopping lists with recipe provenance, extra items, preferred shops, learned aisle order and barcodes.\n- Family meal plans with attendance, intolerances, preferences, cooking time and ongoing feedback.\n- Guided cooking with preset timers, multi-day bread/pizza schedules, and later brewing/fermentation.\n- Inventory, offers, customer-operated link imports and MCP recipe tools.\n\n## Run the ingredient and nutrition demo\nInstall stable Rust (declared minimum: 1.85), then:\n```sh\ncargo run --locked\ncurl --fail http://127.0.0.1:3000/health\n```\nThe response is `ok` plus a newline. `MEALSHOP_LISTEN_ADDR` overrides `127.0.0.1:3000`.\nOpen [the demo](http://127.0.0.1:3000/) to calculate recipe/serving nutrients and inspect\nsources, unknowns and component evidence. The English-base catalogue contains 313 ingredients\nwith German names: 284 source-backed BLS/USDA profiles and 29 explicitly unknown product identities.\n[Data and licences](https://github.com/Th3Link/mealshop/blob/main/data/README.md) · [Demo, API and custom catalogue guide](../page_docs_guides_nutrition_md/index.html).\nRecipes can be created, saved, loaded and revised using catalogue ingredients; per-serving\nestimates retain their saved source snapshots. The executable stores data in `var/recipes`\n(or `MEALSHOP_RECIPE_DIR`) and keeps the default local/demo mode loopback-only. Nextcloud uses the authenticated transport described below.\n[Recipe API, storage and recovery guide](../page_docs_guides_recipes_md/index.html).\nUse Ctrl-C for graceful shutdown. A saved recipe can be previewed at another serving count. Optional protected local household profiles use an operator token; see [household setup and limits](../page_docs_guides_household_md/index.html). Standalone hosted authentication remains future work. Tests: `cargo test --locked`. Full gate: [development guide](../page_docs_guides_development_md/index.html).\n\nThe current browser surface is a pragmatic test demo, not the product UI. It supports German\nand English interface/quantity presentation; untranslated source content stays explicitly original.\nA native Vue 3 Nextcloud client now covers shared-household recipes, profiles, planning and the catalogue ([setup and verification](../page_docs_guides_nextcloud_md/index.html)). The standalone Vue shell remains planned ([F30](../page_specs_0030_standalone_vue_client_specification_md/index.html));\nfull cross-client localization is tracked in [F29](../page_specs_0029_cross_client_localization_specification_md/index.html).\n\n## Deployment and business model (planned)\nRust/Tokio/Axum server, standalone or as a Nextcloud `ExApp`; Flutter client connecting to an\nown server or our hosted service. Storage options: Git-backed files, PostgreSQL, SQLite and\nMariaDB. Nextcloud installations remain free. Hosted accounts have free and paid tiers.\nThe distributed app costs a few euros; self-compilation and installation remain possible.\nOnly paid, signed app releases are intended to connect to our operated service; technical\nadmission and licensing details need validation (ADR 0004). Future federation may exchange\nrecipes and compatible meal plans between independent servers.\n\n## Contribute\nStart with [AGENTS.md](https://github.com/Th3Link/mealshop/blob/main/AGENTS.md), the [roadmap](../page_docs_roadmap_md/index.html) and an affected feature spec.\nThe repository uses spec-driven development inspired by Curvyo and CI adapted from\nhomeautomation; exact source revisions and adaptation notes are in [provenance](../page_docs_provenance_md/index.html).\n\n## License\nAGPL-3.0-only. See [LICENSE](https://github.com/Th3Link/mealshop/blob/main/LICENSE). Paid distribution does not remove recipients' rights\nto use, modify or redistribute the software under that licence.\n\nProtected family planning links saved recipe revisions with household constraints, preparation\nbudgets and consented calendar attendance imports. Changes have an explicit shopping-quantity\npreview before apply. See the [family planning guide](../page_docs_guides_meal_planning_md/index.html).\n\nNative household shopping lists offer searchable item tiles, inline exact-quantity entry and last-check\nundo while preserving recipe/plan contributions and purchased receipts in item details.\nChoose shops, set manual order or opt into local session learning; packaged products support manual\nGTIN entry, optional supported-camera scanning and consented Open Food Facts lookup.\n[Shopping guide](../page_docs_guides_shopping_md/index.html) · [source/provenance evidence](../page_specs_0006_shopping_list_validation_md/index.html) ·\n[visual shopping](../page_specs_0032_visual_shopping_specification_md/index.html).\n\nNative recipe reading/editing uses compact, always-open parts and ingredient plus actions.\nExplicit homemade ingredients keep their finished quantities while measured base inputs alone feed\nnutrition and shopping. [Recipe guide](../page_docs_guides_recipes_md/index.html) ·\n[F33 contract](../page_specs_0033_compact_recipe_preparation_specification_md/index.html).\n\nNative guided cooking now saves independent preset timers and reviewed multi-day baking schedules.\nPersonal Nextcloud reminders retain delivery uncertainty and deadlines across restart.\n[Cooking guide](../page_docs_guides_cooking_md/index.html) · [F12 evidence](../page_specs_0012_guided_cooking_validation_md/index.html) ·\n[F13 evidence](../page_specs_0013_bread_schedules_validation_md/index.html).\n\nF34 adds explicitly labelled comparison envelopes alongside immutable reported composition. All 313\nbundled foods and 73 organizational groups have energy/protein/fat/carbohydrate coverage; unsupported\nindividual nutrients remain unknown. Recipe totals include exact source readings and comparative\nranges, with outward rounding and visible incomplete subtotals. Own declared ranges are supported.\nSee [F34](../page_specs_0034_evidence_nutrition_ranges_specification_md/index.html) and\n[the evidence protocol](https://github.com/Th3Link/mealshop/blob/main/data/README.md).\n\nNative collections organize ordered, explicitly pinned recipe revisions. Household owners can grant\nread access to Nextcloud accounts or whole households; incoming invitations and content appear in\nShared with me. Imported recipes expose only their original source URL to recipients, including\nwhen an older revision was shared before its origin was corrected.\n[Sharing and collections guide](../page_docs_guides_sharing_md/index.html) · [F05](../page_specs_0005_sharing_collections_specification_md/index.html).\n\nCompleted cooking history records actual attendees, optional reported effort and household/profile\nratings. Owner-controlled local learning starts fresh on opt-in/reset, explains suggestion evidence\nand keeps the planner's hard exclusions and explicit review. [History and preferences](../page_docs_guides_history_and_preferences_md/index.html).\n\nHousehold assistant tools now offer explicitly granted MCP ingredient/recipe search, canonical previews\nand confirmed, idempotent recipe creation through existing Nextcloud authentication. Imported content\nremains source-only. [Connection, permissions and recovery](../page_docs_guides_mcp_md/index.html).\n\nNative reviewed recipe import fetches supported public structured sources after explicit consent,\nretains original fields separately, and offers manual private entry when retrieval is blocked.\nIngredient/quantity review is mandatory and imported sharing remains source-link-only.\n[Import, source boundaries and recovery](../page_docs_guides_recipe_import_md/index.html).\n\nHousehold inventory records exact lots, reviewed shopping reservations, explicit purchased/completed-cooking\nbookings, reversals and manually entered shop offers. [Inventory guide](../page_docs_guides_inventory_md/index.html) ·\n[F15 evidence](../page_specs_0015_inventory_offers_validation_md/index.html).\n\nNative brewing adds source-linked BIAB/all-grain/extract/partial-mash and named/DIY equipment templates,\nplus independently researched cider, white/red wine and traditional mead processes,\nimmutable brew recipes, reviewed independent batches, physical equipment reservations, personal reminders\nand exact instrument/manual reading history. [Brewing guide](../page_docs_guides_brewing_md/index.html) ·\n[F14 research](../page_specs_0014_brewing_fermentation_research_md/index.html).\n\nThe native Android client starts in [F35](../page_specs_0035_flutter_mobile_client_specification_md/index.html).\n[Five device-testable implementation PRs](../page_specs_0035_flutter_mobile_client_plan_md/index.html) reconcile the\n[customer mobile pack](https://github.com/Th3Link/mealshop/blob/main/references/mobile-product-spec-2026-11-12/README.md) with existing APIs.\nP1 implements Nextcloud Login Flow v2, shared shopping and a protected durable offline outbox.\nSee the [mobile guide](../page_docs_guides_mobile_md/index.html) for the pinned emulator, APKs and upgrade instructions,\nand F35 verification for actual evidence and physical-handset testing limits.\n"]
pub mod page_readme_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0001: stack and delivery\n\nStatus: Accepted for bootstrap; future integrations planned\nDate: 2026-11-07\n\n## Context\nThe customer requests Rust/Axum, a Flutter app, Nextcloud `ExApp` deployment and the homeautomation CI baseline.\n\n## Decision\nUse a single Rust package with Tokio/Axum and a small reusable router plus executable. Declare Rust 1.85 (edition 2024 baseline) and verify it in CI. Ship only /health now. Publish embedded project docs through rustdoc and Pages. Adapt the upstream workflows to a host binary, keeping build, coverage, MSRV, SPDX SBOM, audit, licences, Renovate and size metrics.\n\n## Alternatives\nSeparate crates now would add unused boundaries. A different backend or frontend stack would contradict the request.\n\n## Consequences and open questions\nFlutter, Nextcloud `AppAPI` authentication and packaging need later integration specs. A healthy process is not proof of database readiness. Metrics are binary size, not latency or nutrition accuracy.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0001_stack_and_delivery_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0002: quantities and nutrition\n\nStatus: Proposed implementation; customer unit preferences accepted\nDate: 2026-11-07\n\n## Context\nRecipes accept varied input while the customer wants only grams/kilograms in personal views. Nutrition and educational effects require provenance.\n\n## Decision\nRetain exact decimal input and unit, dimension, source and conversion context. Normalize mass to grams, volume to millilitres and counts to pieces within their own dimensions. Convert across dimensions only with ingredient/state-specific density or piece mass. Keep serving scaling rational/decimal until display. Persist source values and conversion versions for reproducibility. Nutrition per serving includes units, reference basis, source, missingness and uncertainty. Educational component notes carry evidence and conditions.\n\n## Alternatives\nStoring everything as grams loses volume/count meaning and needs unavailable densities. Bare binary floating point and universal teaspoon conversions obscure precision. Preserve-only-without-normalization complicates aggregation.\n\n## Consequences and open questions\nNumeric library/scale, regional spoon standards, nutrient provider and cooking retention factors are unresolved. No conversion is fabricated. Display preferences never rewrite recipe source data.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0002_quantities_and_nutrition_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0003: persistence and data boundaries\n\nStatus: Customer storage targets accepted; schema and adapters proposed\nDate: 2026-11-07\n\n## Context\nThe same household data must work in Git/files, PostgreSQL, SQLite and MariaDB, standalone or in Nextcloud.\n\n## Decision\nDefine versioned domain records with stable identities, explicit provenance, household ownership and recipe revisions before an adapter. Implement adapters incrementally with shared behavioural fixtures. Keep Git history/merge semantics separate from SQL transactions. Require backup/export/import validation and conflict handling. Do not select an ORM or create a generic repository trait in the bootstrap.\n\n## Alternatives\nSQL-only would exclude Git/files. A single SQL dialect assumed across all engines is unverified. Git does not substitute for authorization or concurrent-write control.\n\n## Consequences and open questions\nFirst adapter, offline sync, encryption, attachment storage and transaction boundaries need feature-level design. Plans/purchases/stock movements must not silently overwrite one another.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0003_persistence_and_data_boundaries_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0004: licensing hosting and sharing\n\nStatus: AGPL and business goals accepted; admission/commerce/federation proposed\nDate: 2026-11-07\n\n## Context\nThe customer requests AGPL-3.0 software, free Nextcloud installations, paid official app distribution, optional self-builds, hosted tiers and later paid content/federation.\n\n## Decision\nLicense project code AGPL-3.0-only with complete source. Treat app purchase/hosted service entitlement as service policy, separate from software rights. Validate platform attestation plus server-side account entitlement before implementing official-client admission; never rely on a secret in an open client. Preserve source-link-only sharing on every output path for imported recipes. Purchased recipe content needs a separate explicit content licence and provenance. Federation is opt-in and later.\n\n## Alternatives\nObscuring source or prohibiting AGPL redistribution conflicts with the stated licence. A signature alone does not establish account payment or prevent all custom clients. Republishing imported content contradicts the customer sharing restriction.\n\n## Consequences and open questions\nPlatform feasibility, AGPL compliance of admission terms, content rights, refunds/tax and federation trust remain gates before commercial launch. No restriction on self-hosting or self-building is introduced by this bootstrap.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0004_licensing_hosting_and_sharing_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0005: privacy and learning\n\nStatus: Proposed safeguards for customer-requested learning\nDate: 2026-11-07\n\n## Context\nMeal planning combines household attendance, dietary constraints, ratings and shopping behaviour. These can reveal sensitive personal information.\n\n## Decision\nScope records and MCP tools to authorized households. Separate hard exclusions from soft preferences; unknown allergen data needs review. Store check-off/cooking/rating events with correction and deletion support. Start from explainable rules; evaluate opt-in incremental learning against that baseline. Keep manual overrides, history reset and opt-out. Calendar and external lookups disclose what leaves the server and request appropriate consent.\n\n## Alternatives\nUnconstrained collaborative training could expose health information or override exclusions. Cold-start ML alone has no useful evidence. Silent calendar writes exceed the request.\n\n## Consequences and open questions\nAuthentication, retention, model choice, external APIs and consent UX remain open. Imported text/remote MCP content is untrusted data, not executable instructions.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0005_privacy_and_learning_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0006: durable process schedules\n\nStatus: Proposed shared process model\nDate: 2026-11-07\n\n## Context\nCooking, multi-day dough and brewing need timers, dependencies, phase reminders and equipment-specific variants.\n\n## Decision\nModel a process instance as versioned steps with dependencies, active/passive durations, target windows, equipment and persisted deadlines/completion. A server scheduler dispatches idempotent reminders with acknowledgement state. Clients show delivery limits and recover deadlines on reconnect. Preview downstream effects when delays or target times change. Store absolute instants with intended local timezone for civil-time scheduling.\n\n## Alternatives\nA transient in-app countdown cannot survive restarts or multi-day schedules. A separate scheduler per food type duplicates dependency logic. Automatic equipment control is beyond the request.\n\n## Consequences and open questions\nNotification providers, offline authority, retries and daylight-saving semantics need tests. Fermentation duration and notification delivery remain uncertain; no guaranteed outcome or safety claim.\n\n## Native Nextcloud implementation\nF12/F13 delivery decisions and acceptance evidence are now recorded in\n[ADR 0016](../page_docs_adr_0016_durable_cooking_and_civil_time_md/index.html). This earlier shared proposal remains\nopen for brewing, other clients and notification transports outside that verified scope.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0006_durable_process_schedules_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0007: localization and web clients\n\nStatus: Customer direction accepted; product integration details proposed\nDate: 2026-11-07\n\n## Context\nAfter merging PR #4, the German-speaking customer requested internationalization and\nexplicitly classified the existing browser workbench as a pure demo. Further demo design\ninvestment should stay pragmatic; substantial UX/UI work should be handed to a dedicated\nUX/UI agent when the customer chooses to involve one. Nextcloud and the standalone server\nneed product web clients, with Vue 3 as the preferred low-friction direction. Flutter remains\nthe separate app target from the original brief.\n\n## Decision\n- Keep `src/web/` a disposable developer/test demo, visibly labelled as such. Use it to try\n  domain/API behaviour; do not develop a product design system or Nextcloud shell there.\n- Introduce F21 for cross-cutting i18n: interface messages, localized ingredient/content names,\n  language negotiation/fallback, number/date/plural presentation, exact locale-aware quantity\n  entry and stable language-independent identities/contracts. Start with German and English.\n- This change adds a small de/en demo message catalogue, record-owned ingredient\n  display names, exact decimal formatting/input and explicit original-language content fallback.\n  It does not introduce a production translation service or localize stored nutrient values.\n- Plan F22 for Vue 3 product web clients: a Nextcloud-hosted shell using compatible Nextcloud\n  components and AppAPI-aware asset/API routing, and a standalone server web shell. Share\n  domain API contracts and translation semantics, while letting host navigation/auth differ.\n- Choose concrete packages, compatibility targets and component-sharing boundaries when F22\n  becomes Ready. Do not install Vue/build tooling or start a framework migration in this slice.\n\n## Evidence and alternatives\nNextcloud's [JavaScript standards](https://docs.nextcloud.com/server/stable/developer_manual/getting_started/coding_standards/javascript.html)\nrecommend Vue and its provided components for UI consistency. The\n[ExApp overview](https://docs.nextcloud.com/server/stable/developer_manual/exapp_development/development_overview/ExAppOverview.html)\ndescribes a Vue frontend with proxy-aware loading paths. These support the requested direction;\nthey do not prove compatibility of the current root-relative demo with a Nextcloud installation.\n\nGrowing the demo into the product would mix a debugging surface with host integration and\nconsume design effort the customer explicitly does not want spent now. Starting two full web\nprojects immediately would add unrequested implementation before the UX and integration specs.\n\n## Consequences\nF02 is accepted through merged PR #4; its browser surface remains demo-only. F21/F22 retain\nseparate completion criteria. The first i18n slice leaves source metadata, uncertainty and\neducational content in the original language with an explicit label when no reviewed\ntranslation exists. Full versioned ingredient/recipe/content translations are later F21 work.\nThe demo may remain framework-free or be removed once product clients provide equivalent\nreview flows. A UX/UI agent is not spawned by this decision; the customer decides when to involve one.\n\n## Customer-approved scope update (2026-11-10)\nF21/F22 now close the verified Nextcloud scope. F29/F30 retain their original full acceptance\ncontracts verbatim for remaining content, standalone and Flutter work. No requirement was dropped\nor declared implemented merely by moving it. ADR 0012 records the actual native integration.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0007_localization_and_web_clients_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0008: curated ingredient sources and English-base identities\n\nStatus: Customer scope accepted; import/model details proposed\nDate: 2026-11-07\n\n## Context\nThe customer requested a broad kitchen catalogue and clarified that everything coded needs\nan English base plus identifiers for language changes. German flour types and dairy grades\nmust be distinguished rather than approximated by generic US foods. BLS 4.0 provides native\nEnglish/German names and detailed component provenance; USDA complements it.\n\n## Decision\n- Canonical ingredient IDs remain stable English slugs; category IDs are English identifiers.\n  `name`, `aliases` and `form` are the English base. Optional `translations.<language>` contains\n  localized names/aliases/forms, keyed by ingredient identity and field. English remains the base,\n  never an overriding entry in the translation map. Old schema-1 files default to English/other\n  category with no translations, so existing operator fixtures remain loadable.\n- Curate exact source-record selections in a separate manifest. Commit selected source facts,\n  original descriptions, nutrient units, acquisition methods/references, source sheet/row or numeric\n  JSON tokens and archive/workbook hashes. Regenerate the runtime catalogue deterministically.\n- BLS source data retain CC-BY-4.0 attribution to Max Rubner-Institut, version 4.0 (2025), DOI\n  10.25826/Data20251217-134202-0. USDA SR Legacy data retain CC0/public-domain status. Software\n  stays AGPL-3.0-only; provider licences are not relabelled as AGPL.\n- Runtime amounts support six decimal places. Imported source representations are rounded once\n  to that precision using half-up normalization; original cell/token strings remain in snapshots.\n  Missing, negative/unrepresentable and below-resolution positive values remain unknown, not zero.\n- BLS `CHO` maps to `available_carbohydrate`; it does not populate USDA `carbohydrate` (by difference).\n  BLS `FOLFD` and USDA nutrient 1187 map to `food_folate`; total folate/DFE stay separate. Units and\n  basis are checked before importing. No energy, fibre or carbohydrate value is inferred from another.\n- Unresolved product identities exist with no nutrient profile. Do not substitute related flour,\n  a neighbouring fat grade, a brand formula or a generic whey reference for the requested variant.\n- The demo chooses language by message IDs and translation fields; API numbers remain dot-decimal.\n  Additive error/warning codes support localized presentation without matching English sentences.\n\n## Alternatives\nUsing only USDA would miss German flour types and fat grades. Importing all foods without\nreview would add composite dishes and ambiguous mappings. Assigning plausible averages would\nhide source gaps. Mixing available and by-difference carbohydrate would produce misleading totals.\n\n## Consequences\nThe curated catalogue contains 313 items; 29 intentionally remain identity-only. The current\n17-definition nutrient registry is a subset of the providers' data, not exhaustive coverage.\nThe file-based demo catalogue is close to its pre-existing 1 MiB validation bound; future wider\ncoverage belongs with explicit storage/loading work rather than an unreviewed bound increase.\nFull versioned content translations and production language negotiation remain F21 work.\n\n## Primary sources\n- [BLS 4.0 download and licence](https://blsdb.de/download).\n- [Max Rubner-Institut BLS overview](https://www.mri.bund.de/de/institute/ernaehrungsverhalten/forschungsbereiche/bundeslebensmittelschluessel-bls/).\n- [USDA downloads](https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/download-datasets/) and [API/data licence](https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/api-guide/).\n\n## F34 amendment\nADR 0019 adds separate source-backed comparison envelopes and explicit own ranges without\nreplacing original primary readings or inferring dietary safety. The enriched catalogue has a\nbounded 2 MiB loader envelope; the independent 2 MiB recipe publication/restart guard remains.\nHistorical evidence above describes the decision at that time. See\n[F34](../page_specs_0034_evidence_nutrition_ranges_specification_md/index.html) for scope and current status.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0008_curated_ingredient_sources_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0009: local recipe revisions and pinned nutrition inputs\n\nStatus: Proposed implementation for the customer-authorized recipe slice; review pending\nDate: 2026-11-07\n\n## Context\nThe customer accepted PR #5 and requested creating/saving recipes with catalogue ingredients\nand nutrition per serving. F04 needs a usable persistence slice before the broader F18 adapters,\nauthentication, Git synchronization or product clients are ready.\n\n## Decision\nStart with a dedicated local directory, one household per server, without additional crates.\nEach immutable schema-1 JSON revision includes a stable recipe ID, monotonically increasing\nrevision, author-supplied title/language/author, exact gram strings, batch serving count,\npreparation flag, ordered instructions with active/passive seconds, and the full referenced\ncatalogue subset/version. An empty instruction list is allowed for drafts. User text is\nretained in its declared en/de language; coded fields/messages remain English identifiers.\nAuthor is attribution text, not an authenticated identity. This slice supports locally authored\nrecipes only; imports and their sharing constraints remain F16.\n\nPOST creates a recipe; PUT requires the expected latest revision. GET retrieves latest or\nan explicit historical revision; listing returns latest summaries. Nutrition is calculated\nfrom the saved snapshot, retaining unknowns, provenance and definition-specific partial totals.\nA new revision deliberately uses the current server catalogue; old revisions remain unchanged.\nChanging servings repartitions the same batch; proportional ingredient scaling is deferred.\n\nUse exclusive `create_new` directory locking for the application lifetime, plus an in-process\nmutex. Filesystem operations run on blocking workers. Write a private `.pending` file, sync\nits contents, publish using a same-directory hard link (fails if a target already exists),\nsync the directory on Unix, remove `.pending`, then sync the directory again. Only acknowledge\nsuccess after publication and synchronization. An ambiguous I/O failure makes further writes\nunavailable until operator recovery; no silent overwrite or success response. Load validates\nall records and consecutive histories before accepting traffic. Linux/local filesystems with\nhard-link and fsync support are the initial deployment target; network filesystems, external\nwriters and cross-platform crash durability have not been validated.\n\nThe executable only binds loopback until authentication exists. Recipe responses use\n`Cache-Control: no-store`. The embedding library explicitly documents its trusted-client\nboundary. New directories/files use Unix 0700/0600 (existing directory permissions stay intact).\nLimits: 128 KiB request bodies, 100 ingredient lines, 100 steps, 1–1000000 integer servings,\n1000 total revisions and 2 MiB per persisted revision. The catalogue loader bound stays 1 MiB.\nThis intentionally bounded in-memory revision index must be replaced/expanded under F18.\n\n## Alternatives\nBrowser localStorage would not satisfy server persistence or reproducible source retrieval.\nStarting all SQL/Git adapters together would delay the requested authoring slice. A mutable\nsingle JSON file would obscure revision conflicts/history; Git commits alone do not provide\napplication-level transactions or household authorization. No repository trait/ORM is needed.\n\n## Consequences\nAn exclusive lock survives crashes as a stale file and needs deliberate operator recovery.\nBackups/restore, ambiguous-write recovery and startup failures are documented in the\n[recipe guide](../page_docs_guides_recipes_md/index.html). No automatic Git commit, deletion, synchronization,\nrecipe translation, authentication or production web design is included. F04/F18 remain\nIn progress until their broader contracts and customer acceptance are complete.\n\n## F34 amendment\nADR 0019 adds separate source-backed comparison envelopes and explicit own ranges without\nreplacing original primary readings or inferring dietary safety. The enriched catalogue has a\nbounded 2 MiB loader envelope; the independent 2 MiB recipe publication/restart guard remains.\nHistorical evidence above describes the decision at that time. See\n[F34](../page_specs_0034_evidence_nutrition_ranges_specification_md/index.html) for scope and current status.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0009_local_recipe_revisions_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0010: protected local household profiles\n\nStatus: Proposed implementation for the customer-authorized F09 slice\n\n## Context\nF03/F04 are accepted. F09 covers household members, hard dietary constraints and soft dislikes.\nRecipe sharing/collections remains F05. Household health data needs explicit access controls\nbefore profile storage; hosted identity integration remains separate future work.\n\n## Decision\nProvide an opt-in local household operator Bearer credential, distinct from public catalogue\nand recipe routes. All profile reads/exports/writes/removals and dietary reviews require it.\nKeep one household per dedicated directory with schema/revision validation, exclusive locking,\nprivate file permissions and atomic snapshot replacement. Token stays in runtime configuration\nand page memory, never stored with profiles. Replacement snapshots allow correction/deletion\nwithout an immutable health-data history. No new crate is needed.\n\nKeep allergy/intolerance notes, hard ingredient exclusions and soft dislikes separate. Group\nreview unions hard constraints, conservatively resolves known ingredient aliases and reports\nunknowns. Without complete allergen mapping, every unblocked result requires human review;\nno safety claim or automatic interpretation of free text is made.\n\n## Alternatives\nUnauthenticated profiles would expose household data through the existing demo. Reusing the\nimmutable recipe history would retain deleted health data. Building a hosted identity system\nhere would extend beyond this local slice. One operator credential is a bounded first access\nmodel; it does not implement multiple household roles or federated authorization.\n\n## Consequences\nThe operator credential grants access to all members. Rotation requires restart; previously\nexported copies/backups cannot be revoked. Profile storage and recovery must remain separate\nfrom recipe storage. See the household operator guide. Hosted authentication and richer\nallergen data remain explicit future work; F09 is complete for the documented local access contract.\n\n## Completion amendment\nStructured hard allergen groups and positive source-backed classifications now augment free-text\nnotes; read-only credentials may export/evaluate but cannot modify/remove profiles. Unknown\ncoverage still requires review. These changes complete the local F09 acceptance contract.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0010_local_household_access_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0011: protected family meal plans and preview/apply\n\nStatus: Implementation for customer-requested F10; review pending\n\n## Context\nF03/F04/F09 are accepted. Family planning needs pinned recipe inputs, current household\nconstraints, explicit attendance and time budgets, and inspectable changes before persistence.\nNo calendar provider, external identity service or shopping-list provider has been selected.\n\n## Decision\nAdd protected planning routes to the household access boundary. Recipes and planning share one\nlocked recipe store, so plans refer to actual immutable local recipe IDs/revisions. Household\nprofiles and plans use separate revisioned snapshots in the same exclusively locked household\ndirectory. All I/O-heavy planning work runs on blocking workers with a consistent lock order:\nrecipes, profiles, plans. A preview returns actual profile/plan revisions; apply recomputes the\nsame constraints while holding those locks, requires the matching revisions and explicit review\nacknowledgement, and never permits a known hard dietary or time conflict. Readers can inspect,\npreview and request suggestions; only operators can apply/remove. No new dependency is needed.\n\nPlans identify a Gregorian date and named meal slot, pinned recipe revision, selected attendees,\ntarget servings and active/elapsed/advance budgets. Duration estimates conservatively sum\nordered step active/passive seconds (no inferred overlap). Missing steps mean timing unknown.\nAdvance lead is elapsed duration beyond the meal-day window. Times refer to the original\nrecipe yield and are not silently extrapolated for scaled batches; this uncertainty is shown\nin the mandatory preview. Nutrition retains F03 rational scaling. Shopping requirements derive\nfrom the saved plan's pinned recipes; preview computes the signed mass delta for a slot before\nreplacement/removal. This is planning-derived shopping demand, not completion of F06's shop,\nextra-item, purchase or inventory workflow.\n\nCalendar access is an explicit read-only attendance snapshot import: source, event ID, date\nand already mapped household member IDs. User consent is required; the source/proposal remains\nvisible. Manual attendance can override it without changing any external calendar. Snapshots\ncan be supplied through the API or the demo's bounded JSON file import. No live OAuth provider,\nICS recurrence/timezone parser, calendar polling or external write is claimed. Provider adapters\ncan later supply this same bounded contract; the current import is an actual local input path.\n\nSuggestions evaluate up to 50 explicitly selected candidate revisions, reject known conflicts\nwith reason codes and rank remaining candidates by soft dislikes. Unblocked suggestions remain\nreview-required under F09. Plans retain the profile revision and allergen reference version\nreviewed when applied. Later profile/reference changes mark slots for renewed review; they do\nnot rewrite historical choices or approve them automatically.\n\n## Alternatives\nApplying arbitrary client-authored recipes would bypass pinned source/history guarantees.\nAutomatic application or using soft preferences to bypass exclusions is incompatible with F09.\nA provider-specific calendar integration without a chosen provider would introduce unnecessary\ncredentials/dependencies. Mutating an external shopping list would exceed the current F06 scope.\n\n## Consequences\nOne household/operator boundary remains local and loopback-only. Plan snapshots are bounded to\n100 slots/1 MiB and use synced private pending-file publication with fail-closed recovery.\nAPI export and backups may contain attendance/calendar identifiers. Profile deletion leaves\nhistorical plan member IDs; operators can explicitly remove those meal slots. Source data and\ncooking-time estimates are not food-safety guarantees. See the meal-planning operator guide.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0011_family_meal_planning_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0012: native Nextcloud client and shared household boundary\n\nStatus: Accepted, 2026-11-09; verified native client and test-container integration (see F22 validation).\n\n## Context\nBefore this change, the repository served a loopback-only single-household Rust API and a disposable\nframework-free demo. Customer requests a complete native Nextcloud 35 client and chose shared\nhouseholds with explicit invitations and read/write roles. The supplied test installation is\nNextcloud 35.0.2 with `AppAPI` 35.0.0; no `ExApp` or deployment daemon was registered initially.\n\n## Alternatives\nPublishing the existing server exposes unowned recipes and operator-token health APIs.\nPer-user directories cannot represent shared households. A shared instance-wide directory has\nno household boundary. A new general repository framework or authentication service is unnecessary.\n\n## Decision\nAdd an opt-in `AppAPI` server mode with authenticated lifecycle, registered top menu/assets,\nallowlisted proxy routes and server-side household membership. Keep the local mode intact.\nAuthenticate the signed `AppAPI` user/secret pair with constant-time comparison; never trust a\nbrowser-supplied user ID or expose the `ExApp` secret/operator credentials to the browser.\nStore membership separately from domain data. Each household has dedicated existing recipe,\nprofile and planning stores, preserving revision and nutrition provenance rules. Check current\nroles before forwarding any request; readers cannot write even when a domain route lacks its\nown local role check. Invitations are addressed, explicit and revocable.\n\nUse Vue 3, `@nextcloud/vue` 9.14.1 and a single Vite IIFE plus CSS for `AppAPI`'s actual script loader.\nUse `@nextcloud/router` to generate the embedded history base and PHP proxy URLs, and Nextcloud\naxios for session/CSRF behavior. No origin hardcoding, CSP relaxation or CORS workaround.\nSchema-validate responses at the `TypeScript` boundary. Keep decimal amounts as strings.\n\nAdditional Rust dependencies must serve this accepted boundary: base64 for `AppAPI` credentials,\nsubtle for constant-time comparison, reqwest for authenticated Nextcloud OCS registration, and\ntower util for dispatching already authorized requests into existing domain routers. No new crate\nwithin the workspace and no new storage adapter are introduced.\n\n## Consequences\nNextcloud identity supplies authentication; household membership supplies authorization.\nDomain data is not migrated from arbitrary local directories. Unknown data remains unknown.\nThe local test daemon is a development deployment, not evidence for `HaRP`/container packaging.\nF22/F18 stay In progress until their complete acceptance criteria have actual evidence.\n\n## Dependency and packaging evidence\nNextcloud Vue 9.14.1 depends on Vue Router 5.4; using Router 4 alongside it breaks router-aware\nnative buttons. The client uses one Router 5 instance. `AppAPI` 35 appends `.js`/`.css` to its\nregistered asset paths; registration therefore uses `js/mealshop` and `css/mealshop`.\nThe Vite IIFE defines Nextcloud Vue's `appName`, `appVersion` and production environment.\nThe FRP entrypoint is copied from the installed Nextcloud exapp-development reference with its\ncopyright and licence intact; `appinfo/info.xml` uses a local-only image registry, not a published\nrelease. TCP and `HaRP` Unix-socket transports use the same authenticated routes.\n\nRust's TLS chain introduces ISC (`ring`, `rustls-webpki`, `untrusted`) and the bundled trust-store\nCDLA-Permissive-2.0 (`webpki-roots`) licences. deny.toml grants only exact-version exceptions for\nthese packages, retaining the existing default policy. `RustSec` findings in quinn-proto and\nwebpki-roots were resolved with patched versions, without advisory ignores. A minimatch override\naddresses the npm advisory in the Nextcloud component dependency graph; no component downgrade.\n\nPrimary integration reference: [Nextcloud 35 `ExApp` overview](https://docs.nextcloud.com/server/stable/developer_manual/exapp_development/development_overview/ExAppOverview.html).\nThe installed `AppAPI` 35.0.0 PHP controller and asset services were also read and tested directly.\n\n## Scope follow-up (2026-11-10)\nThe customer approved closing the delivered Nextcloud portion of F21/F22. Their complete original\nacceptance criteria are retained verbatim in Draft F29/F30. This supersedes the F22 status statement\nabove without claiming standalone/Flutter delivery; F18 remains In progress.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0012_nextcloud_shared_households_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0013: household ingredient declarations and recipe images\n\nStatus: Accepted for implementation, 2026-11-09 (customer-requested F24)\n\n## Context\nThe native client needs inline ingredient creation, archive, favourites, tags and composite identities, and optional recipe images. The bundled catalogue is curated primary data and must remain immutable. Saved recipe sources and hard dietary exclusions must survive subsequent edits.\n\n## Alternatives\nEditing the global bundled catalogue breaks isolation and provenance. Inventing a primary source or estimating a composite from a nearby food is unacceptable. A new database adapter is unnecessary for this bounded existing file-store deployment.\n\n## Decision\nStore a bounded, revisioned household ingredient overlay beside profiles under the existing exclusive household lock. Share that store with recipe creation and dietary review. Household declarations carry an explicit provenance kind; an absent source URL is permitted only for an explicitly declared, unknown-nutrition record. Do not fabricate source URLs, nutrient readings or licences. Keep English canonical fields and reviewed translation fields separate.\n\nStore composition as ingredient membership, without implicit mass proportions. Validate references, duplicates and cycles. Expand both current and pinned composition during dietary review and retain their union of positive allergen declarations. Tags remain descriptive unless explicitly added to a person’s hard excluded tags; exact case-insensitive tag matches then block meals, including nested/pinned composition. Structured declared allergens and ingredient exclusions also block meals. Ordinary tags never establish safety. Preserve components in pinned recipe snapshots. Hold the shared ingredient guard through plan review and durable application, and generate the reviewed version from that same guarded state. A separate dietary revision ignores favourite/archive-only changes; legacy overlays without it conservatively use their existing global revision. Archiving affects new selection, not historic reads or constraints.\n\nRecipe images are immutable private household assets, independent of recipe revisions. Accept bounded raster uploads only; validate signatures and dimensions, reject active formats, serve fixed MIME types with no-sniff and private cache policy. Recipe revisions retain optional image IDs; removing the current image does not destroy historic assets. Use existing authenticated household routes and filesystem primitives, without additional crates or external image requests.\n\n## Consequences\nLocal and Nextcloud household APIs share the same overlay semantics. Existing catalogue and recipe JSON remain readable through defaulted additive fields. User-provided labels and allergen declarations are shown as declarations, not reviewed primary evidence. Composite nutrition remains explicitly unknown without exact composition evidence. Shared favourites are household annotations. Existing source records are archived only within that household.\n\n## F34 amendment\nADR 0019 adds separate source-backed comparison envelopes and explicit own ranges without\nreplacing original primary readings or inferring dietary safety. The enriched catalogue has a\nbounded 2 MiB loader envelope; the independent 2 MiB recipe publication/restart guard remains.\nHistorical evidence above describes the decision at that time. See\n[F34](../page_specs_0034_evidence_nutrition_ranges_specification_md/index.html) for scope and current status.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0013_household_ingredients_and_images_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0014: Recipe-owned parts and optional constituents\n\nStatus: Accepted for the customer-authorized F26 slice\n\n## Context\n\nRecipes need preparation branches and explicit ingredient-to-step associations. Optional food\nconstituents must accumulate without being presented as universal nutrients. Existing calculations,\nshopping provenance, immutable snapshots, household permissions and exact quantities must remain intact.\n\n## Alternatives\n\nNested or independently versioned recipe references would need cross-record snapshot resolution,\ncycle handling and yield/conversion semantics not specified in this request. Reusing ingredient\ncomposition as a recipe structure would confuse membership with measured recipe mass. Treating every\npossible constituent as a mandatory nutrient would falsely imply common availability or known absence.\n\n## Decision\n\nUse recipe-owned named parts with stable IDs and an acyclic prepared-part dependency graph. Keep one\nflat list of measured ingredient lines, each belonging to at most one part. Steps reference stable line\nIDs and prepared parts. Nutrition, quantities, shopping and exclusions continue to consume each flat\nline once. Legacy flat records load through defaults; references are validated before atomic publication.\n\nDeclare optional constituents in the existing own nutrient profile with stable IDs, explicit g/mg/µg\nunits, exact decimal amounts, names/translations and the profile's evidence. Inheritance resolves the\nwhole profile into the pinned ingredient snapshot. Sum by stable identity with matching units; unknown\ncontributions remain unknown and known subtotals remain explicit. Legacy beta-glucan/soluble-fibre\nkeys remain readable for existing API/demo clients; the native client separates the constituent view.\nNo physiological benefit or chemical conversion is inferred.\n\nSorbitol uses a separate intolerance registry and explicit household declaration. It is not appended\nto the regulated fourteen-allergen identity mapping. The existing household constraint review resolves\nboth current and pinned declarations, including composed ingredients. The existing free-text intolerance\nfield stores its canonical ID when selected; no diagnosis or tolerance threshold is inferred.\n\n## Consequences\n\nNo new crate, storage adapter or host-specific route is needed. Source records stay immutable. Recipe\nparts do not promise a parallel timer schedule, independent reuse or cooking-loss corrections. New\nfields require matching-version backups for downgrade, while old records remain supported on upgrade.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0014_recipe_parts_and_constituents_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0015: shopping provenance, shop order and product boundaries\n\nStatus: Accepted for F06/F07/F08\n\n## Context\nThe customer requests all three shopping features together, backend and native frontend in ordered\nusable slices. Existing meal-plan shopping totals are read-only aggregates, without purchases or extras.\n\n## Alternatives\nMutating aggregate quantities would lose origin and purchase history. Inferring ingredient equivalence\nfrom names or packages would compromise source identity. Unconsented global learning/lookup exposes\nhousehold behaviour. Additional persistence crates are unnecessary for the accepted file-backed slice.\n\n## Decision\nPersist a revisioned, atomically replaced household shopping file under the existing exclusive household\nlock. Separate current source requirements from immutable purchased receipts. Aggregate only identical\ningredient IDs/base dimensions; retain original input and pinned recipe/package provenance. Rescaling\nor removing a source never reverses a purchase or alters another source's/manual contributions.\n\nMaintain household-only shops, preferences, assignments and explicit manual order. Opt-in completed\nsessions provide explainable integer position averages with a three-session minimum; corrections do not\ntrain and manual order wins. Disabling/resetting learning preserves list requirements and purchases.\n\nKeep barcode products separate from the nutrition catalogue. Validate GTIN check digits, preserve leading\nzeros, and require explicit package dimensions and ingredient-match confirmation. Native browser scanning\nis optional. Open Food Facts requests require per-request consent; a fixed provider, bounded responses,\ntimeouts, no redirects and request throttling prevent arbitrary outbound requests. Retain source/licence/\ntimestamps and original package text; import no nutrition or allergy claims.\n\n## Consequences\nPrivate storage and optimistic revisions extend existing auth/CSRF/role boundaries. Unknown or incompatible\nunits remain separate. Shopping is not inventory accounting. Camera support varies; manual entry remains\ncomplete. External lookup availability is not guaranteed. UI tests use recorded synthetic households and\narchive their fixtures afterward, preserving all customer data.\n\n## Sources\n[Open Food Facts API contract](https://openfoodfacts.github.io/openfoodfacts-server/api/),\n[native barcode support](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Barcode_Detection_API).\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0015_shopping_provenance_and_local_learning_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0016: durable cooking instances and civil-time planning\n\nStatus: Accepted for the customer-requested F12/F13 implementation, 2026-11-10\n\n## Context\nRecipe revisions already contain ingredient/part references and exact active/passive step durations.\nF12 needs independent recoverable timers; F13 adds reviewed dependency schedules across civil-time changes.\nThe customer requests both native Nextcloud frontend and backend in one PR, retaining existing data/auth.\n\n## Alternatives\nBrowser-only countdowns cannot dispatch while closed. Treating local timestamps as UTC corrupts DST\ntransitions. Separate dough/timer engines duplicate deadlines and recovery. Guessing fermentation durations\nor multiplying recipe time by servings invents information. A new scheduler service is unnecessary.\n\n## Decision\nUse one household cooking file under the existing writer lock. Instances pin recipe revision/servings;\nstages retain their recipe-step or explicitly household-defined origin. Timers store absolute UTC seconds\nand paused remaining durations. Reopening never resets duration. Completion is explicit, including overdue\nphases; starting a step requires completed dependencies. Existing recipe parts seed an editable dependency\nDAG. No automatic biological outcome, equipment control or nutrition changes.\n\nTarget-time planning uses backward duration bounds and forward dependency/actual-state constraints.\nRepeated stages expand into named occurrences with explicit waits. Preview every target/delay/timer change;\nretain the exact proposal in a short-lived actor-bound server cache and apply only its current domain\nrevision. Completed work remains fixed. Show active/equipment overlaps, missed targets and declared\nfermentation/temperature uncertainty instead of silently resolving them.\n\nAdd the `jiff` dependency with standard-library support and an always-bundled timezone database. This\naccepted feature needs IANA timezone resolution in minimal containers; fixed offsets and browser parsing\ncannot validate folds/gaps. Reject nonexistent local times and require an explicit earlier/later choice\nfor repeated times. Absolute deadlines remain authoritative; display the chosen timezone and UTC offset.\n\nA bounded Tokio worker uses internal typed request extensions, never browser-provided identity headers,\nto claim/settle persisted notification attempts. Only explicitly subscribed current writers receive\nNextcloud reminders. Persist sending before requesting the documented `AppAPI` notification endpoint.\nAccepted means accepted by Nextcloud, not confirmed on a device. Ambiguous outcomes become unknown and\nare never automatically replayed; definitive connection failures may retry with bounded backoff. Startup\nmarks interrupted sending unknown. Disable/complete/cancel/replan cancels obsolete pending attempts.\nNotification bookkeeping does not invalidate an otherwise unchanged user schedule preview.\n\n## Consequences\nReaders inspect but cannot operate timers or subscribe. Browser permission is device-specific and reported\nas such; no guaranteed OS alarm or push delivery. Offline clients retain a stale read-only view and elapsed\nclock display until reconnect. Notification failures and missed events stay inspectable on reopening.\nNo account credentials, household health data, recipe instructions or notification bodies are logged.\nPreview cache loss requires review again. Storage and notification limits fail explicitly; backup/rollback\ncovers membership, recipes and all household files together. No new standalone or Flutter shell is added.\n\n## Sources\n[Nextcloud 35 notifications API](https://docs.nextcloud.com/server/stable/developer_manual/exapp_development/tech_details/api/notifications.html),\n[Jiff civil-time ambiguity](https://docs.rs/jiff/latest/jiff/tz/struct.AmbiguousZoned.html).\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0016_durable_cooking_and_civil_time_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0017: Unified preparation and recipe-line instructions\n\nStatus: Accepted for the customer-authorized F31 implementation\n\n## Context\nMealprep and long preparations must share the meal plan. A recipe-line temperature describes how to use a quantity, not another ingredient. A later intelligent scale needs typed exact values rather than parsing translated prose.\n\n## Alternatives\nIndependent calendar copies lose provenance and duplicate shopping. Arbitrary untyped JSON or ingredient-name suffixes cannot distinguish units, targets and measurements. A device protocol now would invent an integration contract.\n\n## Decision\nKeep recipe-owned part references and add optional versioned line extensions. The initial discriminated variant is temperature: Celsius, exact signed decimal, original input and recipe-author provenance. Validate at the API boundary. Do not scale temperature with servings or treat it as measured water temperature. Add production and meal allocations to protected planning with revision-pinned recipes; aggregate production once and retain existing household safety checks.\n\n## Consequences\nLegacy lines remain valid. New data needs matching-version backup for downgrade. Existing recipe JSON can carry instructions to a future device adapter, but no connection or device execution is promised. Calendar anchors and elapsed timers remain separate. Preserve original quantity input and unknowns.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0017_unified_preparation_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0018: Explicit homemade ingredient inputs\n\nStatus: Accepted for F33\n\n## Context\nA catalogue food such as toasted bread may be purchased or made. Treating every finished ingredient\nas a purchase is incorrect; guessing raw ingredients, yield or availability from its name is unsafe.\nExisting recipe-owned parts already hold measured ingredient lines, instructions and dependencies.\n\n## Alternatives\nA global non-purchasable catalogue flag cannot represent different recipes. Simply omitting a homemade\nitem from shopping loses its base requirements. New nested independently saved recipes would add a\nseparate revision/yield model outside this slice.\n\n## Decision\nAdd optional `prepared_part` to a finished recipe ingredient line, referencing an existing part with\ninstructions and measured transitive inputs. It is a declared output reference, excluded from input\ncomposition and purchasing; its exact original quantity/identity stay preserved. Base lines count\nonce. Pin all identities; review all conservatively for dietary constraints. Reject unresolved refs,\ncycles and ambiguous multiple outputs. Stateless nutrition accepts resolved measured inputs only.\nCooking derives the producer dependency. Legacy absence means purchased as before.\n\n## Consequences\nNo inferred roasting recipe, output/input ratio, nutrient retention or water loss. Estimates explicitly\nmark cooking unmodelled. Simple unshared preparation removal is confirmed; nested/shared work needs\nexplicit dependency resolution. Older binaries may reject new saved fields: back up complete data\nbefore installation; rollback restores the matching image and snapshot. No endpoint/crate is added.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0018_homemade_recipe_inputs_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0019: Explicit supplemental nutrition and comparison ranges\n\nStatus: Accepted for F34 implementation; review with the PR.\n\n## Context\nThe customer requests useful approximate composition for existing foods, yellow recipe indicators,\nranges and comparison sources. F02/F23 previously left unmatched profiles entirely unknown. The\nprimary catalogue is already close to its 1 MiB serialized-input limit. Recipes freeze primary inputs.\n\n## Alternatives\nCopying a nearby food into the original nutrient map loses uncertainty and identity. A universal\npercentage lacks evidence. Dropping missing inputs undercounts totals. Rewriting old recipe files\nbreaks the append-only history. Provider calls at runtime add availability/privacy dependencies.\n\n## Decision\nKeep original readings intact. Add optional self-contained supplemental evidence to an ingredient,\nwith reviewed reference facts and bounds. Direct same-source supplements remain reported facts;\ncomparative/group envelopes and user-declared bounds remain estimates. Calculate bounds exactly,\nround outward and distinguish complete ranges from covered subtotals. Never transfer dietary safety.\nUse a frozen versioned offline registry, with immutable v1 fallback for legacy snapshots matched to\noriginal stable identities/source state. New revisions pin the full effective evidence. Do not write\nold persisted histories while reading them. Expand the bounded catalogue envelope only as measured\nsource payloads require; preserve the separate 2 MiB recipe-publication/restart validation boundary.\n\n## Consequences\nComparison min/max reflects selected observed records, not population coverage or confidence.\nUnsupported individual nutrients remain explicitly incomplete. Original source values, carbohydrate\nsemantics, quantities, household roles and homemade input accounting remain authoritative. Complete\nprivate backups precede installation; old binaries may reject new optional persisted fields.\n\nMethod references: [FAO/INFOODS matching guidelines](https://www.fao.org/fileadmin/templates/food_composition/documents/Nutrition_assessment/INFOODSGuidelinesforFoodMatching_version_1_2.pdf),\n[FAO missing composition data](https://www.fao.org/4/y4705e/y4705e14.htm),\n[USDA data documentation](https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/data-documentation/).\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0019_source_backed_nutrition_ranges_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0020: revision-pinned recipe collections and explicit sharing\n\nStatus: Accepted for implementation; customer selected accounts and households\nDate: 2026-11-11\n\n## Context\nF05 requires private or explicitly shared recipes/collections, revocable server access and source-link\nsharing for imported content. Existing ownership is household-based and recipe revisions are immutable.\nPrivate audit findings must not be used as implementation fixtures or published acceptance evidence.\n\n## Alternatives\nJoining a household to read a recipe grants excessive access. Anonymous bearer links are not assumed.\nFollowing a recipe's latest revision silently changes collections and may expose later edits.\nRepublishing cached imported content violates the established sharing restriction. A database adapter\nor new identity system is unnecessary for this bounded file-store delivery.\n\n## Decision\nKeep current household ownership and roles. Store collection metadata separately from immutable recipe\nrecords, with explicit recipe/revision entries, monotonic identities and optimistic revision checks.\nUse private atomic pending-file writes under the existing single-process root lock; fail closed on\nambiguous storage errors or pending recovery. The collection API validates references to its own\nhousehold's saved recipes. Revision-pinned meal plans keep their existing behavior.\n\nAdd defaulted, omitted-for-owned recipe provenance. Once imported, a recipe retains the original\nHTTPS source URL across revisions and restart. Future imports must set that provenance at creation;\nF05 does not implement fetching/parsing external recipes (F16). A share projection branches on origin\nbefore constructing the response: imported content becomes a source-link-only result. User-entered\nauthorship is a declaration, not independently verified rights ownership.\n\nImplement personal account grants, addressed household invitations accepted by a destination owner,\nand direct grants to other households owned by the source owner. Current authentication, enabled\nstate and CSRF remain mandatory. Recheck the current resource-specific grant for every shared output,\nincluding collection entries, scale requests and images. Never grant access to the source household's\nprofiles, ingredients catalogue, plans, shopping or unrelated recipes. Do not retain recipient content\nin browser storage. No public source fetches, link previews or third-party sharing provider are added.\n\n## Consequences\nExisting recipe files remain readable and byte-identical. Collection edits do not change recipes or\nhistorical plans. Revocation governs subsequent server access, not copies already downloaded. Current\nfile limits and actionable conflict/recovery responses remain explicit. UI flows and the final role/\nrecipient matrix are recorded in the feature specification. Collection editors cannot add externally\nvisible content without owner authority once a collection has any outbound grant. Pending invitations\nexpose metadata only. Source/target owners may revoke; target-household members are authorized against\ntheir current membership on every request.\n\n\n## Implementation boundaries\n`library.json` contains bounded household collection/grant metadata. Its optimistic household\nrevision protects outgoing writes; each grant has a separate version for recipient acceptance/removal.\nCollection deletion removes its grants in the same atomic write. A pending or ambiguous write makes\nthe store read-only until the operator restores a verified document and restarts the single writer.\n\nA recipe read resolves transient `sharing_origin` from its latest revision while retaining the requested\nimmutable recipe and ingredient snapshot. Correcting a formerly owned recipe to imported therefore\nrestricts all shared historical revisions without rewriting their files. Imported provenance cannot\nbe removed or its source replaced in a later revision. Sharing checks this effective origin before\nconstructing a summary, detail, scale or image response. Legacy owned files remain readable unchanged.\n\nThe native picker follows Nextcloud file-sharing visibility. The server independently verifies the\nselected canonical account ID with the authenticated `AppAPI` users utility; lookup failure prevents\ncreating a grant. See the official [`AppAPI` users utility](https://docs.nextcloud.com/server/stable/developer_manual/exapp_development/tech_details/api/utils.html).\nNo remote directory response is retained or logged. The existing `AppAPI` route scopes are sufficient.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0020_recipe_sharing_collections_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0021: completed cooking history and local preference epochs\n\nStatus: Accepted and implemented under F11\nDate: 2026-11-11\n\n## Context\nF11 needs actual cooking/ratings and incremental preferences without treating plans as observed\nmeals or weakening dietary constraints. Cooking sessions and dietary profiles are household-owned;\nprofile IDs are not authenticated personal voting identities. The existing planner already checks\ncurrent plus frozen declarations, time budgets and exact quantities.\n\n## Alternatives\nCounting planned meals fabricates observations. Timer estimates are not measured active effort.\nRemote/cross-household training and an opaque framework are unnecessary before evaluation. Replaying\nall retained ratings after opt-out would silently rebuild removed preference state. Independent\ncandidate-batch rankings do not provide a coherent recommendation order for a large recipe library.\n\n## Decision\nAdd bounded atomic household history with explicit manual completion or a verified completed session.\nRetain pinned recipe/source metadata and actor/time provenance; correct/delete with optimistic revision\nchecks. Session records are unique and survive removal of the session. Ratings are explicitly reported\nwithin the household's existing reader/writer visibility; no private personal-vote claim is made.\n\nOnly the owner controls learning, off by default, with optional selected profile-rating scopes.\nChanging/enabling/resetting scope starts a fresh epoch and clears aggregates. Only new/explicitly\ncorrected current-epoch records contribute. Opt-out disables learning; separate history deletion\ncontrols retained raw observations. Incremental integer sums/counts are checked against eligible\nrecords on restart, so corrupted counters fail closed rather than changing rankings silently.\n\nFilter with canonical planner constraints before a versioned, explained integer preference rule.\nRank the whole bounded candidate pool and return a limited number of full previews. Preserve cold\nstart and discovery; evidence counts are not probabilities, clinical confidence or allergy guarantees.\nEvaluate model changes offline against the baseline with a versioned synthetic holdout and explicitly\nlabelled acceptance, variety and rating-utility proxies. Do not claim measured human satisfaction or\ncausal improvements without real evidence. No private household dataset is exported or published.\n\n## Consequences\nNo new crate, identity system or storage adapter is needed. Existing recipe/history/plan/grant files\nremain compatible. A history file and its learning aggregates share one atomic transaction; ambiguous\nwrites stop mutations until verified recovery. Hard dietary constraints and explicit plan review keep\npriority over preference ordering. API/UI visibility, reset semantics and acceptance commands remain\nin the F11 contract. Individual account consent/privately authenticated voting is outside this existing\nhousehold profile model; the UI states who reports and can see feedback.\n\nFeedback is pooled by stable recipe identity, while counts from earlier revisions are explicit in\neach explanation. Current target-revision constraints always take precedence. Both rule and learned\nordering retain deterministic discovery within equal declared-dislike groups; the learned version\nuses candidates without evidence in the current epoch. Recency counts the seven reported civil days\nending on the selected planning day, without pretending that a date-only history is a measured timeline.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0021_completed_history_and_local_preferences_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0022: scoped MCP access and atomic recipe creation receipts\n\nStatus: Accepted for implementation under F17\nDate: 2026-11-12\n\n## Context\nF17 needs authenticated recipe tools without bypassing household roles, exact quantities or imported\nsource-only sharing. Nextcloud already supplies authenticated account identity through `AppAPI`.\nRecipe publication is one append-only, private, synced file transaction; a second idempotency journal\nwould create a crash window after recipe publication and before receipt persistence.\n\n## Alternatives\nExposing the service secret would grant all households. New OAuth infrastructure is not justified\nby the existing deployment. Client-declared read-only flags cannot protect backend tool calls.\nStoring a separate successful-operation record after saving a recipe duplicates on ambiguous retries.\nReimplementing recipe validation in MCP would drift from the regular API.\n\n## Decision\nReuse the Nextcloud authenticated proxy; owner-managed account grants default to Disabled and intersect\ncurrent membership/role on every call. Publish only recipe/ingredient tools. Use bounded stateless\nJSON-RPC with 2026-07-28 and 2025-11-25 compatibility; reject Origin-bearing RPC requests and keep native\nconfiguration on existing protected REST. Supply a standard-library stdio bridge using environment\ncredentials; prohibit redirects and nonlocal plaintext transport. App passwords retain normal account\npowers, so a dedicated least-privilege account is required for clients outside the owner's trust.\nThis is existing Nextcloud authentication, not a new standalone auth provider or an OAuth claim.\n\nUse canonical validation and a short-lived actor/scope/catalogue-bound preview. Explicitly confirmed\ncreation atomically adds an audit/idempotency receipt to the first recipe revision. The existing store\nlock serializes duplicate keys; restart loads the immutable receipt. There is no second commit to lose.\nA receipt is private persistence metadata. Native activity exposes actor/time, never keys; ordinary\nrecipe and MCP projections omit the receipt.\nImported effective provenance controls every MCP projection, including historical revisions and previews.\n\n## Consequences\nNo new crate, remote fetch, model provider, generic persistence adapter or global permission is needed.\nThe official MCP client is a pinned development-only dependency for reproducible wire interoperability tests.\nExisting files without receipts remain compatible and are not rewritten. Failed/ambiguous publications\nretain existing fail-closed recovery. Preview bodies live only in bounded memory and expire; clients\nretry completed writes using the original key and preview ID, even after preview expiry or restart.\nRemote hosted OAuth, per-client delegation and non-Nextcloud shells remain separate accepted features.\n\n## Primary sources\n- [Current MCP transport](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/basic/transports/streamable-http): stateless requests, mirrored headers and Origin validation.\n- [MCP tools](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/server/tools): schemas, structured errors, annotations and confirmation.\n- [MCP discovery](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/server/discover) and [version compatibility](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/basic/lifecycle).\n- [MCP stdio](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/basic/transports/stdio) and [authorization](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/basic/authorization): environment credentials for stdio; no false OAuth interoperability claim.\n- [Nextcloud ExApp authentication](https://docs.nextcloud.com/server/stable/developer_manual/exapp_development/tech_details/Authentication.html); installed `AppAPI` proxy source and existing role checks are authoritative for this deployment.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0022_scoped_mcp_recipe_creation_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0023: safe public recipe retrieval and explicit private review\n\nStatus: Accepted for implementation under F16\nDate: 2026-11-12\n\n## Context\nF16 adds customer-operated public URL import to the existing Nextcloud recipe editor. URLs, DNS,\nHTML and structured source content are hostile inputs. Import must not reach internal services,\nexecute scripts, reuse credentials, infer ingredient identities or remove imported sharing rules.\n\n## Alternatives\nA hosted scraper/model provider adds a new privacy boundary and is unnecessary. Headless browsing\nwould execute supplied scripts and encourage login bypass. Regular expressions are not an HTML\nparser. A separate successful-import journal can drift from recipe publication after a crash.\n\n## Decision\nUse the existing reqwest transport with a deliberately narrow public HTTPS policy, DNS validation\nand address pinning, peer verification, no system proxies/cookies/credentials, manual validated\nredirects, bounded bodies and one total deadline. Bounded CPU slots retain their permit inside parser tasks even after cancellation. Respect robots policy and explicit paywall flags;\ntreat missing/unavailable robots resources under RFC 9309 conservatively (auth/rate-limit/policy\nerrors remain blocked); report unsupported/login/blocked sources without bypass. Source restrictions do not imply that a\npublic page grants publication rights. Manual private entry remains available without any fetch.\n\nUse `html5ever` 0.35.0 directly for a non-executing HTML5 token stream and `robotstxt` 0.3.0 for\nrobots matching. A newer scraper failed Rust 1.85; its compatible version pulled in unnecessary CSS\nselectors, an unmaintained hash dependency and licences outside the existing policy. A token stream\nneeds no DOM/selectors, uses bounded collected text, and passes the unchanged licence/advisory gates.\nThese parsers are required by this feature. No new service, model, auth provider or browser runtime.\n\nExtract bounded Schema.org Recipe JSON-LD candidates, including arrays/graphs and nested steps.\nKeep source text/language and optional yield/durations; missing values stay explicitly absent.\nNever fetch JSON-LD contexts, images, scripts or linked instructions. Source nutrition, allergy and\ndiet labels never populate the ingredient catalogue or assert safety. Ingredient mapping is explicit;\nonly unambiguous metric amounts may be suggested, with source text retained.\n\nUse the native existing editor for review. Required canonical quantities/servings must be supplied;\nstep times absent from the source remain labelled as such and any entered times (including zero)\nare explicit user declarations under the review confirmation. Do not allocate total cooking time\nacross steps or silently translate. Preserve unknown original fields independently of reviewed values.\n\nA bounded actor-bound preview lives in memory for 30 minutes. Final save checks current write role,\ncanonical recipe/images/ingredients and explicit review. Atomically attach an immutable import receipt\nand selected original source snapshot to the first recipe revision using the existing publication\ntransaction. Repeated identical actor/key/preview/candidate/body returns that revision across restart;\nchanged payload under the same key conflicts. Ordinary edits retain original URL/status and expose\nthe first source snapshot only to the owning household; shared and MCP projections stay source-only.\n\n## Consequences\nNo HTML or complete page is persisted; only selected bounded source fields and reviewed recipe data.\nNo secrets or recipe contents enter logs. Existing files remain readable and unchanged. An older\nserver cannot read a new import receipt; downgrade requires an explicit format-preserving migration.\nTests use synthetic sources/households, never retained private directories. New API transport seams\nexist only to test DNS/redirect/body policy deterministically, not to provide a production bypass.\n\n## Primary sources\n- [Schema.org Recipe](https://schema.org/Recipe): ingredient text, yield and nested instructions.\n- [RFC 9309](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9309.html): robots policy and unavailable resources.\n- [reqwest 0.12.28](https://docs.rs/reqwest/0.12.28/reqwest/struct.ClientBuilder.html): pinned resolution,\n  redirect policy, HTTPS-only requests and disabling environment proxies.\n- [IANA IPv4 special registry](https://www.iana.org/assignments/iana-ipv4-special-registry/) and\n  [IPv6 special registry](https://www.iana.org/assignments/iana-ipv6-special-registry/): conservative\n  globally reachable address policy, excluding transitional and special-use ranges.\n- [html5ever](https://docs.rs/html5ever/0.35.0/html5ever/tokenizer/) and [robotstxt](https://docs.rs/robotstxt/): parser APIs.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0023_reviewed_recipe_link_import_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0024: atomic household stock, reservations and manual offers\n\nStatus: Accepted\nDate: 2026-11-12\n\n## Context\nF15 links stock, shopping and completed cooking without treating a plan or check-off as physical\nconsumption. Existing shopping.json already atomically publishes demands and immutable purchases.\nSeparate independently written stock/shopping files would allow double coverage after a crash.\n\n## Alternatives\nA second stock journal needs cross-file recovery; automatic purchase/cooking side effects hide review.\nA generic transaction framework or new database/crate is unnecessary. External offer providers introduce\nunapproved licensing/privacy contracts. Universal mass/volume/count conversion invents evidence.\n\n## Decision\nAdd optional/default-empty F15 state to the existing versioned private shopping snapshot, within its\nexisting 4 MiB publication boundary. Reuse fixed-point amounts and g/kg, ml/l, piece dimensions. Keep\noriginal quantities and explicitly resolved ingredient/product identities; unmatched lots never match\nrecipes by name. Manual offers retain their supplied source and exact package/price/currency.\n\nAll F15 changes have a no-write preview and explicit reviewed confirmation with a stable actor/key,\nexpected shopping revision and immutable command receipt in the same atomic publication. Identical\nretries survive restart; changed key payloads conflict. The current writer role is checked on every\ncall. Locks follow profiles -> recipes -> cooking -> ingredients -> shopping; source stores are read\nonly and independent completed actions are never silently reversed.\n\nReservations reduce proposals without debiting stock. Source changes release obsolete holds; used\nallocations remain linked to their original source identity. Purchase receipts enter stock only when\nreviewed. Completed cooking consumes reviewed matching lots, optionally using explicitly selected\ncompatible reservations; unbooked input remains visible. Reversals append opposite physical movements\nand release the reversed use's coverage; they cannot make stock negative or steal another hold.\n\nOffer entry is manual. Adding it to shopping rechecks validity on the explicitly selected shopping\ndate and current intended household profiles; known exclusions block it and unknown coverage stays\nunknown. No source claims, nearby food matches, substitutions, scraping or automatic purchases.\n\n## Consequences\nOld shopping files remain readable; new F15 snapshots require the current reader on downgrade.\nPreserve the entire shopping file and its receipts in backup/recovery. Existing single-writer locks,\nprivate permissions, fsync/rename and fail-closed pending-write recovery remain authoritative.\nNo retained private household inspection, data reset, new runtime dependency or provider is required.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0024_household_stock_and_offers_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0025: native brewing and fermentation processes on the durable household scheduler\n\nStatus: Accepted for the customer-requested F14 delivery\nDate: 2026-11-13\n\n## Context\nF14 adds independently validated beverage-specific templates, measured batches and equipment reservations. The customer explicitly\nasks for researched BIAB, Grainfather, Brew Monk, Braumeister, DIY all-grain, extract and partial mash.\nThe [research](../page_specs_0014_brewing_fermentation_research_md/index.html) distinguishes fermentable strategy,\nvessel layout, heat and sparging. Existing cooking timers already persist deadlines, dependencies,\nIANA civil time and personal `AppAPI` delivery uncertainty. Their food recipe/servings reference is not\na valid representation of a brew recipe and target liquid volume.\n\n## Alternatives\nA second scheduler/notification worker duplicates recovery and delivery semantics. Fake food recipes,\nservings-as-litres or synthetic recipe IDs corrupt provenance and F15/history integration. Browser-only\ncountdowns fail across restart. Automatic manufacturer/device integrations would invent unsupported\nprotocol and credential contracts. Unrestricted generic workflow frameworks are unnecessary.\n\n## Decision\nKeep the existing single household cooking transaction and scheduler. Add optional/default-empty brewing\nrecipes, equipment and readings in that atomic file. Make each process source explicitly a food-recipe\nreference or a brew-recipe reference. Preserve the exact old food JSON through a validated wire adapter;\nno false food/servings record is created for drinks. Existing food APIs, history and inventory consumption\nremain food-only. Brew routes reuse the same deadline/dependency/timezone and notification operations.\nCross-family preview/apply boundaries must be checked, not inferred from a client URL.\n\nBrew recipes have immutable revisions, an exact target liquid volume, entered ingredient quantities,\nsource-linked process choices and editable phases. A batch pins the revision and its actual equipment.\nReservation checks share the same writer lock and publication as batch plans/actions. Planned overlaps\nare visible and block conflicting allocation. Started or paused work retains physical equipment until\nexplicit completion/cancellation, including overdue fermentation. Brewing/fermentation dependencies require explicit\ncompletion; an elapsed biological timer cannot imply readiness. Support long declared fermentation\nwindows without broadening food-only policy accidentally.\n\nStore original readings with exact units, civil/absolute measurement time, authenticated entry time/\nactor, manual/instrument attribution and explicit known/unknown calibration. Corrections append a new\nrecord with a reason and reference the original. Instrument transcription is supported; no unconfigured\npolling, vendor-cloud connection, implicit SG/Plato/Brix conversion or inferred chemistry is claimed.\n\nAll mutations retain current household permissions, review points, optimistic revisions, private synced\natomic publication and fail-closed recovery. Batch creation/reading retries must not duplicate facts.\nUI uses existing native components and EN/DE contracts. No new crate or storage adapter is needed.\n\n## Consequences\nOld snapshots remain readable; new brewing state needs the current reader or an explicit preserving downgrade\nmigration. Back up the complete household cooking file, including receipts and notification state.\nNo protected household inspection or reset is required. Cider, white/red wine and traditional mead have separately reviewed sources and typed process choices,\nwithout beer-method fields. Keep the original beer method JSON and default-omitted beer batch identity\nunchanged for preserving old snapshots and operation receipts. No heater/pump control, automatic inventory consumption, ABV/IBU/priming calculator,\nuniversal yeast handling or food-safety guarantee is introduced.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0025_native_brewing_processes_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0026: Flutter phone delivery and the first offline boundary\n\nStatus: Accepted — customer Android/emulator decision and explicit implementation Go, 2026-11-13\nDate: 2026-11-13\n\n## Context\n\nThe customer supplied a mobile product pack and requests fewer, larger PRs which can be tried on a\nsmartphone from the start. Flutter is already the app target (N01 / ADR 0001). `main` now has substantial\nRust/Nextcloud domain functionality, but no Flutter application, mobile login or shopping outbox.\nThe source pack explicitly describes a target, not an audited inventory. Its agent rules must not\nreplace the repository's `AGENTS.md`.\n\nExisting shopping revisions prevent stale writes but do not provide durable request replay. A manual\nadd with no source ID allocates a new source; a lost successful response followed by a fresh retry can\nrepeat intent. Timers have canonical server deadlines, while Nextcloud notification delivery is not\nnative mobile push. These risks must be resolved in their user-facing slices, not hidden by UI mocks.\n\n## Alternatives\n\n1. Port the pack in its listed order, starting with a five-tab shell/profile tour: easy to scaffold,\n   but delays a useful phone workflow and discovers transport/offline failures late.\n2. Separate backend, sync framework and each screen into many small PRs: locally reviewable, but\n   conflicts with the requested larger smartphone-testable deliveries and leaves disconnected pieces.\n3. Rewrite the server or build all adapters/hosted authentication first: discards working contracts and\n   greatly delays a usable Nextcloud app without a corresponding accepted requirement.\n4. Deliver five coherent end-to-end PRs with smaller internal commits and explicit install evidence.\n\n## Decision\n\nChoose option 4. P1 connects a real native Flutter app to Nextcloud and completes independent shopping;\nP2 adds people/content/activity, P3 week/physical mealprep/stock, P4 cooking/fermentation/history, P5\ncamera/share/OCR review. All required Rust, Nextcloud compatibility and phone changes stay in that\nfeature's PR. Do not implement a placeholder tab, `WebView` product shell or a new general sync framework.\n\nUse `clients/flutter/`, feature-oriented UI/state modules and focused API/cache services. Keep Rust as\ncanonical domain/authorization/calculation/scheduling authority; Dart retains exact DTO values and local\npresentation/intent. Pick concrete maintained packages, toolchain versions and cache migrations within\nP1 with licence/OS review, rather than installing a speculative dependency set in this planning PR.\n\nPrefer Login Flow v2 in the system browser, device-specific revocable Nextcloud app credentials in\nplatform-protected storage, and authenticated proxy access. Retain installation prefixes and validate\nreturned login/poll origins. No client receives `ExApp`/operator secrets or embeds the user's master\npassword. Probe actual proxy writes, permissions and revocation on Nextcloud 35 before claiming support.\nStandalone/hosted identity remains its own explicit unresolved contract.\n\nP1 adds bounded atomic shopping receipts before automatic offline write replay. Scope keys and cached\nrecords by canonical server/account/household, preserve the reviewed request, recheck current authority,\nand distinguish pending/confirmed/conflict. Do not rebase a check-off over unseen new contributions.\nLocal persistence and receipt publication are one domain-specific contract, not global last-write-wins.\nLater offline domains must audit their own replay and irreversible effects before being enabled.\nOffline devices cannot observe immediate remote revocation: define bounded cache retention and local\nprotections, revalidate on reconnect, and stop pending writes/clear protected data when revocation is known.\n\nAndroid APK is the accepted initial distribution path, with an accelerated Android emulator explicitly requested. Establish\napplication ID, stable development signing/upgrade path and physical-device reachability in P1. For\niPhone-first delivery, establish macOS/Xcode/provisioning there instead; an unsigned build is not phone\nacceptance. Do not leak signing keys to untrusted PR jobs. Protected distribution/local retained-key\nbuilds provide upgradeable phone artifacts; ordinary untrusted CI still compiles and tests without keys.\n\n## Consequences\n\nEvery implementation PR has immediate phone value, explicit dependencies and regression evidence.\nThe planner and mobile notification work remain larger separate review units because they introduce\ndifferent correctness/OS risks. Each can contain thin, tested commits without multiplying customer PRs.\n\nThe imported source remains byte-preserved in `references/mobile-product-spec-2026-11-12/`; its agent\nfile is renamed `.md.reference` to prevent activation. F35 is In progress after the explicit Go. F18, F29,\nF30, hosted admission and unimplemented storage adapters retain their honest independent statuses.\nP1 adds the Android app and shopping receipts under ADR 0027; the preserved source remains unchanged.\n\n## Primary references\n\nReviewed on 2026-11-13; they support the proposed approach, not a claim of completed integration:\n\n- [Nextcloud 35 Login Flow v2](https://docs.nextcloud.com/server/stable/developer_manual/client_apis/LoginFlow/index.html#login-flow-v2): system-browser device credential acquisition.\n- [Flutter offline-first](https://docs.flutter.dev/app-architecture/design-patterns/offline-first): separate cached/remote access and deliberate synchronization semantics.\n- [Flutter Android distribution](https://docs.flutter.dev/deployment/android): APK build, signing and device installation.\n- [Flutter iOS distribution](https://docs.flutter.dev/deployment/ios): macOS/Xcode, provisioning and publishing requirements.\n- [Android Debug Bridge](https://developer.android.com/tools/adb): device connection and installation tooling; verify loopback reverse forwarding during P1 setup.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0026_flutter_phone_delivery_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0027: Android shopping credentials, storage and request receipts\n\nStatus: Accepted for F35 P1\nDate: 2026-11-13\n\n## Context\n\nP1 must remain usable after a disconnected Android process restart. Existing shopping mutations use\nan expected revision but cannot identify a successful request whose response was lost. The native\nVue contracts and shared shopping/inventory transaction must remain compatible. Android is the\ncustomer-approved first platform, including a real emulator. The account credential, household\nnames and shopping content are private; no medical profile is needed for P1.\n\n## Alternatives\n\n- Retry ordinary shopping POSTs: can allocate a second manual source after response loss.\n- Add a global sync engine or separate receipt file: unnecessary domain scope and non-atomic writes.\n- Return new fields in all existing responses: risks strict existing client schemas.\n- Use an explicitly versioned mobile route backed by the existing atomic shopping file.\n\n## Decision\n\nUse `GET/POST /api/households/{id}/domain/api/household/shopping/mobile`. GET returns version 1,\nserver time, maximum operation age, current role and the unchanged shopping snapshot. A separate\n`ingredient_groups` map projects only group IDs, names and translations for ingredients actually in the list;\nmanual text stays unclassified. This metadata comes from existing explicit group membership, never inferred\nnutrition or new category rules. Both GET and POST include it; the legacy snapshot remains identical. POST accepts an\noperation ID, issuance timestamp, frozen expected revision and an existing shopping command. It returns\nan immutable applied revision/operation ID and **separately the current snapshot**, including on replay.\nA receipt does not claim that a later snapshot is the original historical response.\n\nCheck current household permission on every request, before inspecting any receipt. Namespace receipt\nkeys by household and authenticated actor. Reusing a key with a changed command, revision or issuance\ntime conflicts. Publish the command effects and receipt in the same existing clone/validate/fsync/rename\ntransaction, including the existing inventory reconciliation. No new Rust crate or storage writer.\nAn unsent new manual addition may carry `purchased: true`; create its source and exactly that\nsource's purchase atomically. It must never check older aggregated contributions. The client can\nchange that flag only before the first dispatch; attempted envelopes remain immutable.\n\nNever rebase an attempted request. In particular, a stale aggregate check conflicts if another client\nhas added an unseen contribution. A new add without an existing source can bind the current revision\non its first dispatch; the serialized request is persisted before network dispatch and then immutable.\n\nAccept intent age at most seven days (five minutes of forward clock tolerance). Retain receipts for\neight days from issuance; never evict a live receipt to make room. Limit to 1,024 receipts per household,\n8 KiB per request and the existing 4 MiB shopping file. Capacity errors preserve all existing state.\nExpired intent is rejected even if its receipt has been pruned, so it cannot become a new duplicate.\nThis is a bounded small-household delivery contract, not an unlimited operation history.\n\nUse optional `mobile_receipts` in shopping schema v1, absent for untouched legacy stores. The new reader\nvalidates them; existing response bodies omit them. An older binary using deny-unknown-fields refuses a\nreceipt-bearing file: do not downgrade that file into an old writer. Recover using a verified pre-upgrade\nbackup or a reviewed forward-compatible migration, never strip receipts while clients may retry.\n\nUse Flutter 3.50.0 (official stable commit `ab2912035e160d9cad6306df307576da6128ebdb`), Dart 3.14.0,\nthe official extracted `material_ui` package, `http` and `url_launcher`.\nA focused Android platform channel uses the OS keystore AES-256-GCM key and `AtomicFile`, explicitly\nfsyncing contents and the directory before acknowledging a write. The reviewed secure-storage plugin\n11.2.0 uses `SharedPreferences.apply` for ordinary writes, which acknowledges before durable disk write;\nit was therefore removed before delivery. No iOS persistence implementation is claimed in P1.\nKeep a single bounded protected application record (1 MiB) for credential, locale, household selection,\nshopping snapshots and at most 100 pending intents per household. Avoid a new database/encryption layer.\nReview/pin the complete package lock and licences in the mobile guide. No analytics SDK.\n\nAcquire a revocable device app password using Nextcloud Login Flow v2 in the external system browser.\nKeep poll token only in memory. Preserve installation prefixes; reject changed login/poll/credential\norigins and credential-bearing redirects. HTTPS and normal certificate verification are mandatory;\nonly debug builds may use explicit loopback HTTP. Never collect a master password in the app or ship\nan `ExApp` secret. Logging out removes the app's protected record; server revocation remains a Nextcloud\naccount action. Disable Android backup and device-to-device extraction of protected app data.\n\nOnly the selected account has local records, scoped to its configured origin/user/household. Bound\ncache and intent lifetime to seven days; clear unauthorized household data after reconnect. A disconnected\ndevice cannot observe revocation immediately. Freeze uncertain requests across restart, retry the same\nID after response loss, expose conflicts for explicit review and prevent silently dropping unknown\noutcomes. Foreground polling/resume refresh is sufficient; no background execution promise.\n\n## Consequences and verification\n\nThe UI can distinguish a saved local intent from a confirmed purchase. Permission removal and\nconcurrent demand cannot silently expand offline purchases. Storage failure stops transmission.\nThe protected-record limit can require finishing pending work before more entries are added.\n\nAndroid API 36 Pixel 6 emulation uses KVM, a dedicated ADB port and its own AVD; API 36 build tools\nfollow the pinned Flutter SDK. A retained local development signing key supplies upgradeable test APKs;\nuntrusted PR builds use ephemeral keys. Production signing and iOS provisioning remain separate gates.\nSee F35 verification and the mobile guide for actual evidence; emulator tests are not physical-phone proof.\n\n## Primary references\n\n- [Nextcloud Login Flow v2](https://docs.nextcloud.com/server/stable/developer_manual/client_apis/LoginFlow/index.html#login-flow-v2)\n- [Official Flutter Material package](https://pub.dev/packages/material_ui)\n- [Dart HTTP package](https://pub.dev/packages/http)\n- [Official URL launcher](https://pub.dev/packages/url_launcher)\n- [Android Keystore](https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/keystore)\n- [`AtomicFile`](https://developer.android.com/reference/android/util/AtomicFile)\n- [Flutter platform channels](https://docs.flutter.dev/platform-integration/platform-channels)\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0027_mobile_shopping_receipts_md {}

#[doc = "# Architecture decisions\n\nUse the next free four-digit number. Record context, alternatives, decision, consequences\nand status; supersede accepted decisions explicitly rather than silently rewriting history.\nA proposal is not customer acceptance of an unresolved choice.\n\n- [ADR 0001: stack and delivery](../page_docs_adr_0001_stack_and_delivery_md/index.html) — Accepted for bootstrap; future integrations planned.\n- [ADR 0002: quantities and nutrition](../page_docs_adr_0002_quantities_and_nutrition_md/index.html) — Proposed implementation; customer unit preferences accepted.\n- [ADR 0003: persistence and data boundaries](../page_docs_adr_0003_persistence_and_data_boundaries_md/index.html) — Customer storage targets accepted; schema and adapters proposed.\n- [ADR 0004: licensing hosting and sharing](../page_docs_adr_0004_licensing_hosting_and_sharing_md/index.html) — AGPL and business goals accepted; admission/commerce/federation proposed.\n- [ADR 0005: privacy and learning](../page_docs_adr_0005_privacy_and_learning_md/index.html) — Proposed safeguards for customer-requested learning.\n- [ADR 0006: durable process schedules](../page_docs_adr_0006_durable_process_schedules_md/index.html) — Proposed shared process model.\n- [ADR 0007: localization and web clients](../page_docs_adr_0007_localization_and_web_clients_md/index.html) — Customer direction accepted; product integration proposed.\n- [ADR 0008: curated ingredient sources](../page_docs_adr_0008_curated_ingredient_sources_md/index.html) — Customer scope accepted; import/model details proposed.\n\n- [ADR 0009: local recipe revisions](../page_docs_adr_0009_local_recipe_revisions_md/index.html) — Local authoring slice; review pending.\n\n- [ADR 0010: protected local household access](../page_docs_adr_0010_local_household_access_md/index.html) — Proposed local F09 slice.\n\n- [ADR 0011: family meal planning](../page_docs_adr_0011_family_meal_planning_md/index.html) — Local protected preview/apply and consented attendance import.\n\n- [0012: native Nextcloud client and shared household boundary](../page_docs_adr_0012_nextcloud_shared_households_md/index.html)\n\n- [0013: Household ingredient declarations and recipe images](../page_docs_adr_0013_household_ingredients_and_images_md/index.html)\n\n- [0014: Recipe-owned parts and optional constituents](../page_docs_adr_0014_recipe_parts_and_constituents_md/index.html)\n\n- [0015: shopping provenance, shop order and product boundaries](../page_docs_adr_0015_shopping_provenance_and_local_learning_md/index.html)\n\n- [0016: durable cooking instances and civil-time planning](../page_docs_adr_0016_durable_cooking_and_civil_time_md/index.html)\n\n- [0017: Unified preparation and recipe-line instructions](../page_docs_adr_0017_unified_preparation_md/index.html)\n\n- [0018: Homemade recipe inputs](../page_docs_adr_0018_homemade_recipe_inputs_md/index.html)\n\n- [0019: Source-backed nutrition ranges](../page_docs_adr_0019_source_backed_nutrition_ranges_md/index.html)\n\n- [0020: Revision-pinned recipe collections and explicit sharing](../page_docs_adr_0020_recipe_sharing_collections_md/index.html)\n\n- [0021: Completed cooking history and local preference epochs](../page_docs_adr_0021_completed_history_and_local_preferences_md/index.html)\n- [0022: scoped MCP access and atomic recipe creation receipts](../page_docs_adr_0022_scoped_mcp_recipe_creation_md/index.html)\n- [0023: Safe public retrieval and reviewed recipe import](../page_docs_adr_0023_reviewed_recipe_link_import_md/index.html)\n\n- [0024 Household stock and offers](../page_docs_adr_0024_household_stock_and_offers_md/index.html)\n\n- [0025: Native brewing processes](../page_docs_adr_0025_native_brewing_processes_md/index.html) — Accepted for F14.\n\n- [0026: Flutter phone delivery and the first offline boundary](../page_docs_adr_0026_flutter_phone_delivery_md/index.html) — Proposed; customer plan review and implementation Go required.\n"]
