#[doc = "# Brewing in Nextcloud\n\nOpen **Brewing** in the household navigation. **Brew recipes** defines a reusable process;\n**Equipment** contains actual vessels/tools; **Batches** pins a recipe revision, equipment and dates.\nCurrent household owners/editors can write; readers can inspect all three views and saved readings.\nFood recipe shares, cooking history and inventory consumption do not turn a brew into a food recipe.\n\n## Choose and adapt a method\n\nSixteen source-linked starting templates cover beer and independently researched cider, white/red wine\nand traditional mead. The twelve beer templates cover extract, extract with specialty-grain steeping,\npartial mash, BIAB, Grainfather, Brew Monk, Speidel Braumeister and separate-tun/two-vessel/three-vessel/\nHERMS/RIMS DIY setups. BIAB belongs to all-grain. Fermentable strategy, vessel layout, heating,\nsparging and mash approach are independent recipe facts. Top-return baskets and upward-flow malt pipes\nremain distinct. Read the actual model/version source before adapting a template to your equipment.\n[Research and primary references](../page_specs_0014_brewing_fermentation_research_md/index.html).\n\nTemplates supply originally authored phase prompts, not a ready-to-brew formulation or universal\nmash temperature, water ratio, efficiency or fermentation duration. Enter the recipe's exact target\nvolume, ingredients and original quantities, phase instructions, dependencies and declared durations.\nOptional quantities/temperatures are added with plus actions. Enter missing facts as unknown rather\nthan zero. All phases stay visible. Method references remain in every immutable recipe revision;\nlater edits leave existing batches pinned to their original recipe.\n\nA phase can have active work, passive duration and shortest/longest passive bounds, a reminder lead,\ntarget temperature, actual phase volume and uncertainty notes. Beer phase work/passive durations\nsupport up to 365 days each; food-only duration policy is unchanged. Times are estimates, never a\nfermentation/readiness model. A phase without duration runs manually until explicitly completed.\n\n## Equipment and independent batches\n\nRecord each actual kettle, mash tun, hot-water tank, pump, cooler, fermenter or bag separately. Capacity\nis a declared usable liquid volume, not an assumption derived from a manufacturer name. A batch freezes\nits equipment details; subsequent edits/archive do not rewrite that record. Editing equipment checks\nboth the household revision and the original equipment details to prevent a stale rebase overwriting\nanother edit.\n\nChoose a saved recipe revision, intended brew-day date/time/IANA zone and actual equipment per phase.\nA late brew-day start projects unfinished work from the current server time. Optional recipe yeast\npreparation is resolved by **calendar days**, keeping the intended local time across daylight-saving\ntransitions. It is product/recipe specific; choose the recipe reminder, skip it, or enter an explicit\nalternative. A requested preparation reminder already in the past must be skipped or rescheduled.\nA nonexistent civil time is rejected; a repeated time needs an earlier/later choice.\n\nReview resolved phase dates, original quantities and equipment before saving. Known phase volumes are\ncompared to declared equipment capacity using exact ml/l conversion. Missing volume/capacity remains\nunknown. Planned overlapping equipment reservations block a conflicting batch/replan atomically.\nUnknown-duration phases cannot establish a reliable future occupied interval. Starting/resuming checks\nactual equipment use again; running/paused work retains the physical equipment until explicit completion\nor cancellation, even after its projected end. Unassigned equipment cannot be checked or reserved.\nIndependent equipment permits simultaneous batches. Cancelling/pausing/replanning one affects only it.\n\nStart, pause/resume timed phases, adjust a pending start or actual timer deadline through review, and\ncomplete phases yourself. An elapsed brewing/fermentation timer does **not** complete dependencies or release equipment.\nUnknown-duration manual work has no fake countdown. Completing or cancelling releases its held equipment;\narchiving is available after the batch finishes/cancels and preserves recipes, readings and receipts.\n\n## Readings and corrections\n\nRecord the original value in °C, °F, specific gravity, °Plato, °Brix, ml or l. Decimal text retains up to\nsix places; localized display/input changes the decimal separator without floating-point arithmetic.\nMeasurement civil time/zone/fold and resolved instant are separate from authenticated actor/entry time.\nMeasurements cannot be future-dated. Manual input or named instrument attribution is explicit.\nInstrument transcription does not configure a live connector. Authenticated API reporting can declare\ninstrument attribution, without claiming that Mealshop independently authenticated a physical sensor.\n\nCalibration is explicitly unknown or declared with details, optional calibration time, reference\ntemperature and source link. No implicit SG/Plato/Brix conversion, temperature/alcohol correction,\nABV/IBU calculation or automatic readiness conclusion is made. Correct the latest reading with a reason;\nthe original stays saved. History loads in pages of 50 and retains the correction chain.\n\n## Personal reminders, conflicts and recovery\n\nThe existing single durable household worker handles food and brewing/fermentation events. Reminders start off; each\ncurrent writer can opt into their own batch reminders. Browser permission is reported separately.\nPrepare/start/active-ended/elapsed events link to the native batch. **Accepted** means Nextcloud accepted\nthe notification, not that a device displayed it. Interrupted attempts become **unknown** on restart;\nresending an unknown result requires duplicate-risk acceptance. At most three attempts/backoff, personal\nacknowledgements, cancelled obsolete events and missed events older than 24 hours retain the existing\ncooking delivery semantics. Readers neither subscribe nor see another account's subscription/history.\n\nDrafts survive background reads and rejected writes. Load the latest household state while keeping\nentries, then review again; a concurrently changed recipe/equipment still needs explicit reconciliation.\nA lost/uncertain save response retains the original immutable request/key; retrying it returns the same\noperation, even after server restart, rather than creating another recipe, batch or reading. A stale\nview is read-only until current server state is loaded. This is not an offline mutation queue.\n\nBeer recipes, equipment snapshots, batches, observations and receipts share the existing private 0600\n`household/cooking.json` transaction, fsync/rename recovery and 4 MiB publication limit. Recipe revisions\nare immutable; archiving retains history. Limits are 200 recipe revisions, 100 equipment records,\n100 batches/shared runs, 2,000 readings and 2,000 operation receipts; notification limits also apply.\nAn uncertain publication poisons writes/reads until verified recovery. Back up/restore the entire file\nwith the sole writer stopped; a pre-F14 binary cannot read populated F14 state. The delivered test\nverification never copies, hashes or inspects retained household directories.\n\n## API contract\n\nUse the normal authenticated `AppAPI` household proxy prefix, without hardcoded hostnames:\n`/api/households/{id}/domain` followed by:\n\n| Method | Path | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| GET | `/api/household/brewing/templates` | Validated method catalogue and primary source facts |\n| GET | `/api/household/brewing` | Latest recipe heads, equipment, actor-filtered batch state and worker health |\n| GET | `/api/household/brewing/recipes/{id}?revision=N` | Immutable recipe revision |\n| GET | `/api/household/brewing/batches/{id}` | Pinned recipe/equipment, phase state, personal notices and first reading page |\n| GET | `/api/household/brewing/batches/{id}/readings?before=ID` | Earlier batch readings, maximum 50 per page |\n| POST | `/api/household/brewing/preview` | Validated before/after state without publication |\n| POST | `/api/household/brewing` | Reviewed command, durable idempotency receipt and current state |\n\nMutation envelopes contain `expected_revision`, an opaque `idempotency_key`, `command` and `reviewed`.\nCommands save/archive recipes/equipment, create/replan/operate/cancel/archive batches, manage the current\nactor's subscription/notices, or append/correct a reading. `save_recipe` checks its pinned expected recipe\nrevision. Equipment edits include `expected_equipment`; new equipment omits it or supplies null.\nRetries require identical parsed payload, expected revision, actor and key, plus current write authority.\nResponses include the receipt's stable `result_id` so duplicate recipe/batch titles cannot select a different entity, including on replay.\nReceipt keys never appear in normal reads. Reusing a key for a different action conflicts.\n\nIndependently sourced cider-from-juice, white wine, red wine and traditional mead templates include\ntheir own phase order and process classification. White wine presses before fermentation; red wine\nferments on skins before pressing. They do not inherit grain/mash/sparge settings. All timings, readings,\nadditions and completion decisions remain explicitly recipe-specific. Automatic heater/\npump control, configured live device/vendor integrations, food-safety guarantees, automatic stock\nconsumption and non-beer chemistry are outside this delivered contract.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_guides_brewing_md {}

#[doc = "# Guided cooking and multi-day baking\n\nThe native Nextcloud **Cooking & baking** view uses the household's existing writer/reader roles.\nOpen it from the navigation, or choose **Start cooking or baking** in a recipe. Select a saved recipe\nrevision and serving count, review its preparation steps, then save a separate cooking session.\nIngredient quantities are scaled with the existing exact decimal arithmetic. Later recipe changes\nleave the source revision and serving count pinned in the session.\n\n## Prepare and review\n\nCooking starts when you choose a step. Bread, rolls, pizza and baguette plans also require a desired\nfinish and IANA timezone. Steps contain active work, expected passive time, shortest/longest passive\nbounds, dependencies, equipment, reminder lead time and optional repetitions/waits. Added steps require\na source/reason. Repeated occurrences are independent named timers; a later occurrence depends on the\nprevious one. Recipe parts seed dependencies; review any edits yourself.\n\nPlanning works backward from the desired finish, then respects dependencies, explicit start delays and\nactual running/completed work. Review every target, delay or timer adjustment before applying it.\nThe review shows old/new times, windows, equipment conflicts, overlapping active work and missed targets.\nTimes include the timezone and UTC offset. Nonexistent spring-transition times are rejected; a repeated\nautumn time requires the first or second occurrence. The server's bundled timezone data resolves times;\nabsolute UTC seconds, rather than browser-local arithmetic, drive deadlines.\n\nDurations and passive windows are user/recipe estimates, not a fermentation model. Record conditions\nand uncertainty where known; unknown temperature/fermentation conditions remain unknown. No result,\nreadiness, food-safety or health outcome is inferred from a countdown.\n\n## Cook and recover\n\nOne action starts a named timer with the saved active/passive duration. Concurrent timers remain\nindependent. Pause stores the remaining duration; resume preserves it. A deadline becoming overdue\ndoes not restart a timer or complete the step. Complete steps yourself to unlock dependent steps.\nThe planned timeline stays as reviewed until you explicitly review an adjustment; timer controls\nshow the actual deadline separately. An explicit step delay survives later target changes.\n\nSaved deadlines survive server restart. The browser advances its last server clock with monotonic\nelapsed time and refreshes while visible. If disconnected, its retained view becomes read-only with a\nstale-state message; reconnect loads current server state. This is not an offline mutation queue.\n\n## Personal Nextcloud reminders\n\nReminders are off by default. Each current owner/editor can opt into their own reminders for a session;\nreaders cannot change timers or subscribe. Browser permission is reported separately and does not\nestablish device delivery. Denied/unsupported desktop notifications leave due actions visible here.\n\nA server worker checks saved sessions every two seconds and attempts at most eight deliveries per\ncycle, rotating households fairly. It names the action and equipment for preparation/start/active-phase/\nelapsed reminders through Nextcloud's `AppAPI` notification endpoint. **Accepted** means Nextcloud accepted\nit; **unknown** means acceptance could not be established. Neither means a device displayed an alarm.\nInterrupted sending becomes unknown after restart and is never automatically replayed. Connection\nfailures retry at most three attempts with backoff. Retrying an unknown result needs explicit duplicate\nrisk acceptance. Events more than 24 hours late are listed as missed rather than sent as fresh alarms.\nAcknowledgement stops a queued reminder. Cancelling/completing/replanning or opting out cancels obsolete\nqueued reminders; a request already in flight cannot be recalled. Each account sees only its own\nsubscription/delivery history.\n\nSleeping operating systems and unreachable devices do not provide guaranteed alarms. Check the due\nactions and delivery history when returning. A stale worker check or storage failure is shown explicitly.\n\n## Transport and operations\n\nProtected routes live below `/api/households/{id}/domain` in `AppAPI` mode:\n\n| Method | Path | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| GET | `/api/household/cooking` | Session summaries, domain revision, clock and worker health |\n| GET | `/api/household/cooking/template` | Recipe ID/revision/servings/timezone → editable steps |\n| POST | `/api/household/cooking/preview` | Actor-bound create/replan review; no durable mutation |\n| GET | `/api/household/cooking/{id}` | Session, exact ingredients and actor's delivery history |\n| POST | `/api/household/cooking` | Reviewed apply, timer, cancellation, subscription and notice commands |\n\nEvery mutation supplies `expected_revision`. Preview apply also requires `reviewed:true`; the exact\ncached review expires after ten minutes or replacement by a newer review for that actor. Restart\ninvalidates cached reviews. A stale revision is rejected rather than silently overwriting changes.\nDelivery bookkeeping has a separate revision and cannot invalidate an unchanged schedule preview.\n`/api/household/cooking/internal` additionally requires an unforgeable internal Rust extension;\nbrowser headers or an owner bearer token cannot invoke the worker.\n\nSet optional `MEALSHOP_PUBLIC_NEXTCLOUD_URL` to the browser-visible Nextcloud base URL, including an\ninstallation prefix, when `NEXTCLOUD_URL` is an internal container hostname. Both are operator-trusted\nHTTP(S) URLs without credentials, query or fragment; browser requests cannot override them. No hostname\nis compiled into the client. Without the public override, notification links use `NEXTCLOUD_URL`.\n\n`household/cooking.json` is a private, bounded, versioned file under the existing household writer lock.\nAtomic pending-file writes sync data and directory. Corrupt/interrupted writes fail closed; restore a\nverified complete household backup. Back up membership, recipe revisions and all household files together.\nLimits: 100 saved sessions, 100 input stages/200 expanded occurrences per session, 2,000 reminder records\nper household, 4 MiB store, 20 actor-bound cached reviews. Remove completed/cancelled sessions and their\nreminder history to free capacity. No standalone/Flutter shell or external calendar integration is added.\n\n## Calendar anchors and completion-triggered waits (F31)\n\nStages optionally have `start_at` as a local date/time, IANA timezone and DST fold choice.\nThe server resolves and preserves the calendar anchor; ambiguous or nonexistent local times\ncannot silently become different instants. Calendar reminders do not prevent an earlier actual\ncompletion. Explicit reviewed delays still retain their separate `not_before` constraint. Multiple independent stages can share an anchor.\nNew recipe-derived stages with waiting time carry `start_after_work: true`: the completion\naction starts only their passive timer from the actual click. Active work remains an estimate\nfor calendar planning, so 2 minutes of estimated mixing plus 45 minutes waiting still means a\n07:08 completion starts a timer until 07:53. Legacy/custom stages default to the existing timed\nactive-and-passive behavior. Starting the next step acknowledges elapsed waiting timers;\npaused timers and timers still counting active work require their existing explicit transitions.\nThis acknowledges elapsed time, not physical readiness or food safety. No timer is automatically\nstarted by a scheduled notification, and no separate sourdough assessment is required.\n\nFor explicit homemade recipe lines (F33), the first consuming stage waits for the producing\npart's final stage, even when instructions were authored in a different array order. Ingredient\nreads retain `prepared_part` and `prepared_part_name`; the native view labels these as homemade.\nNo yield or replacement ingredients are inferred.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_guides_cooking_md {}

#[doc = "# Development and delivery\n\nStart with AGENTS.md and the affected spec. Use stable Rust; Cargo.toml declares the tested\nMSRV. Cargo.lock is committed; CI uses --locked. Unit preferences are presentation settings,\nnot a reason to erase source quantities. Keep core calculations independent of HTTP/storage.\n\n## Local gate\n```sh\npython3 scripts/generate_docs.py --check\npython3 scripts/import_catalogue.py --check\npython3 scripts/verify_catalogue.py\nnode --check src/web/demo.mjs\nnode --test tests/i18n.test.mjs\ncargo fmt --all --check\ncargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings\ncargo nextest run --workspace --locked\ncargo build --release --locked\ncargo deny check licenses\ncargo audit\nRUSTDOCFLAGS=\"-D warnings\" cargo doc --workspace --no-deps --locked\ncargo msrv verify -- cargo check --all-targets --locked\n```\nInstall cargo-nextest, cargo-deny, cargo-audit and cargo-msrv if absent. CI additionally\nuses cargo-llvm-cov and cargo-sbom. Tests may unwrap assertion setup; production may not.\n\n## Workflows\n- CI: fmt/clippy, nextest/coverage, MSRV, debug/release artifacts, SPDX SBOM and size metrics.\n- Security audit: `RustSec` advisory checks and dependency licence policy; runs daily as well.\n- Docs: strict rustdoc with requirements/specs/ADRs embedded, uploaded and deployed to Pages.\n- Renovate: daily self-hosted Renovate using `RENOVATE_TOKEN` (existing repository secret).\n\nAll workflows support `workflow_dispatch`. GitHub Pages must use the GitHub Actions build\nsource. Workflow permissions are scoped to the jobs that publish or create audit issues.\nNo upstream advisory ignores, ESP targets or machine-specific paths are retained.\nTo repeat a run: `gh workflow run <file>.yml --ref main --repo Th3Link/mealshop`.\nInspect `gh run list` and failed job logs; a dispatched workflow is not evidence of success.\n\n## Documentation\n`src/docs.rs` embeds the Markdown sources as rustdoc modules. Run `cargo doc --no-deps` and\nopen `target/doc/mealshop_server/docs/index.html`. The Docs workflow adds a root landing\npage. After adding a spec or ADR, update its index; docs generation discovers Markdown files.\n\n## MCP interoperability\n\nAfter installing the pinned Nextcloud development dependencies, check the committed tool schema with\n`node scripts/generate_mcp_schema.mjs --check`, run\n`python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -p 'test_mcp_stdio.py'`, then\n`node scripts/test_mcp_client.mjs target/debug/mealshop-server` after `cargo build --locked`.\nThe latter starts isolated synthetic HTTP/stdio fixtures and uses the official MCP client. CI also\nruns these checks against its compiled debug artifact; the SDK never enters shipped browser assets.\nThe `ExApp` container smoke test covers both rootless TCP and Unix-socket MCP adapters.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_guides_development_md {}

#[doc = "# Cooking history and household preferences\n\n## Record what actually happened\nOpen Cooking history in a household and record a completed cook, or use the action on a completed\ncooking session. A plan, saved schedule, elapsed timer or cancelled session never creates a history\nentry automatically. You confirm completion yourself. For a session, every step must already be\ncompleted; the recorded recipe revision and serving count come from that session. A session can be\nrecorded only once while its history entry exists. Removing the session does not remove its history.\n\nManual recording covers cooking performed outside the timers. Select a saved recipe/revision,\nservings, actual civil date/timezone and actual attendees. Effort and household/profile ratings are\noptional. Blank means unknown, not zero. Active effort is explicitly reported whole seconds (the\nform offers seconds/minutes); neither scheduled estimates nor elapsed wall time is presented as a\nmeasurement. Ratings use a labelled 1–5 scale. Individual ratings are household reports for dietary\nprofiles, not privately authenticated votes from linked Nextcloud accounts.\n\nAll current household readers can read this history. Owners/editors can record, correct and delete\nindividual entries. The original recorder/time and last correcting actor/time are retained. Recipe\nlinks open the pinned revision and return to history. Deleted dietary profiles remain referenced by\nID and appear as removed profiles; select current attendees before saving a correction. A future\ncompletion date in its selected timezone, unknown attendees and stale profile/history revisions are\nrejected without discarding the draft. A completed-session entry cannot be reassigned to another\nrecipe or serving count. Manual corrections are explicit source changes, not automatic revision updates.\n\n## Control local learning\nLearning is off by default. Only the household owner can enable it, choose which profile ratings may\ncontribute, reset/opt out, or bulk-delete history. Household cooking frequency and household ratings\nform the base; the selected profile ratings are optional. Everything stays within this household;\nno ratings, health data or training dataset are sent to an external learning service or logged.\n\nEnabling or changing scope starts a new epoch and clears learned aggregates. Only cooks explicitly\nsaved or corrected afterward contribute. Saving an unchanged scope retains the epoch and contributions.\nOld retained ratings are never replayed silently on opt-in,\nreset, restart or re-enabling. Correcting an old entry is an explicit new contribution. Reset disables\nlearning, removes aggregates and retains raw history. Delete all cooking history is a separate confirmed\naction; it removes records/contributions while leaving the chosen learning policy available for future\ncooks. Individual deletion/correction subtracts the prior contribution before adding any replacement.\n\n## Understand suggestions\nThe existing planner checks the entire bounded candidate pool against current attendees, declared\nexclusions, allergens/intolerances, compositional closure, frozen plus current ingredient declarations,\ntime budgets and exact quantity limits **before** ranking. Unknown coverage still needs explicit review.\nA positive rating cannot make a blocked recipe eligible. Selection still opens the normal plan review;\nno recipe is automatically scheduled, and scheduling does not count it as cooked.\n\nRule mode orders by declared ingredient dislikes and stable recipe identity, reserving an early\nposition for a recently added candidate within each equal-dislike group. Learned mode keeps that\nprimary dislike grouping, then applies a versioned integer rule:\n\n- Household rating points: `floor((sum + 6) × 100 / (count + 2)) − 300` (two neutral prior ratings).\n- Profile points: the integer mean of those smoothed points for selected attendees with permitted\n  rating evidence; missing ratings contribute no fabricated zero rating.\n- Add `5 × min(completed cooks, 10)` for recorded familiarity.\n- Subtract `30 × min(recent cooks, 3)` for variety. Recent means the seven reported civil days ending\n  on the selected meal day; date-only records are not treated as measured physical timelines.\n- Keep an early discovery position for a candidate without current-epoch cooking evidence. Each\n  group retains all feasible candidates, accessible through 20-result pages. A fresh epoch can have\n  no learning evidence even though raw history exists.\n\nFeedback is pooled by stable recipe identity. The explanation explicitly counts cooks from earlier\nrecipe revisions; hard constraints are always checked for the target revision. Explanations show\nobserved counts and whether history is absent, limited (1–2 cooks) or repeated (3+). These are history\nsupport labels, not probabilities, guarantees of enjoyment or allergy/health confidence.\n\nVersions `rules-v1` and `history-v1` have a checked synthetic holdout comparison. See\n[F11 evaluation](../page_specs_0011_recommendation_feedback_evaluation_md/index.html). Its acceptance and rating\nutility are offline proxies; real-world satisfaction and causal effectiveness remain unmeasured.\n\n## API and recovery\nProtected household routes use the existing `AppAPI` proxy below `/api/households/{home}/domain`:\n\n| Methods | Path | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| GET, POST, DELETE | `/api/household/history` | Paged history; explicitly record a completed cook; owner-only bulk clear |\n| GET, PUT, DELETE | `/api/household/history/{id}` | Read, correct or delete an entry |\n| GET, PUT | `/api/household/learning` | Current policy/epoch/counts; owner-only scope/reset |\n| POST | `/api/household/mealplan/suggestions` | One coherent candidate pool, explained order and paged previews |\n\nHistory saves supply `expected_revision`, `expected_profile_revision`, `confirmed_completed:true`,\n`source` and `input`. Manual source is `{kind:\"manual\", recipe_id, revision, servings}`; session source\nis `{kind:\"session\", run_id}`. Input contains `date`, IANA `timezone`, unique `attendees`, nullable\n`active_seconds`/`household_rating`, and a `ratings` map restricted to attendees with values 1–5.\nDeletes require `expected_revision` and `confirmed:true`. Policy updates require both expected\nrevisions and `{policy:{enabled,member_ids}}`; disabled policy uses an empty profile scope.\n\nHistory reads support one-based `page` (20 results), `q` up to 200 bytes and optional session identity.\nSuggestion input retains the existing plan `template`, accepts up to 1,000 unique recipe candidates\nand optional one-based `page`. Responses include total/page, model version, learning epoch and history\nrevision; each suggestion retains its ordinary preview and adds explicit recommendation evidence.\nNo caller may forge the recorder or owner role: `AppAPI` supplies the current authenticated actor.\nExisting CSRF, enabled state, household membership, read-only roles and sharing boundaries remain intact;\na recipe grant never conveys history or learning access.\n\n`household/history.json` stores entries and derived counts/sums in one atomic private transaction.\nIt is bounded to 1,000 entries/4 MiB, uses mode 0600 and the existing household writer lock.\nCounters, dates, scope, identity monotonicity and reconstructed current-epoch aggregates are checked\non load. A `.history.pending` marker or corrupted aggregate fails startup. An ambiguous write stops\nmutations until verified recovery. Stop the writer and restore a verified complete household backup;\ndo not remove a pending marker blindly. No migration rewrites existing recipe, plan, cooking or grant\nfiles. Retain source-sharing restrictions when implementing future clients or exports.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_guides_history_and_preferences_md {}

#[doc = "# Protected local household profiles\n\nEnable the F09 slice by providing `MEALSHOP_HOUSEHOLD_TOKEN` (32–200 random ASCII letters,\ndigits, underscores or hyphens). Generate a strong operator secret with your normal secret\nmanager; do not commit it or put it in URLs. Without this variable household routes are absent.\nThe executable remains loopback-only. This is one household with one operator credential,\nnot a hosted account system. An optional distinct `MEALSHOP_HOUSEHOLD_READ_TOKEN` grants profile export and dietary evaluation only; writes/removals require the operator token. Anyone holding that credential can read/export, correct\nand remove every profile in this household. Revocation/rotation means changing the configured\ntoken and restarting; no token is written into profile files or browser storage.\n\n`MEALSHOP_HOUSEHOLD_DIR` defaults to `var/household`. Use a dedicated local directory separate\nfrom recipe storage. Newly created Unix directories/files use 0700/0600; existing directory\npermissions stay intact. Profiles use a schema-1, revisioned `profiles.json` snapshot (at most\n100 members); no profile revision history is retained. Editing/deletion replaces the snapshot.\nThis removes members from subsequent API reads; operator backups and previously exported\ncopies are outside server deletion. Backups contain sensitive profile data and require the\nsame access restrictions as the live store.\n\nThe demo asks for the operator token in a password field; it stays in page memory only.\nLoad profiles before editing. Select an existing member or New member; allergy/intolerance\nnotes are separate from hard ingredient exclusions and soft dislikes. Save or remove submits\nthe full household snapshot with its expected revision. A stale edit returns a conflict; copy\nunsaved changes before reloading/reconciling. The review button evaluates current recipe\ningredient identities for every recorded member. No profile data or token is logged.\n\n## API contracts\nAll endpoints require `Authorization: Bearer <operator token>` and return `Cache-Control:\nno-store`. GET `/api/household/profiles` exports the complete schema/revision/member JSON.\nPUT the same path with `{\"expected_revision\":0,\"members\":[...]}` creates/updates/removes\nmembers by replacement. A member has `id` (lowercase identifier), `name`, `allergies` (text\nnotes), `allergen_ids` (structured hard groups), `intolerances` (separate review notes), `excluded_ingredients` and `disliked_ingredients` (canonical catalogue ID arrays).\nRemoving a member means omitting it from the replacement array; an empty array removes all.\nNo separate unauthenticated member lookup or profile route exists.\n\nPOST `/api/household/evaluate` accepts `member_ids` and `ingredient_ids`. Hard ingredient\nexclusions are unioned across every selected member and return `blocked`. Known English/German\nnames/aliases are resolved conservatively to matching catalogue identities for exclusion\nchecks; ambiguous names never establish safety. Dislikes are returned separately and cannot\nremove a hard exclusion. Unknown ingredients remain explicitly listed. Even with no exclusion,\nstatus is `review_required`, never safe: the catalogue lacks complete allergen mappings and\ncross-contamination data. Allergy/intolerance notes are retained for human review; this slice\ndoes not infer an allergen ontology from unreviewed text. No learning mechanism can mutate\nhard constraints. Group selection must reference existing household members.\n\n## Persistence and recovery\nWrites run on blocking workers, validate the whole replacement, sync a private `.pending`\nfile, rename it over the current snapshot, and sync the directory on Unix. Ambiguous I/O\nfailures disable further writes until operator recovery. Startup rejects malformed/unknown\nschemas, invalid IDs, oversized data and an interrupted pending write. Exclusive `.household.lock`\nprevents concurrent process writers. After a crash, stop all writers, back up the entire\nprotected directory and inspect the snapshot/pending file before removing a verified stale\nlock. Preserve only a validated current snapshot in the recovery directory; never blindly\nreplace a good snapshot with an incomplete pending file. Restart and verify authorized export.\nNo automatic Git history, cloud sync or external writers are supported. Linux local filesystems\nare the initial target; cross-platform crash durability has not been validated.\n\n## Structured allergen constraints\nGET `/api/household/allergens` returns the versioned reference registry to authorized readers.\nThe demo offers 14 English-base allergen groups with German labels. Selected `allergen_ids`\nare hard constraints. 106 positive food-identity classifications retain exact source food IDs,\nforms and an FSA source reference. These are reference identity classifications, not laboratory\nmeasurements of retail products. Milk-group constraints also match explicitly named lactose-free\nmilk; absence of lactose does not remove the milk identity. See the source selection documentation\nin data/README.md. Other allergy and intolerance notes remain review-required; exact legacy group\nIDs in those notes are also treated conservatively as hard constraints. No natural-language\ninterpretation or personalised threshold is inferred.\n\nResults include `allergen_matches` with member/ingredient/group/source, the classification version,\nhard ingredient matches and separate dislikes. Every unblocked result requires review because\nallergen coverage is positive-only and cross-contact is unmodelled. Hosted user management and\nadditional source coverage do not alter this rule. Read-only credentials cannot correct or\nremove members; attempts return 403. Unknown credentials return 401. Rotate either configured\ncredential by restarting. Existing schema-1 profiles load with empty new fields.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_guides_household_md {}

#[doc = "# Household inventory and manual offers\n\nOpen **Inventory** in the native Nextcloud navigation. **Inventory**, **Offers** and **Stock history**\nshare the current household and its existing owner/editor/reader permissions. Readers can inspect\nrecords; only current writers can review and confirm changes. Nothing uses a separate login or host.\n\n## Record what is actually there\n\n**Add stock** records an ingredient, an explicitly saved product or an unlinked item, an exact\nquantity/unit, location and optional date/batch. Unlinked stock never matches recipes by name.\nQuantities retain the original input and normalize only within mass, volume or count: kg/g and l/ml\nare compatible; water does not imply a volume-to-mass conversion. A product purchase matches its\nexact barcode; an ingredient requirement may use product stock only through its explicit mapping.\n\nEdit location/date without rewriting the original quantity. **Correct quantity** records an observed\nremaining balance, including zero, and a reason. Active reservations cannot be undercut; release them\nfirst. Empty lots can be archived. Passed dates remain visible but are not proposed for use; missing\ndates are explicit and never represented as a safety guarantee.\n\nEvery change first shows **Review changes**. Compare before/after stock, reservations, purchase\nrequirements and any unbooked remainder before **Confirm reviewed changes**. Review alone writes\nnothing. Stock history retains who booked what and when, the original source and correction reason.\n\n## Cover shopping and record physical changes\n\n- From the shopping list choose **Use stock**, select contributions and a use date, then review.\n  Compatible lots are reserved in date order. A planned meal's production date also constrains the\n  reservation. Held quantities reduce proposed purchases but do not consume physical stock.\n- Fully reserved/consumed entries appear under **Covered by stock**, separately from **Purchased**.\n  Partial coverage shows reserved and used quantities alongside the remaining purchase. Changing or\n  removing a source releases obsolete holds; already consumed coverage retains its original facts.\n- Shopping check-off creates a purchase receipt. **Record purchased stock** explicitly selects\n  unbooked receipts and their storage metadata; confirmation creates lots. Check-off alone never\n  changes physical stock. Reverse the stock booking before unchecking a booked receipt.\n- After completing a cooking session, choose **Record cooking consumption**. Select the compatible\n  shopping contributions it fulfils and whether to use unreserved stock. Requirements come from the\n  saved recipe revision and servings, not guessed screen text. Own reservations are used first;\n  another contribution's holds cannot be consumed. Missing quantities remain visibly unbooked.\n- **Reverse booking** appends the opposite movement with a reason. It does not erase history and\n  cannot make stock negative or steal reservations. Restoring a previously archived lot revives it.\n\n## Manually record an offer\n\nCreate shops in the shopping list, then **Record offer** with an explicit ingredient/saved product,\npackage quantity, positive price (at most two decimal places), currency, inclusive validity dates,\nsource label and optional HTTPS source link. No website is contacted and no substitution is inferred.\nSupported currencies are EUR, GBP, USD and CHF; prices are recorded without currency conversion.\n\n**Add offer to shopping** chooses package count, shopping date and intended profiles. Current known\nexclusions block the operation; unknown dietary data requires explicit review. Confirmation checks\ncurrent profiles again. Archived/out-of-date offers cannot be added. Editing or archiving an offer\nnever changes existing shopping contributions: each retains its package, shop, price and source.\n\n## Retry, concurrency and persistence\n\nThe expected revision protects changes shared with shopping. On a definite conflict, load latest\nstate while keeping entries, adjust the draft and review again. A confirmed request with an ambiguous\nnetwork result is locked. **Retry confirmed booking** sends the same actor-bound key and exact body,\nreturning the original booking even after server restart; it cannot double-book. Leaving requires\nexplicit discard. Check history before starting another request after leaving an uncertain result.\nNo operation keys or household content are saved to browser storage.\n\nInventory, offers and operation receipts live together in the existing private `household/shopping.json`\ntransaction, with 0600 permissions, synced atomic replacement and the 4 MiB cap. Preserve the complete\nfile for backup/restore. Old snapshots with no inventory remain readable. Once used, downgrade requires\nan explicit migration that preserves movements, receipts and shopping coverage; never delete receipts\nor retry keys to repair a failure. Pending/ambiguous publication fails closed until storage is recovered.\n\nLimits per household: 500 lots, 200 offers, 2,000 allocations, 1,000 physical movements and 2,000\noperation receipts; selections are bounded to 100 entries. History is paginated. Capacity errors are\nexplicit; there is no silent deletion of audit facts. Provider imports, automatic purchasing and\nnutrition/expiry safety claims are outside this delivery.\n\n[Specification](../page_specs_0015_inventory_offers_specification_md/index.html) ·\n[Atomicity decision](../page_docs_adr_0024_household_stock_and_offers_md/index.html) ·\n[Acceptance evidence](../page_specs_0015_inventory_offers_validation_md/index.html)\n\n## API contract\n\nPaths are relative to the authenticated household domain and retain its existing JSON request marker,\nCSRF boundary, enabled-state and current membership checks.\n\n| Request | Contract |\n| --- | --- |\n| `GET /api/household/inventory` | Exact lot balances, allocations, offers and current dietary status, unbooked purchase receipts and completed cooking metadata. `limit` 1–500 (default 50), `offset`; `history_limit` 1–200 (default 50), `history_offset`. Optional `run_id` adds canonical completed-recipe requirements and compatible source IDs. |\n| `POST /api/household/inventory/preview` | `{ expected_revision, idempotency_key, reviewed: false, command }`; validates the same bounded transaction without publishing and returns before/after lot/shopping changes, allocations, new movements and changed offers. |\n| `POST /api/household/inventory` | Same immutable request with `reviewed: true`; returns operation ID, current inventory and `replayed`. Reuse the exact body/key on an uncertain result. |\n\nCommands are `create_lot`, `update_lot`, `adjust_lot`, `archive_lot`, `reserve`, `release`, `receive`,\n`consume`, `reverse`, `save_offer`, `archive_offer` and `add_offer`. The Rust tagged commands and typed\nVue contracts define their payloads. Keys are actor-bound opaque ASCII strings of 16–128 characters.\nThe revision belongs to the shared shopping transaction; changing key payloads or stale new intents\nreturns a conflict. Replays require current write permission and healthy storage, then return the\ncurrent projection without reapplying the original physical movement. No operation keys or private\ncommand receipts appear in the read projection.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_guides_inventory_md {}

#[doc = "# MCP recipe tools\n\nF17 supplies six household-scoped tools in the existing Nextcloud installation. It does not\nconnect a model or send data until you configure and invoke your own MCP client. Access defaults\nto Disabled for every account. The local protected demo exposes the same contracts with its\nexisting bearer token; this guide describes the delivered Nextcloud connection.\n\n## Connect a client\n\n1. Open a household's **Assistant tools** view. The owner selects **Read and preview** or\n   **Read, preview and create** for each current account and saves. A reader's role always caps\n   access at read/preview, even if an older creation grant remains after a role change.\n2. Copy the exact **Household MCP endpoint** from that view. It uses Nextcloud's configured\n   installation path and proxy; do not substitute the container address or service secret.\n3. Create an app password in that account's Nextcloud security settings. App passwords retain\n   the account's ordinary Nextcloud permissions; they are **not** household-scoped OAuth tokens.\n   For a client outside your trust, use a separate Nextcloud account with only the household role\n   and MCP grant it needs. The UI never asks for or stores passwords.\n4. Place this checkout's `scripts/mcp_stdio.py` on the MCP host. Python 3 uses only its standard\n   library. Configure the host's stdio command as `python3` and its sole argument as the absolute\n   path to that script. Supply these values through the host's protected environment:\n\n   ```text\n   MEALSHOP_MCP_URL=<exact copied endpoint>\n   MEALSHOP_MCP_USER=<Nextcloud account ID>\n   MEALSHOP_MCP_APP_PASSWORD=<app password from protected storage>\n   ```\n\n   Do not put the password in command arguments, a checked-in configuration, shell history or\n   browser storage. The native view provides a credential-free environment example.\n5. Allow read operations as needed. Configure your host to show the validated preview and request\n   explicit confirmation before `recipe_create`. A recipe's text is untrusted content and cannot\n   grant tools, change accounts or authorize a write.\n\nThe bridge requires HTTPS, except literal loopback/localhost HTTP for local testing. It refuses\nredirects and URL credentials/query/fragment, uses the existing Nextcloud authentication and write\nmarker, and emits only newline-delimited JSON-RPC on stdout. Errors omit upstream HTML and credentials.\nRequests are limited to 128 KiB, responses to 4 MiB and network waits to 30 seconds. A lost response\ncan be ambiguous: preserve the original confirmed write's preview ID and key when retrying.\nNo remote fetch, SSE subscription, sampling, tasks or OAuth service is advertised. A generic hosted\nHTTP client that requires OAuth discovery is outside this authentication contract; use the supplied\nstdio bridge. Protocol support is MCP 2026-07-28 and legacy 2025-11-25, verified with the official SDK.\n\n## Tool flow\n\n| Tool | Input | Result / effect |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `ingredient_search` | Optional localized `query`, `page` | Up to 20 declared catalogue/household ingredients and stable IDs. |\n| `ingredient_get` | Ingredient `id` | Original declarations, sources, available conversions and unknowns. |\n| `recipe_search` | Optional `query`, `page` | Up to 20 owned title summaries or imported source links. |\n| `recipe_get` | Recipe `id`, optional pinned `revision` | Owned recipe with nutrition from its saved inputs, or original source URL only. |\n| `recipe_preview_create` | Structured `recipe` matching discovered schema | Canonical validation, normalized exact values, preview ID and expiry; saves nothing. |\n| `recipe_create` | `preview_id`, opaque `idempotency_key`, `confirmed: true` | First immutable recipe revision and replay status; creation scope required. |\n\nThe tool list contains machine-readable schemas. Use stable ingredient IDs and **decimal strings**,\nfor example a line with `ingredient_id: \"oats\"` and `quantity: { amount: \"0.100500\", unit: \"kg\" }`.\nCanonical validation preserves the original quantity and normalizes it to `100.500000` grams.\nA floating-point number, unknown ingredient, invalid image reference, invalid recipe composition or\nimplicit volume/mass conversion fails with a structured tool error, as in the regular recipe API.\nUnknown nutrition/allergens remain unknown. Tools never claim dietary safety.\n`ingredient_get.reference_conversions` contains only bundled portions matching that exact ingredient ID,\nform, source record and source URL, retaining version, licence and full conversion evidence. Copy the\nselected entry's `evidence` into the recipe line's `conversion` when using its `unit`. An empty entries\narray means no matching bundled portion is known; use mass or provide your own explicit evidence.\nNever reuse a neighbouring food's portion or assume ml equals grams.\n\nImported recipes expose **only their retained original HTTPS source URL**, including preview,\nsearch and older revisions subsequently marked imported. No imported title, ingredients, instructions,\nimage or nutrition is exported. Search matches imported source URLs rather than their private titles.\nProfiles, plans, shopping, history, preference learning and other households are unavailable.\n\n## Confirmation, expiry and retries\n\nA preview is bound to the current account, scope-settings revision and ingredient revision. It expires\nafter ten monotonic minutes; a bounded household cache holds at most 16 previews and 4 per account.\nExpired bodies are removed when adding previews and by a household cleanup task within a further 30 seconds.\nPreviews never reach disk. A restart requires a new preview for an **uncommitted** write. Changing\nscope settings or ingredient declarations invalidates a pending creation; obtain and review a new\npreview. Saving identical grants preserves a valid draft. Household membership is checked on every call.\n\nBefore creating, the host must let the user inspect and deny the proposed recipe. `confirmed: true`\nis the client's attestation, not cryptographic evidence of a physical human review. Generate and retain\none opaque ASCII key (16–128 characters: letters, digits, `_`, `-`, `.`) for the confirmed insertion.\n\nThe first immutable recipe file atomically includes the authenticated actor, timestamp, preview ID and\nkey. Two concurrent requests or a retry after timeout, expiry or server restart return the **same first\nrevision**, without another recipe or audit row. A different preview under that actor/key returns a\nconflict. Another account cannot use your preview. Scope/role revocation applies even to retries.\nLater edits keep the receipt in revision one; subsequent imported restrictions still apply to replays.\nThe native activity list displays actor/time and pinned revision links, never keys or credentials.\n\n## Revoke and recover\n\nSet an account to Disabled and save to stop subsequent tool calls. Reduce its household role or remove\nmembership when appropriate. Revoke an app password in Nextcloud to invalidate that credential across\nits underlying account authority. Nextcloud's own authentication cache can delay credential revocation;\ndisable the MCP grant to block subsequent tool calls directly in Mealshop. A request already admitted before revocation may finish; revocation\nis enforced on subsequent calls. There is no background assistant or speculative execution.\n\nSettings are private `household/mcp.json` (schema 1, mode 0600), with optimistic revision checks and\nsynced atomic rename. A settings conflict retains the browser draft until explicit reload. An interrupted\n`.mcp.pending`, invalid settings or an ambiguous write fails closed. After a failed settings write,\nall RPC tools and scope reads return unavailable rather than using stale grants. Stop **all** writers, inspect only\nthe affected household's verified settings and recover deliberately; never silently reset grants.\nRecipe publication/recovery uses the [existing immutable recipe procedure](../page_docs_guides_recipes_md/index.html). Do not delete a\nsuccessful first revision or its receipt to retry. Backups and restoration must preserve the complete\nfirst revision. Older servers do not understand creation receipts: do not downgrade a household with\nMCP-created recipes without an explicit format migration preserving audit/idempotency information.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe [F17 evidence](../page_specs_0017_mcp_validation_md/index.html) records real Nextcloud, browser, installed restart\nand container checks. Reproduce isolated wire compatibility with:\n\n```sh\ncargo build --locked\nnpm --prefix clients/nextcloud ci --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund\nnode scripts/generate_mcp_schema.mjs --check\npython3 -m unittest discover -s tests -p 'test_mcp_stdio.py'\nnode scripts/test_mcp_client.mjs target/debug/mealshop-server\n```\n\nThe official MCP SDK is pinned as a development dependency only; the Rust runtime and Python bridge\nadd no dependency. CI exercises modern direct HTTP and modern/legacy stdio with isolated synthetic data.\nReal-instance Playwright checks require the existing test credentials in protected environment variables\nand create only ledger-recorded synthetic households. Never use retained customer households as fixtures.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_guides_mcp_md {}

#[doc = "# Family meal planning\n\nEnable household access as described in household.md. The executable links recipes and protected\nplanning automatically when `MEALSHOP_HOUSEHOLD_TOKEN` is configured. Embedding applications use\n`family_router`; separate standalone household builders have no recipe store and planning returns\n`recipe_store_unavailable`. Operator and reader roles are the same as F09. No tokens or profile\nsnapshots are stored in browser storage. Planning responses are `no-store` and data is not logged.\n\n## Demo flow\nEnter the household token, then Load family and saved plans. Choose date/meal, attendees,\nrecipe revision, target servings and available active/elapsed/advance seconds. Use the candidate\nmulti-select to request suggestions. The native Nextcloud client submits its whole recipe pool\n(up to 1,000 unique recipes) and pages the coherent order in 20-result pages. A manual selection or suggested alternative\nmust be previewed before applying. Preview shows exclusions, selected members' current review\nnotes, duration estimates, scaled nutrition and signed changes in required shopping grams.\nBlocked candidates cannot be applied. Every unblocked meal still requires explicit review of\nunknown dietary coverage and batch-time uncertainty. Changing inputs invalidates the preview.\n\nAfter acknowledgement, Apply saves the meal slot and refreshes aggregate shopping requirements\nfor all saved slots. Load a saved meal to change attendance/recipe/servings/time. Removal has its\nown negative-quantity preview and operator-only apply. No shopping item is purchased or external\nlist modified. A profile/plan revision conflict requires reloading and a fresh preview; stale\nchoices are never silently applied. Profile/reference changes mark saved slots for renewed review.\n\n## Read-only calendar import\nThe file input accepts an attendance JSON snapshot up to 32 KiB. Example:\n```json\n{\"source\":\"Family calendar export\",\"event_id\":\"event-42\",\"date\":\"2026-11-09\",\"member_ids\":[\"member-id-from-household-export\"]}\n```\nConsent must be checked after import; merely choosing a file does not consent. Check Use calendar\nattendance to use its proposal; uncheck it and use the attendee selector to override. Source,\ndate and proposed IDs stay visible. Calendar date must match the meal date. Clear removes the\nsnapshot from the draft. Member IDs must already be mapped to this household; unmapped attendees\ncannot silently become missing dietary constraints. No network calendar connection, recurrence\nexpansion or external calendar write occurs. This adapter consumes read-only snapshot data;\nprovider integrations remain separate deployment work.\n\n## API\nGET `/api/household/mealplan` returns schema/revision, saved meals, current profile revision,\nslots requiring renewed review and derived `shopping_requirements`.\nPOST `/api/household/mealplan/preview` accepts:\n```json\n{\"date\":\"2026-11-09\",\"meal\":\"dinner\",\"recipe\":{\"id\":\"recipe-00000001\",\"revision\":1},\n \"attendees\":[\"member-id\"],\"calendar\":null,\"servings\":2,\n \"active_seconds\":1800,\"elapsed_seconds\":3600,\"advance_seconds\":0}\n```\n`attendees: null` uses `calendar.member_ids`; that calendar object additionally requires\n`consent: true`. Explicit attendees override it. Targets are 1–1000000 integer servings.\nActive/meal-day elapsed budgets are 0–86400 seconds; advance lead is 0–2592000 seconds.\nMeal keys are lowercase ASCII letters/underscores, 1–40 bytes. Dates are validated, including\nmonth lengths/leap years. Empty steps yield `preparation_time_unknown`; totals are sequential\nrecipe estimates and do not infer parallel work or batch-time scaling.\n\nPreview returns `plan`, `profile_revision`, `plan_revision`, resolved attendees/source/override,\nF09 review evidence, `feasible`, conflict codes, active/elapsed/advance timing, rationally scaled\nnutrition and signed `shopping_delta`. No persistence occurs.\n\nPOST `/api/household/mealplan/suggestions` takes `{\"template\":{...plan...},\"candidates\":[\n{\"id\":\"recipe-00000001\",\"revision\":1}],\"page\":1}` (1–1,000 unique candidates).\nThe entire feasible pool is ranked once per request, with at most 20 full previews returned per page.\nResponses include model/history versions, total/page and recommendation evidence; owner-controlled\nlearning and reset are described in [Cooking history](../page_docs_guides_history_and_preferences_md/index.html).\nEvery rejected candidate includes conflicts/errors. No match returns `no_feasible_meal`;\npreferences cannot relax exclusions.\n\nPOST `/api/household/mealplan/apply` takes `{\"plan\":{...},\"expected_revision\":0,\n\"profile_revision\":1,\"reviewed\":true}`. Use the revisions from the preview. Matching date/meal\nreplaces one slot; recipe revisions stay pinned. A conflict returns 409; hard/time conflicts or\nmissing review return 422. Readers cannot apply (403). Acknowledgement means human review, not\nan allergen-safety certificate. The server recomputes constraints; client `feasible` fields are\nnot accepted. GET subsequently derives shopping quantities from the saved pinned revisions.\n\nPOST `/api/household/mealplan/remove-preview` with the meal settings previews removing that\nslot. Apply with `remove: true`, current revisions and review acknowledgement removes it.\nThe removal preview carries the correct negative quantities, including after constraints change.\n\n## Storage/recovery\n`mealplan.json` is a schema-1 snapshot in the protected household directory, at most 100 slots\nand 1 MiB. The existing household process lock covers it. Writes use private `.mealplan.pending`,\nfile sync, rename and Unix directory sync. Ambiguous I/O failure disables further plan writes;\nprofiles remain independently accessible. Startup rejects malformed, oversized, duplicate-slot\nor interrupted plans instead of silently creating an empty planner. Export JSON and backups can\ncontain attendance and calendar IDs; protect them like profiles. Removing profiles does not\nrewrite existing plans; remove affected slots explicitly if their historical attendance must\nalso be removed. Previously exported copies/backups are outside server revocation.\n\nFor recovery, stop all writers and back up both recipe and household directories consistently.\nInspect any pending file and snapshot before removing a verified stale household lock. Work on\na recovery copy, preserve all recipe revisions referenced by plans, restore a validated complete\nsnapshot, then verify protected plan export, nutrition and shopping quantities. Do not directly\nedit files while the application is running. Network filesystems and cross-platform crash\nbehaviour are not validated; Linux local filesystems are the initial target.\n\n## Production and mealprep allocations (F31)\n\nA saved plan's `servings` is the production count. Optional `allocations` contains date, meal and\npositive whole serving count for each eating day. The total cannot exceed production; fewer\nallocated portions leave the remainder unallocated. An explicit empty array means no eating days\nare assigned. Omitting allocations preserves the existing single-slot behavior. The root date/meal remains the stable production identity during edits,\nincluding when an eating day is moved. One shared attendee list applies conservatively to every\nallocation; existing hard exclusions and profile revisions still apply.\n\nOptional `preparation` contains a date and HH:MM time. Preparation must not follow an eating day.\nThe native calendar shows these in the same week as meals. Explicit preparation-only entries use\nmeal `preparation`, with no invented attendees; assigning actual eating days requires attendees\nand the usual dietary review. Shopping requirements count production once. Selecting two eating\ndays of the same production exports one canonical source to shopping. Removing the entry removes\nall its allocations; the native review explains the resulting shopping delta.\n\nOpening cooking from a saved entry pins its recipe and production count and records a checked\n`plan_slot` origin. Root stages inherit the chosen calendar preparation time; individual stage\nanchors can be edited in the reviewed cooking draft. Saved active stage times appear in the\nmeal-plan calendar and link to their cooking session. Preparation-only metadata does not itself\npromise notification delivery: create/review the durable cooking session and explicitly subscribe.\nChanges to a meal plan do not silently rewrite an already reviewed cooking session.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_guides_meal_planning_md {}

#[doc = "# Android app development and installation\n\nF35 P1 provides native shared shopping against the existing Nextcloud installation. Rust remains the\nauthority for quantities, membership and shopping transactions. Shopping and More are the active\nnavigation destinations; profiles, recipe authoring, plans, cooking and camera capture follow P2–P5.\n\n## Toolchain and emulator\n\nThe customer selected Android APKs and requested an actual emulator. The pinned toolchain is in\n`clients/flutter/toolchain.json`: Flutter 3.50.0/Dart 3.14.0, JDK 17, Android compile/target 36,\nminimum 24, NDK 28.2.13676358, API 36 Google APIs `x86_64` Pixel 6 emulator. Linux `x86_64` and working\n`/dev/kvm` access are required by the local setup helper. It verifies official download checksums,\nkeeps tools under ignored `var/mobile/`, accepts the Android SDK package licences for this development setup, and never resets Nextcloud or another AVD.\n\nFrom the repository root:\n\n```sh\npython3 scripts/mobile.py bootstrap\npython3 scripts/mobile.py doctor\npython3 scripts/mobile.py emulator --host-gpu\npython3 scripts/mobile.py status\npython3 scripts/mobile.py browser\npython3 scripts/mobile.py build\npython3 scripts/mobile.py install\n```\n\nWait for status `1` and `Mealshop_API36` before installing. The `browser` step selects the Chrome\nbrowser supplied by the official Google APIs system image, only inside this AVD. No personal browser\nprofile or account synchronization is used. A compatible external browser is required for Login Flow v2.\nThe abandoned API 35 guest had system-service/FUSE failures; its data was retained. The default/CI uses\n`SwiftShader`; `--host-gpu` selects local hardware rendering. Both disable Vulkan and `GuestAngle`. On this\nNVIDIA Linux host, `SwiftShader` blocked the guest OpenGL pipe and caused system-server watchdog\nrestarts; the visible emulator uses `--host-gpu`. Stop only this AVD before changing renderers; retain\nits data. These are emulator settings, not application rendering changes.\n\n`install`, `browser` and `integration` verify the AVD identity, restore port forwarding, supply AC power\nand wake/unlock only this emulator. A cold restart otherwise loses its reverse forwarding.\n\nThe emulator uses its own ADB server on\n5038 and console port 5556, leaving the default ADB server alone. Use `emulator --headless` in CI;\nthe default opens a real emulator window. The helper retains an existing AVD. The pinned old\n`avdmanager` compatibility command creates only this API 36 AVD; package installation uses the new\nAndroid CLI with metrics disabled. Tools/downloads/keys and emulator state are ignored, never committed.\n\n`install` forwards emulator loopback port 8080 to the host's 8080. Enter `http://127.0.0.1:8080` in\nthis debug build. An emulator's loopback alone does not reach the host. Real installations use a\nconfigurable HTTPS Nextcloud URL, including its installation prefix. Do not disable TLS verification.\nOnly debug manifests and client validation allow explicit loopback HTTP; arbitrary LAN HTTP is denied.\n\n## APKs and stable upgrades\n\nThe development application ID is `org.mealshop.pocket.dev`; display name Mealshop. The helper creates\nand retains `var/mobile/development.keystore`, owner-only, for local development upgrades. This is a\ndevelopment identity, with Android's standard development key password, not production signing.\nBack up the key privately if its installed development app must survive moving this checkout.\nNever commit or upload it. Losing the key requires uninstalling that development app and loses its\nlocal credential/cache. Production release signing and store distribution remain separate work.\n\n`python3 scripts/mobile.py build` writes:\n\n- `clients/flutter/build/app/outputs/flutter-apk/app-arm64-v8a-debug.apk`: most current Android phones.\n- `app-armeabi-v7a-debug.apk`: supported older ARM phones.\n- `app-x86_64-debug.apk`: this emulator.\n- Matching `.apk.sha256` and `.apk.build.json`: checksum, source commit, dirty-source flag and app ID.\n\nInstall an upgrade with `adb install -r <apk>` using the same retained signing identity. Do not uninstall\nor clear application data as an upgrade step. Debug build numbers can be overridden using the Flutter\ncommand passthrough if needed. Physical-device USB installation requires that device's USB debugging\nand authorization; no physical handset was supplied to the agent. Emulator and real-phone evidence\nare distinct in F35 verification. For this local HTTP-only test instance, an explicitly authorized\nUSB-connected phone can use `adb -s <phone-serial> reverse tcp:8080 tcp:8080`, then enter the same\nloopback origin. Install the matching ARM APK with `adb -s <phone-serial> install -r <apk>`. The helper\nintentionally targets only its owned emulator; it never automatically changes a physical phone.\n\nThe GitHub Android workflow analyzes/tests, runs an accelerated emulator and uploads APKs. Each job\nuses an ephemeral development key, with **no retained distribution key in an untrusted PR job**.\nThose CI APKs are useful fresh-install artifacts, but are not upgrades of a locally signed app or a\ndifferent CI run. The retained local build supplies the continuous handset trial path.\n\n## Sign in and choose a household\n\nEnter the installation URL and continue in the external system browser. Nextcloud Login Flow v2\ncreates this device's revocable app password; the app never asks for your master password and never\nreceives the `ExApp` secret. Login/poll targets and the returned canonical installation must match the\nconfigured server origin/prefix. Credential-bearing redirects are rejected. Authenticated API calls use the explicit `index.php`\nfront controller, so installation prefixes work without assuming Pretty-URL rewrites.\n\nReturn to Mealshop after approving access. Select or create a household, or accept an invitation.\nThe last selected household is remembered. With several households on a first login, choose explicitly;\nthe app does not automatically load the first private household. Reader roles have no mutation actions.\nMore contains account/server details, household switching, language, shops/order, products, pending\nchanges and sign-out. Sign-out deletes the protected local record and its encryption key. Revoke the\napp password in Nextcloud account security to remove the device's server access as well.\n\n## Shopping and offline behaviour\n\nAdd a free-text item or select a real ingredient. Existing ingredient groups supply localized labels and\na group filter; unclassified free text remains unclassified. Shop ordering is preserved within filters. Enter an exact positive quantity with up to six\ndecimal places and an explicit unit. German uses a comma and English a dot; thousands separators\nare not accepted. No binary floating point or implicit mass/volume conversion. Separate purchased\namounts remain visible, with undo; item details show each manual/recipe/plan/product/offer source.\nManual sources can be edited or removed without rewriting purchase provenance.\n\nAssign shops in item details; manage shops, ordering, shopping rounds and opt-in order learning in\nMore. Products support manual GTIN/check-digit input, package quantity, reviewed ingredient matching,\nexisting saved products and explicit consent before Open Food Facts lookup. Lookup evidence retains\nits original label, source and licence. Camera scanning follows P5.\n\nA locally saved add/check is visibly pending until the server confirms it. New offline entries\nappear directly in the list and can be checked before first transmission. The server then creates\nand purchases only that new source atomically; older demand for the same ingredient stays untouched.\nAn already attempted addition must be reconciled before changing its local purchase flag. Offline changes survive\nprocess death in an AES-256-GCM encrypted bounded record, with a non-exportable Android Keystore key.\nThe native adapter uses `AtomicFile` and explicitly syncs file contents and directory metadata before\nacknowledging a save. Backup/device transfer is disabled. No household content, app password or login\npoll token is logged; the poll token remains in memory only.\n\nOn reconnect the app refreshes authority and shopping data before replaying the frozen request.\nThe server publishes effects and an actor-scoped operation receipt atomically. A successful request\nwhose response was lost is replayed using the same ID/payload/revision, including after restart.\nA changed revision stops the request for explicit review; it never silently checks newly added demand.\nDo not discard an unknown outcome: retry it safely first. Source edits, shops, ordering and product\nmanagement require an online connection; only manual additions and check/undo are queued offline.\n\nThe record is bounded to 1 MiB and 100 pending changes per household. Cache and intent lifetime is\nseven days; expired local shopping data is removed. Receipts are retained eight days, at most 1,024 per\nhousehold and 8 KiB per command. Capacity errors preserve existing data. The server rejects expired\nintent even after receipt pruning. A known revocation clears the affected household cache; a disconnected\ndevice cannot learn immediate remote revocation. Storage errors stop writes rather than pretending\nthat a volatile change is durable. The app refreshes in the foreground and on resume; no background\nexecution or live push is promised.\n\n## Verification and dependency review\n\n```sh\npython3 scripts/mobile.py flutter pub get --enforce-lockfile\npython3 scripts/verify_mobile_packages.py --audit\npython3 scripts/mobile.py flutter analyze\npython3 scripts/mobile.py test\npython3 scripts/mobile.py integration\npython3 scripts/mobile.py build\n```\n\nRun integration tests on the dedicated synthetic-data emulator, not on a personal installation: the\nFlutter integration runner installs/replaces its test app and can uninstall after a failed run.\n`integration_test` runs through the official `flutter drive` integration driver with `--no-dds`.\nFlutter 3.50's direct test runner failed to start DDS on this host, and its no-DDS golden-stream\nsubscription failed even though both native tests passed. The integration driver keeps every native\nassertion and exits successfully without that unused golden subscription. It exercises real Android\nkeystore/file persistence and native shopping widgets with\nsynthetic transport; real Nextcloud browser login/proxy/offline tests are separately recorded in\n`specs/0035-flutter-mobile-client/verification.md`. Mocked widgets alone are not transport proof.\n\n`clients/flutter/dependencies.json` records all resolved Dart package versions, licence classification\nand licence-file SHA-256. The verifier checks it against the immutable lock installation. `--audit`\nsends only public package/version pairs to the OSV Pub advisory API; it never reads device data.\nThis inventory covers Dart packages, not every native SDK/engine notice; Flutter includes its platform\nand package licence notices in the APK. SDK/JDK/emulator build tools are not shipped as app code.\nThe full repository Rust/catalogue/i18n/native-client gates still apply before push.\n\nDo not downgrade a receipt-bearing shopping file into an older server writer. See ADR 0027 for\nfail-closed downgrade/recovery behaviour and F35 for actual acceptance evidence and remaining work.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_guides_mobile_md {}

#[doc = "# Native Nextcloud client\n\nMealshop has an opt-in `AppAPI` transport and Vue 3 client for shared households. The existing\nloopback-only server/demo remains available. This guide describes the current test deployment;\nit does not claim a published app-store release or a verified production `HaRP` installation.\n\n## Verified target and client structure\n\nTarget installation: Nextcloud 35.0.2, `AppAPI` 35.0.0. Client: Vue 3, Composition API,\nstrict `TypeScript`, `@nextcloud/vue` 9.14.1, Vue Router 5.4 and Vite 7. The `AppAPI` loader\nreceives one IIFE (`js/mealshop.js`) and stylesheet (`css/mealshop.css`). It adds extensions to\nregistered paths, so registrations use `js/mealshop` and `css/mealshop`.\n\n`NcContent`/`NcAppNavigation`/`NcAppContent` use the actual Nextcloud header, theme, navigation\nand skip actions. Application CSS is scoped to `.mealshop`; no reset, web font or replacement\nshell is installed. The router base and API proxy use `@nextcloud/router`, preserving webroot\nand `index.php` behavior. No origin is embedded in product code.\n\n## Application workflows\n\nCreate a household or accept an invitation addressed to your exact Nextcloud user ID.\nOwners manage invitations and current roles; editors modify recipes, dietary profiles and\nplans; readers can inspect, export and review without applying changes. Invitations explicitly\nexplain access to allergy information. Dietary profiles are people eating, distinct from the\nNextcloud accounts granted access. Use **Refresh access** after another person accepts; this\nupdates access without overwriting unsaved dietary edits. Non-owners can leave from the\nhousehold list; ownership must first be transferred to another accepted member.\n\nRecipes support creation, immutable revisions, original-language content and exact serving\npreviews. Ingredient quantities accept g, kg, ml, l, tsp, tbsp and pieces. Non-mass inputs need\nan explicitly selected exact-record reference portion or declared density/piece/spoon evidence.\nThe original amount, unit and evidence persist; no nearby food record supplies missing data.\nRecipe title search retains its query when opening a result and returning. Empty ingredient results\ncan reset search, favourites and archive filters together. Profile notes preserve multiline input and\nkeep structured intolerance selections separate. Changed drafts are protected during household\nswitches, internal navigation and browser exit (subject to browser before-unload support). Explicit\nreload/discard actions confirm changed drafts; failed saves retain their inputs. Meal review receives\nkeyboard focus and a different proposal always requires a fresh confirmation.\nThe per-browser recipe display preference requests g/kg presentation from Rust. Kilograms may\nneed nine fractional digits to represent normalized micrograms exactly; this changes neither\nstored input precision nor ingredient quantities. Repeated edits use expected revisions.\nConflicts retain the draft and require an explicit reload/review rather than silent overwrite.\n\nProfiles separate structured hard allergen exclusions, free-text allergy/intolerance notes,\nexcluded ingredient IDs and soft dislikes. Export uses the current saved profile revision;\nremoval is confirmed and saved as a replacement snapshot. Unknown coverage stays unknown.\n\nPlanning uses pinned recipe revisions and titles, actual attendees, active/elapsed/advance\nbudgets, explicit calendar-snapshot consent and override, suggestions, review, shopping mass\ndeltas, apply and removal. Suggestions explain rejected constraints. Hard conflicts cannot be\nacknowledged away. Reference batch times and uncertainty are visible before apply. No live\ncalendar provider or purchase workflow is implied by the snapshot and quantity preview.\n\nThe catalogue searches English/German names and aliases, retains exact nutrient strings,\ndistinguishes unknown values from measured zero, and exposes source/version/licence/uncertainty.\nSource educational content stays original and marked as such when no reviewed translation exists.\nUI language is a browser preference; invalid quantity text is never silently reinterpreted when\nswitching languages. No token, recipe or health profile is stored in browser storage.\n\n## Trust boundaries\n\nThe transport validates `EX-APP-ID` and the base64 user/secret pair from `AUTHORIZATION-APP-API`\nwith constant-time secret comparison. Anonymous heartbeat is the only public infrastructure\nendpoint. Empty service users cannot access household APIs. Current membership is checked for\nevery request before forwarding into a dedicated existing recipe/profile/plan store. Browser\nBearer credentials and cookies never select the household authority; the internal operator token\nis supplied server-side only. All domain responses containing household data are `no-store`.\n\n`AppAPI` 35's PHP proxy is explicitly `NoCSRFRequired`. Mealshop therefore requires\n`X-Mealshop-Request: 1` on browser writes, in addition to authenticated identity and authorization,\nand requires JSON content type for membership bodies. Cross-origin simple forms cannot set the\ncustom header; no CORS allowance or OPTIONS route is added. Nextcloud axios retains native\nsession/request-token behavior. The app does not relax the host CSP or log request credentials,\nsource recipe bodies or dietary profiles.\n\nMembership uses a private, bounded, atomically replaced registry with one owner per household,\naddressed invitations, an exclusive writer lock and fail-closed corrupt/pending-file handling.\nThe current principals are Nextcloud user IDs: revoke household access before deleting/reusing\nan account ID. Automated account-deletion reconciliation and identity-backend migrations are\nnot implemented by this frontend slice. Do not treat this test deployment as production admission.\n\n## Local development deployment\n\nBuild with supported Node 24 (minimum 24.15), npm 12.2.2 and Rust:\n\n```sh\ncd clients/nextcloud\nnpm ci --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund\nnpm run build\ncd ../..\ncargo build --locked\n```\n\nProvide a private JSON environment file (mode `0600`) with string values for `APP_SECRET`\n(32–200 ASCII letters/digits/underscore/hyphen), `APP_HOST`, `APP_PORT`, `NEXTCLOUD_URL`,\n`APP_PERSISTENT_STORAGE` and absolute `MEALSHOP_ASSET_DIR`. Never commit it. Register a\nmanual-install daemon pointing at an address reachable by Nextcloud and start:\n\n```sh\npython3 scripts/run_nextcloud.py /absolute/path/to/private-runtime.json\n```\n\nRegister `mealshop` with that daemon, the same private secret/port, and the `USER` routes in\n`appinfo/info.xml`. The authenticated enable hook idempotently registers menu and assets.\nNo `/init` work is required: 404 means initialization is unnecessary. Startup restores the\nactual enabled state from Nextcloud; disabling denies subsequent domain requests. SIGINT/SIGTERM\nrelease storage locks cleanly. Do not remove a live writer's lock; follow the existing recipe\nand household recovery guides after an interrupted write. Restart only one writer per directory.\n\nOn the supplied test machine, the private runtime files are under ignored `var/nextcloud/`;\nthe active app data is `/data/mealshop-nextcloud/mealshop`, separate from Nextcloud's `html`.\nA pre-migration copy of the initial synthetic app data remains under ignored\n`var/nextcloud/data`. The Nextcloud reset script has not been used.\n\n## Container artifact and verified test deployment\n\n```sh\ndocker build -t mealshop.local/mealshop:0.1.0 .\npython3 scripts/test_exapp_container.py\n```\n\nThe image pins base digests, uses the Rust 1.99 compiler image and builds the Vue IIFE with\nNode 24/npm 12.2.2. The declared Rust 1.85 MSRV is checked separately. The image includes the\nmanifest, assets, CA store and FRP entrypoint. Runtime uses a supported Alpine\n3.24 release. The isolated smoke test exercises both TCP and Unix socket, lifecycle registration,\nassets, authorization, CSRF and private storage as UID 1000 with a read-only root filesystem.\nIts OCS mock is a transport fixture, not evidence of a real `HaRP` tunnel.\n\nThe copied Nextcloud reference entrypoint retains its copyright/licence. Local adaptations set\n`umask 077` and put its FRP configuration in `/tmp/frpc.toml` for rootless/read-only operation.\n`HP_SHARED_KEY` selects `/tmp/exapp.sock`; otherwise `APP_HOST`/`APP_PORT` select TCP.\nThe manifest uses a local-only image registry; nothing has been published to it.\n\n`deploy/nextcloud/compose.yaml` can attach a separately managed test container to the existing\nNextcloud network, with the same secret and data, no added capabilities and an internal bridge\nport binding. It requires private environment/settings files for the image, data owner UID/GID,\nport, data path and external network. It does not modify the original Nextcloud Compose file.\n\nThe customer explicitly approved adding the internal service hostname `nextcloud` to the test\ninstance's `trusted_domains` on 2026-11-09. It was added at index 3, preserving existing entries.\nThe manual process was gracefully stopped before starting `mealshop-exapp-test-mealshop-1`\nwith the same private secret, port and `/data/mealshop-nextcloud/mealshop` directory. The original\nNextcloud Compose configuration was not modified. The image runs as the existing data owner,\nwith a read-only root filesystem, no additional capabilities and `no-new-privileges`.\n\nManage this deployment from the repository root using the private configuration:\n\n```sh\ndocker compose --env-file var/nextcloud/compose.env -f deploy/nextcloud/compose.yaml ps\ndocker compose --env-file var/nextcloud/compose.env -f deploy/nextcloud/compose.yaml restart mealshop\n```\n\nThe manual startup helper remains available for development, but must not run concurrently\nagainst the same directory. Back up the Nextcloud registry/config and app data together before\nchanging identities or resetting the instance. See F22 validation for restart and browser evidence.\n\n## Repeatable checks\n\n```sh\ncd clients/nextcloud\nnpm run format:check\nnpm test\nnpm run build\nnpm audit --audit-level=moderate\nMEALSHOP_TEST_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080 \\\n  MEALSHOP_TEST_USER=your-test-owner MEALSHOP_TEST_PASSWORD=your-test-password \\\n  MEALSHOP_READER_USER=your-test-reader MEALSHOP_READER_PASSWORD=your-reader-password \\\n  npm run test:e2e\n```\n\nThe browser suite creates fictional household/recipe/profile data. Use an explicitly disposable\ninstance and two prepared accounts; do not point it at personal household data. It verifies the\nactual host, read/write boundaries, revocation, conflicts, exact quantities, hard constraints,\nsource portions, deep-link refresh, responsive layout, native light/dark, keyboard and axe checks.\nScreenshots/traces remain local in ignored test-result directories. Formal accessibility\ncertification, a live calendar connector, standalone shell and Flutter are separate work.\n\n## Daily workflow extension (F24)\n\nThe navigation contains a searchable household switcher. App entry restores the last accessible\nhousehold ID for the current Nextcloud account/browser; explicit household management remains\navailable. Both pretty embedded URLs and their `index.php` aliases retain deep links. Search-query\nupdates do not run page focus management. Membership is always rechecked on the server.\n\nRecipe authors are optional. Display quantities trim redundant fractional zeros without changing\nsaved input, exact scaling or nutrient precision. Ingredient rows use searchable native selects,\nwith household favourites first, inline declaration creation and trailing labelled trash actions.\nOptional JPEG/PNG/WebP images are uploaded through protected household routes and referenced by\nimmutable asset ID. Removing the current image preserves historical revisions. Images are limited\nto 5 MiB, 8192 pixels per dimension and 32 million pixels, with 100 MiB/1000 assets per household.\nActive image formats and remote image fetching are not supported. Back up the `images` directory\nwith recipes, profiles, plans, membership and the ingredient overlay. A leftover `.image.pending`\nrequires inspecting/recovering that write before restarting; never remove a live writer's lock.\n\nThe weekly calendar has breakfast, lunch, snack and dinner slots. Selecting a slot opens the\nexisting review/apply workflow with that date and meal. Each person can attend or skip independently;\nservings remain explicit. Empty slots represent no planned meal. Week navigation survives opening\nand cancelling an editor, and applying a future meal returns to its week. Existing custom slot\nidentifiers remain visible/editable rather than being silently discarded. Skipped-person details\ncan be expanded without stretching every calendar row with long names.\n\nThe invitation picker calls the installed Nextcloud file-sharing sharee search using the current\nbrowser session, matching file-sharing account visibility. It selects local people, not groups,\nfederated addresses or guessed accounts. Household acceptance and roles remain unchanged.\n\n### Household ingredient API\n\nUnder each authorized household domain prefix (or the protected local family API):\n\n- `GET /api/household/ingredients`: merged catalogue, nutrient definitions and overlay `revision`.\n- `POST /api/household/ingredients`: `{ expected_revision, ingredient: { name, language, form,\n  recorded_on, source_url?, annotations? } }`; returns stable `id` and new revision.\n- `PUT /api/household/ingredients/{id}`: `{ expected_revision, annotations }`, where annotations\n  contain `tags`, `components`, `declared_allergens`, `favorite`, `archived` and optional `organization`.\n- `DELETE /api/household/ingredients/{id}`: `{ expected_revision }`; archives without deleting history.\n- `GET /api/household/ingredients/{id}/recipes`: latest saved recipes that include this ingredient,\n  directly or through their pinned composition.\n- `POST /api/household/images`: bounded binary body with the actual raster MIME type; returns `id`.\n- `GET /api/household/images/{id}`: authorized raster bytes, fixed MIME type, `nosniff`, `no-store`.\n  Recipe create/update accepts optional `image_id`, referencing an existing household asset.\n\nAll shared mutations require writer permission and the existing browser-write marker. Reader\naccounts can inspect the merged catalogue and reverse lookup but cannot mutate annotations or\nassets. Favourites are explicitly shared household annotations. Reference source records remain\nimmutable; household overrides retain the original reference source and never rewrite catalogue files. Archived ingredients\nare hidden from new selection and cannot be newly introduced through the recipe API, while\nexisting recipes remain readable/editable with their pinned identity.\n\nDeclarations use `source.kind = household_declaration`; a missing source URL is explicit and no\nnutrient value is fabricated. German declarations preserve their entered name/form in German\ntranslation fields with a neutral English canonical identity. Composition records ingredient\nmembership, not inferred mass proportions. Cycles, unknown references and excessive depth fail.\nUser-declared allergens are distinct from primary evidence. Profiles additionally accept\n`excluded_tags`; exact case-insensitive matches explicitly block meals, including tags/allergens\non nested or pinned ingredients. Free-text notes and unmatched descriptive tags never establish\nsafety. Current and recipe-pinned positive constraints are both retained during review.\n\nThe overlay uses `ingredients.json` and `.ingredients.pending` under the existing private household\nlock, with expected revisions and atomic replacement. Conflicts retain the ingredient editor;\nits explicit reload action refreshes the version for review instead of silently overwriting it.\nPlan checks and durable application hold the same ingredient guard, preventing concurrent tags\nfrom being skipped between review and save. A separate dietary revision avoids invalidating\nplans when only a favourite/archive annotation changes; older overlays retain their global\nrevision as a conservative fallback.\n\nThe current F24 test image is `mealshop.local/mealshop:daily-workflows`, selected in the private\nCompose settings. It contains the client assets; no development asset mount is required.\nThe final packaged-browser/restart evidence and synthetic-fixture housekeeping are recorded in\n[F24 validation](../page_specs_0024_nextcloud_daily_workflows_validation_md/index.html).\n\n\n### Ingredient groups and declared nutrient profiles (F25)\n\nHouseholds can organise ingredients into groups and one level of subtypes. Wheat, rye and spelt\nflours are grouped by their existing exact record IDs; their original nutrient values remain intact.\nSearching includes the parent name, and recipe selectors qualify subtypes with their group.\n\n`organization` contains `is_group`, nullable `group_id`, `diet`, `origin` and `nutrition`.\nDiet is `unknown`, `vegan`, `vegetarian` or `meat_fish`; origin is `unknown`, `plant`, `animal`\nor `mixed`. Both are explicit household declarations, not allergen clearance or inferred claims.\nNutrition uses `mode: reference`, `mode: own` or `mode: inherit`. Own profiles contain `values`\nkeyed by the returned nutrient definitions, an evidence `note`, and an optional HTTPS `source_url`.\nAmounts are exact decimal strings per 100 g with the definition's unit; absent/null remains unknown\nand zero is explicit. Salt, total sugars and saturated fat can be entered independently; no conversion\nfrom sodium or inference from another nutrient is performed.\n\nInheritance is explicit, follows only the selected parent, and retains that parent's identity.\nEffective overrides use household-declaration provenance; `reference_source` retains the original\nsource and `nutrition_inherited_from` identifies inherited values. Saved recipe revisions include\nboth the effective values and group record, so editing a group changes future calculations only.\nExisting readers remain read-only and all writes keep revision checks and private atomic persistence.\nBack up the whole private data directory before upgrading; a downgrade must use the matching backup\nbecause older versions do not understand the added organization fields.\n\n\n### Comfortable authoring and optional constituents (F26)\n\nIngredient creation is offered directly in recipe search results and prefills the searched name. It\nreturns to the same quantity row; cancelling or closing a changed dialog requires an explicit discard.\nCompound spellings such as Haferflocken/Hafer Flocken match without substituting another food identity.\nNative selectors retain complete accessible names and the household switcher wraps its selected name.\n\nOwn profiles optionally contain `nutrition.constituents`, keyed by stable constituent ID. Each entry has\n`name`, optional `translations`, `unit` (`g`, `mg`, `µg`) and nullable exact `amount` per 100 g. They share\nthe profile's required evidence note and optional source URL. Inherited profiles include these declarations.\nThe effective ingredient snapshot exposes `constituents`. Saved recipe and serving-estimate responses expose a separate\n`constituents` array with total, per-serving value, known subtotals, status and missing ingredient IDs.\nUnknown is never converted into zero. Built-in beta-glucan/soluble fibre amounts remain readable through\nlegacy nutrient keys for existing API/demo callers, but native nutrient entry/results present them as\noptional constituents. No constituent amount or health effect is inferred from another nutrient.\n\n`organization.intolerance_triggers` accepts `sorbitol`. The separate trigger registry is returned alongside\n`groups` as `intolerance_triggers` from the existing allergen API. Selecting a trigger in a profile writes\nits canonical ID to `intolerances`. Explicit presence blocks affected attendees using current and pinned\ncomposition. The regulated fourteen-allergen data remains unchanged; unselected triggers do not assert\nabsence. Back up private data before upgrading and use matching backups for downgrades.\n\n\n### Full catalogue organization (F27)\n\nThe versioned `data/ingredient-groups.json` maps all 313 bundled IDs into 73 bilingual organizational\ngroups. `python3 scripts/verify_catalogue.py` also verifies this mapping's complete and unique coverage.\nExisting wheat/rye/spelt flour group IDs are retained. Source identities, names, quantities, nutrients and\nallergen mappings remain unchanged; organizational groups start with unknown nutrient values and no\nhealth declarations. Household overrides may move/ungroup records as before. Historical snapshots are\nnot rewritten. Custom replacement catalogues only receive groups for mapped IDs actually present.\n\nMalformed or colliding organizational metadata fails startup. Group metadata is validated separately\nfrom a custom catalogue's source payload, preserving the existing bounded snapshot-publication checks.\n\nOlder organization payloads preserve constituent and intolerance fields they do not know about.\nExplicit empty `constituents: {}` / `intolerance_triggers: []` clears these fields. Favourite changes\nwithout a semantic dietary change keep the existing review version. This protects already-open older\nclients from silently removing new declarations.\n\nGroup reverse lookup includes recipes using current group members as well as pinned historical\ncomposition/group references, so recipes created before grouping remain discoverable without rewriting\ntheir source snapshots.\n\n## Browser fixture ownership\nThe embedded suite records created household IDs privately in var/nextcloud/browser-fixtures.jsonl.\nDependent tests open only the matching recorded setup fixture, waiting for the household list to load.\nRun the complete suite from the repository root after configuring the documented test credentials.\nIndividual dependent specs require their authoring/Nextcloud setup spec first; missing fixtures fail\nclosed instead of opening the last household. Never point tests at customer-owned households.\nArchive only confirmed ledger-owned fixtures after stopping the sole writer. Validate their IDs,\nnames and synthetic contents, and preserve all non-fixture membership/library entries and counters.\nDo not inspect, hash or copy retained household directories for cleanup. Test reports and the fixture\nledger remain ignored. Avoid parallel manual fixture creation during a full browser run: the existing\n20-household account limit is intentionally enforced.\n\n## Cooking and baking\n\nThe household Cooking & baking view and recipe entry point implement F12/F13; see the\n[cooking guide](../page_docs_guides_cooking_md/index.html). Configure `MEALSHOP_PUBLIC_NEXTCLOUD_URL` when the server-side\nNextcloud URL is internal, so notification links use the browser-visible installation.\n\n## Assistant tools\n\nF17 adds explicit per-account MCP grants, connection setup and immutable creation activity in the\nexisting household shell. See the [MCP guide](../page_docs_guides_mcp_md/index.html) for stdio setup, app-password authority,\nconfirmation, source-only imported content and recovery.\n\n## Recipe link import\n\nF16 adds Import from link to the native recipe list and reuses the existing editor for review and\ninline ingredient creation. See the [import guide](../page_docs_guides_recipe_import_md/index.html) for public fetch consent,\nmanual fallback, retained unknowns, frozen retry and source-only downstream access.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_guides_nextcloud_md {}

#[doc = "# F02: ingredient catalogue and nutrition\n\nStatus: accepted through merged PR #4. This is a disposable test demo with\n313 ingredients, 18 nutrient definitions and two legacy constituent identifiers. This page describes\nthe core API and disposable demo; the native Nextcloud client also displays F34 comparison ranges.\n\n## Try the browser demo\n```sh\ncargo run --locked\n```\nOpen [the local workbench](http://127.0.0.1:3000/). The initial input is 100 g oats and 25 g\nchia for two servings. Choose **Calculate nutrition**: energy is 510.50 kcal for the recipe,\n255.25 kcal per serving; protein is 21.03 g for the recipe and 10.51 g per serving.\n\nChange chia to psyllium husks. Complete totals become unknown: the known energy subtotal\nremains 389.00 kcal for the recipe and 194.50 kcal per serving, explicitly labelled partial.\nF34 additionally returns a complete comparative range in a separate field; the native Nextcloud\nclient displays it. The disposable demo retains its legacy reported-only display.\nChange preparation to **Will be cooked / yield unknown** to see the retention/yield warning.\nNegative amounts produce a visible error and preserve the input. Search the notebook by\nname or alias (for example Hafer); expand composition to inspect source values per 100 g.\n\nThe browser uses same-origin API calls and no third-party assets/analytics. It stores no\nrecipe, health or household data, and sends none to external nutrition providers.\n\n## HTTP API\n- `GET /health`: unchanged process-health contract (`ok` plus a newline).\n- `GET /api/ingredients?q=hafer`: catalogue version, name/alias-matched ingredient records\n  and nutrient definitions (ID, label, unit, group). Search is case-insensitive.\n- `GET /api/ingredients/oats`: the complete record including aliases, edible form, basis,\n  source/version/licence, uncertainty, nutrient readings and education. Missing IDs return 404.\n- `POST /api/nutrition/calculate`: stateless source-based aggregation. Invalid amounts,\n  unsupported fields, unknown ingredient IDs or infeasible bounds return a structured JSON\n  error. No mutation endpoint is provided.\n\n```sh\ncurl --fail-with-body http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/nutrition/calculate \\\n  -H 'content-type: application/json' \\\n  --data '{\"lines\":[{\"ingredient_id\":\"oats\",\"grams\":\"100\"},{\"ingredient_id\":\"chia\",\"grams\":\"25\"}],\"servings\":2,\"preparation\":\"as_recorded\"}'\n```\n\nEach nutrient result names its unit and coverage (`complete`, `partial`, `unknown`). `total`\nand `per_serving` are decimal strings only when every contributing ingredient has a known\nvalue; otherwise they are null. `known_total`/`known_per_serving` are labelled subtotals,\nnot complete values. For an entirely unknown nutrient, those known subtotals are zero\nbecause no known contribution exists; status/null totals preserve that distinction.\nMissing ingredient IDs are deduplicated. Repeated recipe lines still contribute separately.\nThe result retains original gram strings, source records, form/basis, catalogue version,\nsource uncertainty, educational conditions/limitations and preparation context.\n\n## Quantity and calculation contract\nAll input masses are grams of the record's edible form; the supported basis is\n`per_100g_edible_portion`. No volume, piece, yield or raw/cooked conversion is inferred.\nInput source values and masses are decimal strings in [0, 1000000000], with at most six\nfractional digits and no sign/exponent/whitespace; recipe masses must be strictly positive.\nServings are integer counts in [1, 1000000]; requests contain 1–100 lines. These are\nbounded-arithmetic/input-validation limits for this implementation, not subscription limits.\n\nAmounts retain their original spelling and an integer scaled by 1000000. For each nutrient,\nthe numerator sums `source_atoms * gram_atoms` and the denominator is `100 * 1000000²`.\nThe per-serving denominator additionally multiplies by serving count. Checked integer\narithmetic preserves the entire fraction until final formatting, with two decimal places\nand half-away-from-zero rounding (all valid values are nonnegative). At the bounds, the\nscaled sum is at most 10³² and the rounding numerator at most 10³⁴, within a 128-bit unsigned\ninteger. Portion values are never calculated from rounded recipe totals. This is arithmetic\nprecision, not a claim that the source food data are accurate to two decimals.\n\nThe registry distinguishes kcal/kJ, g, mg and µg. USDA by-difference carbohydrate (including fibre)\nand BLS available carbohydrate (excluding fibre, including polyols) use separate IDs. Total folate\nand food folate also remain separate; neither is dietary folate equivalents. Energy is retained from the source,\nnot recomputed from macros. Micronutrient coverage is a selected starter subset, not exhaustive.\n\n## Use an operator-provided catalogue\n```sh\nMEALSHOP_CATALOGUE_PATH=/absolute/path/catalogue.json cargo run --locked\n```\nThis file replaces the entire bundled catalogue at startup. The schema matches\n[data/catalogue.json](https://github.com/Th3Link/mealshop/blob/main/data/catalogue.json). Schema 1 requires a nonempty version;\ningredient IDs are unique ASCII identifiers. Records need aliases (possibly empty), form,\nbasis, full source fields, uncertainty, a nutrient map and education (possibly empty).\nNutrient readings contain `amount` (decimal string or null) and `uncertainty`. Omitted nutrient\nkeys are also unknown. Only registry nutrient IDs are accepted; units are fixed by that registry.\nSources and evidence links use HTTPS. Education requires component, title, summary, evidence\nreference, conditions and limitations, independent of measured nutrient amounts.\n\nThe immutable catalogue is public operator-provided reference data. It is not a place for\nprivate household/health data. Catalogue writes/authentication, persistent ingredient editing,\nuser unit preferences, cooking retention models and the Flutter shell remain separate\nfeatures (F03/F09/F18). The [local recipe authoring slice](../page_docs_guides_recipes_md/index.html) now saves recipes\nand pins catalogue subsets independently of this public reference dataset.\n\n## Limits and provenance\nJSON recipe bodies are limited to 128 KiB; catalogue input to 2 MiB and 1000 records.\nThe server rejects malformed/oversized/unsupported catalogues and exits on missing files.\nAn empty catalogue is valid and the browser explains why calculation is unavailable.\nSource dates, sample counts and educational references are snapshots, not live provider sync.\n\nSee [starter data provenance](https://github.com/Th3Link/mealshop/blob/main/data/README.md) and\n[F02 acceptance criteria](../page_specs_0002_ingredients_nutrition_specification_md/index.html).\n\n## Demo versus product UI\nThe customer classified this surface as a pragmatic demo. Keep design effort minimal.\nNextcloud/standalone product shells are F22/F30 (Vue 3); Flutter is separate in F18.\nF21 starts with German/English demo messages, starter names and exact decimal presentation.\nSource uncertainty and educational text remain explicitly original-language until reviewed\ntranslations are available. UI language does not change nutrient IDs, units, source facts or\ncanonical API decimals. German entry uses a comma; English uses a dot, without grouping.\n\n## English base and localized demo\nCanonical IDs/names remain English; German ingredient names/aliases/forms use `translations.de`.\nThe language selector changes keyed interface messages and display, not nutrient values or stored\nAPI decimals. Enter e.g. `100,5` grams in German or `100.5` in English, without grouping separators.\nValid inputs/results survive language changes exactly. Previously invalid inputs require an\nexplicit edit before they can be reinterpreted in a new locale. Unsupported languages fall back to English.\nSearch covers both languages and common aliases/plurals (e.g. Weizen 405, Hafermilch, Eier).\n\nThe 313-item kitchen catalogue is generated offline from reviewed BLS/USDA selections; see\n[data provenance and rebuild instructions](https://github.com/Th3Link/mealshop/blob/main/data/README.md). Available carbohydrate and\nby-difference carbohydrate are separate definitions; food folate is separate from total folate.\nMixed-source recipes may show partial values for these definitions, with an explanatory warning.\nError responses add stable `code` alongside the English `error` text; calculation results add\n`warning_codes` parallel to the retained English `warnings`. The demo localizes these codes.\n\n## Typed quantity conversion (F03 initial slice)\n`POST /api/quantities/convert` accepts `{\"quantity\":{\"amount\":\"1.250000\",\"unit\":\"kg\"},\"target\":\"g\"}` and returns the original input, its dimension and an exact converted quantity. Supported unit identifiers are `g`, `kg`, `ml`, `l`, `tsp`, `tbsp` and `piece`. Cross-dimension and spoon conversions require ingredient data or a named standard and return stable error codes. Conversion rejects values outside the six-decimal precision bound rather than rounding. This endpoint is not yet connected to recipes or user preferences.\n\n## F34: explicit ranges and comparison evidence\n\nIngredient records and calculated ingredient contributions may contain `nutrition_estimates`. Each\nprofile retains its version, method, matching rationale, observed bounds and self-contained primary\nreferences. `source_supplement` adds facts from the identical original source; `comparison_envelope`\nand `group_envelope` are estimates. Original readings take precedence. Reference facts are per 100 g\nin their named state. No implicit carbohydrate, density, raw/cooked or constituent conversion applies.\nSee [public coverage](../page_specs_0034_evidence_nutrition_ranges_coverage_md/index.html) and ADR 0019.\n\nA nutrient row with any estimated input has an additional tagged `range`. For the synthetic\n100 g oats + 100 g psyllium fixture with two servings, its energy row includes:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"energy_kcal\",\n  \"total\": null,\n  \"per_serving\": null,\n  \"range\": {\n    \"status\": \"complete\",\n    \"total\": { \"lower\": \"580.00\", \"upper\": \"597.00\" },\n    \"per_serving\": { \"lower\": \"290.00\", \"upper\": \"298.50\" },\n    \"estimated_ingredients\": [\"psyllium-husks\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis is an excerpt; the existing reported-only status/subtotal/missing fields remain present.\nWhen an input has no bound, `range.status` is `partial`, with `known_total`, `known_per_serving`,\n`estimated_ingredients` and `missing_ingredients` instead of complete total fields. Clients must\nlabel this covered subtotal, never a complete result. Without estimated inputs, `range` is absent\nand the existing reported-only fields apply. Unsupported core inputs receive a separate incomplete\nnutrition warning in the native client. Individual unknown micronutrients remain visible as well.\n\nHousehold `organization.nutrition.mode = \"own\"` accepts a reported value **or** a range in its\nexisting `values` map. For example, a synthetic per-100-g reading is:\n\n```json\n{\"amount\":null,\"range\":{\"lower\":\"10.000001\",\"upper\":\"12.500001\"},\"uncertainty\":\"Explicit household comparison evidence\"}\n```\n\nBoth limits are nonnegative exact decimal strings with at most six places; lower must not exceed\nupper. A nonnull amount and range cannot coexist. Evidence note remains required, with an optional\nHTTPS source. Explicit subtype inheritance carries group ranges and source identity. Constituents\nrequire separate explicit evidence; these ranges do not assert allergy safety or health effects.\n\nChecked integer sums retain original serving ratios, with lower bounds rounded down and upper\nbounds up only at the final two-place display. Observed comparison spans are **not guaranteed bounds\nor statistical confidence intervals**. New recipe revisions pin effective evidence. Legacy history\nfiles stay unchanged and receive immutable-v1 supplemental analysis in memory. The 2 MiB per-recipe\npublication/restart guard remains independent of the 2 MiB catalogue envelope.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_guides_nutrition_md {}

#[doc = "# Reviewed private recipe import\n\nF16 adds **Import from link** beside Create recipe in a Nextcloud household. Only owners and editors\ncan fetch or save an import. Readers retain ordinary recipe access but cannot initiate these operations.\nA model/provider, subscription, browser extension or source-site account is not required.\n\n## Fetch, review, save\n\n1. Enter the original HTTPS recipe URL. Explicitly allow this server to contact the source and\n   validated redirects: the website receives the URL and the server's IP. Nextcloud credentials,\n   cookies and referrers are never forwarded. No source scripts, images or JSON-LD contexts load.\n2. Fetch the preview and choose a recipe if the page supplies several. Supported public Schema.org\n   Recipe JSON-LD can appear in HTML, a JSON document, arrays or graphs. Nested instruction sections\n   keep their headings. This is structured extraction, not guaranteed support for every website.\n3. Continue into the normal compact editor. Original ingredient text sits beside its mapping. Select\n   actual catalogue/household ingredient IDs; missing ingredients can be created inline. Suggestions\n   accept only unambiguous metric prefixes with an appropriate decimal convention. Fractions, ranges,\n   unrecognized units and ambiguous separators require explicit input; no nearby food is substituted.\n4. Enter missing canonical values, including positive servings and exact quantities. Volume/count\n   conversion still needs explicit matching evidence. Total source preparation/cooking durations are\n   retained as supplied and never distributed over steps. All entered step times, including zero,\n   are your explicit declarations under the review confirmation; absent original values stay unknown.\n   Check the reviewed recipe's language; no text is silently translated.\n5. Review ingredient mappings, amounts, servings, language, steps and time estimates, then confirm\n   and save. Subsequent changes clear confirmation. The original URL is fixed. Original source fields\n   remain separate from your reviewed values, including after later edits and historical reads.\n\nImported nutrition, allergy and diet claims never populate the ingredient catalogue or establish\nsafety. Nutrition uses the reviewed IDs/quantities and existing source snapshots/ranges. External images\nare not fetched or hotlinked; the normal explicit image-upload control remains available.\n\n## Unsupported or blocked source\n\nLogin/paywall/robots restrictions, unsafe targets, encoding/type/size limits, timeouts and missing\nstructured recipes have explicit errors. Use **Enter privately without fetching** to open the same\neditor with the original URL and unknown original fields. This path makes **no remote request**.\nIt does not bypass source access controls or imply publication rights. Fill your private copy normally;\nsharing, collections and MCP still expose only its source link. Chefkoch, Instagram and Einfach Backen\nare examples of customer URLs, not a promise that every page permits automated retrieval.\n\n## Drafts, ambiguous responses and retries\n\nPreviews are actor/household-bound, memory-only and valid for 30 monotonic minutes. At most 16 previews\nper household and four per account are retained; a cleanup task removes expired data within another\n30 seconds. No full HTML document reaches persistence or browser storage. Required fields cannot save\nas unknown; the source snapshot still records which original fields were absent.\n\nOnce submitted, an ambiguous request is frozen. **Retry confirmed import** sends the same body,\npreview/candidate and operation key, even if the first response was lost. Publication atomically places\nits authenticated actor/time, immutable source fields and idempotency receipt in revision one. Parallel\nor restarted identical retries return that same first revision; changed payload/key reuse conflicts.\nA definite validation failure unlocks the retained draft for corrections and a new confirmation.\n\nLeaving, replacing or reloading unsaved work requires explicit discard. An expired or restarted\nuncommitted preview must be fetched/reviewed again. If you leave an ambiguous submission, check the\nrecipe list before beginning a new import; no key/source content is persisted in browser storage.\nRevoked membership or a reader role blocks subsequent saves, including retries. Requests already\nadmitted can finish; the normal Nextcloud account/household boundaries remain authoritative.\n\n## Public fetch boundary\n\n- Public-domain HTTPS on port 443 only, without embedded credentials. IP literals, local suffixes,\n  private/local/reserved, mixed DNS sets, IPv4-mapped/transitional IPv6 and special-use ranges are\n  rejected. A conservative IANA-based policy allows ordinary IPv4 and IPv6 global-unicast addresses.\n- Resolve at most 32 addresses, reject the whole set if any is unsafe, pin accepted addresses and\n  verify the connected peer. Recheck every target before each hop. Environment proxies are disabled.\n- At most three recipe redirects; unsafe targets are rejected. Robots redirects/policy/auth/rate-limit\n  failures block the fetch. Missing/unavailable robots resources use the documented RFC-9309 subset;\n  applicable allow/disallow rules and explicit `isAccessibleForFree: false` metadata are respected.\n- A 15-second total network deadline, 3-second connect and 8-second individual request bounds. Page\n  bodies are limited to 2 MiB, robots to 512 KiB and extracted preview/source fields to 96 KiB.\n  UTF-8 HTML/JSON-LD is accepted, including plain-text carriers; compressed/unsupported encodings fail.\n- Four concurrent fetches per process and four bounded parser workers. One active fetch and a\n  ten-second cooldown per household/account pair; there is no background crawling or automatic retry.\n  Parser permits remain held by worker tasks even if an HTTP caller disconnects or times out.\n\nHTML5 tokenization and the established robots matcher add no script/browser execution. The unchanged\nlicence/advisory policy and actual Rust-1.85 compatibility check are verified; no advisory exception was introduced.\n\n## API and recovery\n\nAll paths below are relative to the existing authenticated household domain; normal JSON, request\nmarker, enabled-state and current membership checks apply. No new hostnames or auth flow are required.\n\n| Request | Body / response |\n| --- | --- |\n| `POST /api/household/imports/preview` | `{ mode: \"url\", url, consent: true }` or `{ mode: \"manual\", url }`; returns bounded candidates, requested/final URL, method, retrieval time, preview ID and expiry. |\n| `POST /api/household/imports` | `{ preview_id, candidate, idempotency_key, reviewed: true, recipe }`; zero-based candidate, opaque ASCII key of 16–128 characters, canonical reviewed recipe. Returns the normal saved recipe projection. |\n| `GET /api/recipes/{id}` | Owning-household response includes `import_source` separately from `recipe`; receipt keys/preview IDs are omitted. Historical reads retain that first original snapshot. |\n\nThe private first recipe record gains optional `import_receipt`; old records are unchanged/readable.\nIt is valid only in revision one, must match the imported original URL and uses the existing private,\nsynced append-only publication. Later record files do not duplicate it. Damaged, duplicate or misplaced\nreceipts fail closed on startup. Preserve the complete first revision in backup/restore. Do not remove\nits receipt to retry or downgrade to a server that rejects this format; use an explicit migration that\npreserves provenance/idempotency. Follow [recipe storage recovery](../page_docs_guides_recipes_md/index.html) for pending writes.\n\n[Acceptance evidence](../page_specs_0016_recipe_import_validation_md/index.html) records real Nextcloud/proxy/browser,\nsource, container, restart and synthetic-only cleanup checks. Deterministic transport tests inject DNS\nand HTTP replies into a private boundary; production has no test-network bypass. The public synthetic\nfixture is versioned in `tests/fixtures/import-recipe.html`. Browser tests use an immutable fixture URL\nbefore merge (`MEALSHOP_IMPORT_FIXTURE_URL`) and main afterward; they never inspect retained households.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_guides_recipe_import_md {}

#[doc = "# Local recipe authoring\n\nThe executable stores locally authored recipes in `var/recipes` (ignored by Git). Override\nwith `MEALSHOP_RECIPE_DIR=/absolute/dedicated/path`; an empty value is an error. The directory\nis created on first run. Only loopback listen addresses are currently supported. There is no\nauthentication: this is a trusted local, single-household authoring slice, not hosted access.\nStop the server before operating on the storage files. Use a local filesystem supporting\nhard links/fsync; Linux is the initial validated target. Existing directory permissions are\nnot modified; newly created Unix directories/files use 0700/0600.\n\n## Demo\nUse the existing demo at `http://127.0.0.1:3000/`. Enter a title, author attribution, recipe\ntext language (English/German), catalogue ingredients and gram amounts, serving count and\noptional steps with active/passive seconds. German amounts use commas; API amounts use exact\ndot-decimal strings. Save creates a new recipe; saving a loaded recipe creates a revision.\nThe saved list and load button retrieve persisted recipes and their saved serving estimates.\nLoad/new actions explicitly replace inputs. Failed saves keep all inputs. On a revision\nconflict, copy your edits before loading the latest revision and reconciling them manually.\n\nChanging the authoring serving count divides the same batch. The separate scaling preview uses the saved original revision and rounds each ingredient half up to 0.000001 g. Nutrition uses the exact original serving ratio before display rounding. A\nsaved recipe uses its own source snapshot, even after the catalogue changes. Saving a new\nrevision uses the current catalogue and recalculates nutrition. Missing current ingredient\nIDs cannot be silently substituted: restore the catalogue or explicitly replace them.\nUnknown nutrient values remain unknown. Authors are self-reported, not authenticated.\nThis remains a pragmatic demo; product clients and process timers are separate features.\n\n## API\n```sh\ncurl --fail-with-body http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/recipes \\\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{\n    \"title\":\"Porridge\", \"language\":\"en\", \"author\":\"Local cook\",\n    \"servings\":2, \"preparation\":\"cooking_unmodelled\",\n    \"lines\":[{\"ingredient_id\":\"oats\",\"grams\":\"100.500000\"},\n             {\"ingredient_id\":\"chia\",\"grams\":\"25\"}],\n    \"steps\":[{\"instruction\":\"Mix and rest.\",\"active_seconds\":60,\"passive_seconds\":300}]\n  }'\n```\nPOST `/api/recipes` returns HTTP 201 with `id`, `revision`, `recipe` and `nutrition`.\nGET `/api/recipes` lists latest summaries. GET `/api/recipes/recipe-00000001` retrieves the\nlatest recipe and estimate; append `?revision=1` for a historical revision. PUT that same\npath with `{\"expected_revision\":1,\"recipe\":{...complete recipe fields...}}` creates revision 2.\nConcurrent stale writes return HTTP 409 / `revision_conflict`. No PATCH or DELETE is offered.\nOther stable codes include `invalid_recipe`, `recipe_not_found`, `invalid_revision`,\n`recipe_storage_full` and `recipe_storage_failed`. Recipe responses are marked `no-store`.\nRecipe IDs and revisions are local to this store, not globally federated identifiers.\n\nBounds: 128 KiB body; 1–100 ingredient lines; positive amounts up to 1000000000 g with six\nfractional digits; 1–1000000 integer servings; title/author 1–200 UTF-8 bytes; language en/de;\n0–100 steps with 1–4000 byte instructions and 0–604800 seconds per active/passive duration.\nThe local store holds at most 1000 immutable revisions, each at most 2 MiB. It does not delete\nold revisions to make room. Capacity expansion/migration belongs to F18.\n\n## Backup and recovery\nStop all processes using the directory; copy the entire directory (including every revision)\nto a separate backup location. Never edit files while a server is running: reads use an\nin-memory index and external writers are unsupported. Restore into an empty dedicated directory,\nthen start the server and verify list/detail/portion results. Full adapter migration is not\nimplemented. Do not delete individual revisions: each recipe history must start at 1 and be\nconsecutive. Preserve the original backup when repairing any damaged copy.\n\nNormal graceful shutdown removes `.mealshop.lock`. After a crash it remains. Check that no\nprocess is using the directory before removing a stale lock; PID text is advisory, not proof\nthat no writer exists. Starting with a live/stale lock fails closed.\n\nA `.pending` file means publication may have been interrupted. Stop writers, back up the\nentire directory and inspect `.pending` plus its recipe ID/revision target. If the target\nexists and is byte-identical, it was published; retain the target and remove `.pending` from\nthe recovery copy. If no target exists, preserve `.pending` outside the live directory before\nremoving it there (the save was not published). If they differ, restore a known-good complete\nbackup or reconcile offline; never replace the target blindly. Remove only a verified stale\nlock and restart against the recovery copy. Startup validates schema, source snapshots,\nquantities, filenames and consecutive revision histories; corruption never becomes an empty\nstore. A storage error returns 503 and disables later writes until restart/recovery. After a\nnetwork timeout the save may have committed: refresh the list/history before retrying, since\nPOST retry idempotency is not implemented. Unacknowledged valid published revisions can be\nvisible after recovery, so inspect the latest revision before resubmitting edits.\n\n## Embedding\n`router()` / `router_from_catalogue_json()` remain stateless catalogue/nutrition applications.\n`router_with_recipe_directory(json, directory)` explicitly enables durable recipes and holds\nits exclusive lock until every cloned router/request state is dropped. The caller must enforce\nthe trusted local access boundary; the library does not provide authentication or listener policy.\n\nGET `/api/recipes/{id}/scale?servings=3&revision=1` previews a saved revision at another\nserving count without writing it. Omit revision for the latest. The response includes both\nserving counts, scaled gram strings and nutrition; unsupported or zero rounded amounts fail.\n\n## Original quantity and conversion evidence\nA recipe line can supply `quantity: {\"amount\":\"0.125\",\"unit\":\"kg\"}` instead of grams.\nThe server normalizes to grams and stores both. If both are supplied they must agree exactly.\nLegacy gram-only lines remain valid. For ml/l/piece/tsp/tbsp input supply `conversion` with\n`ingredient_id`, exact catalogue `form`, `source_url` (HTTPS), `version`, `uncertainty`,\n`grams_per_base_unit` (g/ml or g/piece), and optional `spoon_standard` and\n`millilitres_per_spoon`. Spoon input requires both optional fields; name the selected spoon\nand regional standard. These are user-declared evidence, not independently verified sources.\nCross-dimension arithmetic rejects unsupported precision; it never assumes water density.\nThe immutable revision retains this context. Scaling previews normalize from saved grams,\nleaving original inputs/evidence in the source revision. Local demo g/kg preference affects\nmass display only; other users' saved recipes are not rewritten.\n\n## Exact recipe display units\n\nRecipe detail and serving-preview GET requests accept optional `display_unit=g|kg`. The additive\n`display_lines` response contains server-formatted mass amounts and units. Kilograms retain up\nto nine fractional digits to represent normalized micrograms without rounding. This presentation\ndoes not change the stored source quantity, six-place input bound or nutrient calculation.\n\n\n## Recipe parts and step associations (F26)\n\nRecipes optionally contain `parts: [{ id, name, uses_parts? }]`. Ingredient lines may have stable `id`\nand nullable `part_id`; steps may have `part_id`, `ingredient_line_ids` and `prepared_parts`.\nReferences use IDs, never array positions or translated names. A maximum of 30 named parts is accepted.\nUnknown/duplicate references and part dependency cycles fail before saving. A step's prepared-part\nreferences also participate in cycle checks. The native editor moves removed parts' lines/steps to the\nmain recipe and clears references after confirmation.\n\nParts organize one flat ingredient list. Nutrition, serving previews and shopping requirements count\neach measured base input once; finished homemade output references are excluded. Dietary checks retain\nall identities conservatively. They do not infer the yield of an empty part or create\na parallel cooking schedule. Saved records pin all associations and source values; older flat records\nremain supported. Current APIs do not link independently saved recipes as nested references.\n\n## Structured preparation instructions (F31)\n\nRecipe ingredient lines optionally carry `extensions`. The initial variant is:\n```json\n{\"type\":\"temperature\",\"value\":\"60.00\",\"unit\":\"celsius\",\"original_input\":\"60,00 °C\",\"provenance\":\"recipe_author\"}\n```\nThe exact signed decimal is bounded to -273.15 through 1000 Celsius with at most six decimal places.\nThe original author input is retained, independently of UI locale. At most one temperature target\nis accepted per line; unsupported types/units/provenance, duplicate extensions and numeric JSON\nvalues are rejected. Missing extensions remain absent. This is a preparation instruction, not a\nmeasurement, conversion or guarantee. Scaling a recipe changes ingredient masses but never this\ntemperature. Revisions, recipe reads, scaling and cooking ingredient responses preserve it.\n\nParts additionally accept `starts_after` references distinct from consumed `uses_parts` references.\nBoth graphs are checked together for cycles. A step can retain `passive_max_seconds` alongside its\nminimum/default `passive_seconds`; cooking templates preserve the range. Legacy records need no\nnew fields. Back up the entire data directory before upgrading; old binaries cannot read these\nnew fields after new revisions have been saved.\n\nThe existing JSON is a possible future intelligent-scale input, not a device integration API.\n\n## Compact authoring and homemade inputs (F33)\n\nThe native Nextcloud editor keeps parts, instructions and metadata open. Ingredients and instructions\nsit beside one another on wide screens and stack on small screens. Ingredient headers appear once;\nrow controls retain accessible names. The row's **+** menu adds an optional temperature, moves a line\nor selects **Prepare at home**. Ingredient creation remains inside the searchable selector.\n\nPreparing a line creates a named part with a blank base ingredient and instruction to complete.\nThe output line gains `prepared_part: \"part-id\"`; the referenced part must have instructions and\nmeasured inputs, directly or through other consumed parts. Declare the finished mass explicitly.\nNo mass loss, recipe, yield or conversion is inferred from a name such as toasted bread.\nOne output per producing part is supported, with combined cycle validation. Independent saved\nrecipes are not nested in this model. Legacy lines without `prepared_part` remain purchases.\n\nThe output identity, original quantity, extensions and pinned source remain saved, displayed and\nscaled. Only measured base lines enter nutrient/constituent calculations, shopping and meal-plan\ndeltas, once each. Homemade calculations report `cooking_unmodelled`; values do not claim a cooked\nyield. Dietary review checks both finished identities and inputs, preserving hard exclusions and\nunknowns. Cooking waits for the producing part. Stateless nutrition rejects unresolved preparations.\nDetail/scale `display_lines` additionally expose each frozen ingredient's name and translations.\n\n**Use purchased ingredient** confirms removal of a simple preparation's inputs and steps; cancelling\npreserves everything. Removing its output asks the same question. Shared/nested preparations must\nhave dependencies resolved first. Removing an ordinary part still moves its contents to the main\nrecipe. Save uses the existing immutable revision/conflict checks. Back up complete data before\ninstallation; rollback restores the matching prior image and data together (ADR 0018).\n"]
pub mod page_docs_guides_recipes_md {}

#[doc = "# Recipe sharing and collections\n\n## Use collections\nOpen Collections in a household, create a named collection, select recipes and choose their exact\nrevision. Arrange entries with the move buttons and save. Opening an entry reads that saved revision;\nOpen current revision is an explicit way to return to the current recipe. Later recipe edits never\nadvance a collection or a meal-plan reference automatically. Owners and editors curate private\ncollections; readers browse. When a collection has any outgoing grant or pending invitation, only\nits owner may change it. Deleting a collection revokes all its grants together.\n\n## Grant read access\nThe household owner opens Share on a recipe or collection. Choose a person with the native Nextcloud\npicker, invite a household through its owner, or select another household you own. Household invitations\nremain content-free until the contact accepts as destination owner. Acceptance explains that all\ncurrent and future members of that household gain read access while they remain members, and its\nname becomes visible to the sender. No directory of unrelated household names is published.\n\nShared recipe grants pin the displayed revision. After editing a recipe, Share this revision explicitly\nupdates a recipient's published version. Collection grants follow the owner's explicit collection edits,\nwhile each recipe entry remains pinned. The dialog lists read grants with revision/pending state,\ncopy-link and confirmed revoke actions. A copied link still requires its addressed recipient's normal\nNextcloud login; it is not a public bearer link.\n\n## Read incoming content\nShared with me is available outside the source household. Recipients can read and scale owned recipes\nand their authorized images. They cannot edit, grant access onward, or browse the source household's\nprofiles, catalogue, plans or shopping list. Personal recipients may remove their access. Only a\ndestination owner may remove a household-wide grant. A pending contact can decline the invitation.\n\nRecipe from another source records an original HTTPS URL. Once saved, that provenance is retained.\nShared imported items contain only this URL, including in collection entries and previews: no cached\ntitle, author, ingredients, instructions, image or nutrition is republished. Correcting an existing\nrecipe to imported also restricts already-shared older revisions. This feature does not fetch or\nparse external recipes; automatic import is F16.\n\nEach server read rechecks current grant/membership, including image and scaling requests. Revocation\nremoves subsequent server access. The client clears protected content after an access failure and\nrefreshes on visibility changes or Refresh access. Content is not stored in browser storage, and all\nfeature responses use `Cache-Control: no-store`. Revocation cannot remove a copy already downloaded.\n\n## API contract\nAll routes below are authenticated `AppAPI` user routes under the existing `ExApp` proxy. The browser\nuses generated Nextcloud URLs; no hostnames or bearer credentials are embedded in client code.\n\n| Route | Methods | Contract |\n|---|---|---|\n| `/api/households/{home}/collections` | GET, POST | Household list/search; create with `expected_revision` and `collection: {name, description?, entries}` |\n| `/api/households/{home}/collections/{id}` | GET, PUT, DELETE | Pinned content; update/delete with `expected_revision` |\n| `/api/households/{home}/shares` | GET, POST | Owner-only grants; create with `expected_revision`, resource, recipient |\n| `/api/households/{home}/shares/{id}` | PUT, DELETE | Owner-only explicit recipe publication or revocation with `expected_revision` |\n| `/api/shared` | GET | Current recipient's metadata and projection-safe summaries; pending content is null |\n| `/api/shared/{home}/{share}` | GET, DELETE | Authorized detail or recipient removal with `expected_version` |\n| `/api/shared/{home}/{share}/accept` | PUT | Accept addressed invitation with `expected_version`, `household_id` owned by actor |\n| `/api/shared/{home}/{share}/recipes/{recipe}` | GET | Current collection entry only, at its pinned revision |\n| `/api/shared/{home}/{share}/image` | GET | Granted owned recipe image only |\n| `/api/shared/{home}/{share}/recipes/{recipe}/image` | GET | Granted collection's owned entry image only |\n\nA recipe reference is `{recipe_id, revision}`. Resource variants are `recipe` plus its reference or\n`collection` plus `collection_id`. Recipient variants are `account`/`household_invitation` plus `user`,\nor `household` plus `home_id`. Stale revisions/versions return 409 without overwriting data.\nMissing/unauthorized incoming content returns 404. Private writes respect household roles; outbound\nsharing requires owner. A selected account is independently verified against `AppAPI`'s users utility;\nunknown IDs and failed lookups do not create grants. Imported detail is exactly\n`{kind: \"source_link\", source_url: \"https://…\"}` inside its content envelope.\n\nLists use one-based `page` and `per_page` (maximum 50, default 20; outbound grants default 50).\nCollection search uses `q` up to 200 bytes. Outgoing grants can be filtered by `kind` and `resource_id`.\nShared recipe detail accepts `servings` and `display_unit=g|kg`; calculations reuse exact recipe scaling.\nOptional `revision` must equal the granted/entry revision. No mass/volume inference is introduced.\n\n## Storage and recovery\n`library.json` lives beside `membership.json` under the existing exclusive storage-root lock. Schema\nversion 1 permits up to 100 collections and 1,000 grants per household, 200 unique recipe entries per\ncollection, 1,000 library households, and a 2 MiB serialized document. Names are at most 200 bytes and\ndescriptions 2,000 bytes. Monotonic collection/share identities are never reused. Recipe content remains\nin its existing immutable store; the library contains references and access metadata only.\n\nAtomic private `.library.pending` writes sync file and parent directory before replacing the document.\nAmbiguous write failures stop further writes; a pending marker fails startup. Stop the single writer,\nretain the failed files privately, restore a verified complete `library.json` (or resolve the pending\ntransaction against it), then restart and verify access. Never delete a pending marker without checking\nthe committed document. Do not edit metadata while the service is running. Existing recovery guidance\nfor [recipes](../page_docs_guides_recipes_md/index.html) and [Nextcloud](../page_docs_guides_nextcloud_md/index.html) still applies.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_guides_sharing_md {}

#[doc = "# Household shopping\n\nOpen **Shopping list** inside a household. Readers inspect; owners/editors change it.\n\n## Finding and checking goods\nThe list opens in shopping mode. Open goods appear as compact, named tiles with exact quantities;\ntap a tile or focus its checkbox and press Space to check it off. Purchased goods remain under the\n**Purchased** disclosure and can be unchecked. Text and checkboxes accompany color. Category icons\ncome only from explicitly linked ingredient metadata; unlinked goods use a neutral basket.\n\n**Find an item** filters canonical and translated names without changing saved data or order. Long\nnames wrap to two lines; their complete names remain accessible and visible in item details. Press\nEnter in search or choose **Add item** to enter an explicit quantity/unit inline. A name never selects\nan ingredient automatically. Saving returns focus to search; failed writes retain the draft. The last\nconfirmed check/uncheck exposes **Undo** until another mutation or reload replaces that context.\n\nEach tile has a separate **Item details** action for full provenance, receipts and shop actions.\n**Manage list**, beside search, reveals recipe/meal-plan sources, shops, products and history controls.\nClear search before changing a shop's item order; a filtered subset cannot replace the full order.\n\n## Sources and purchases\nAdd goods with exact g/kg/ml/l/piece quantities, optionally linked to an ingredient. Select a saved\nrecipe revision/servings, or explicitly choose meal-plan slots. Updating that selection replaces only\nmeal-plan contributions. Identical ingredient IDs/base dimensions aggregate; mass, volume and counts\nnever merge. Inspect **Recipes and contributions** for original purchased receipts. Source edits/removal\nchange only outstanding requirements. Undo reverses purchases without resurrecting removed requirements.\nPurchases belong to their source; excess amounts are not reassigned automatically to another recipe.\n**Shopping mode** returns to the focused list while retaining item details and large check-off targets.\n\n## Shops and learning\nCreate/rename/remove shops and set ingredient first/second choices under **Shops and shopping order**.\nAssignments/fallback moves change only metadata. Filter by a shop for keyboard/touch up/down ordering;\nmanual order wins. Clear it to use learning/default order. Unranked items use deterministic name order.\n\nLearning defaults off. Enable it, select a shop and explicitly start/complete a session. Only actual\ncheck-offs at that assigned shop count. Three completed multi-item sessions per item are required for\nan integer normalized position average. A correction excludes its whole session, including historical\nsessions containing that item. Sparse history is labelled uncertain. No location or remote training.\nInspect, discard, reset or disable history independently of shopping data. Disabling discards an active\nsession; completed history remains. Shop removal clears its metadata/history, preserving list sources\nand receipts; complete/discard an active session first.\n\n## Products and barcodes\nType a checked EAN-8/UPC-A/EAN-13/GTIN-14. Original leading zeros remain; equivalent codes share a\ncanonical GTIN-14 key. UPC-E expansion is unsupported. Household products resolve locally. Unknown\nproducts have complete manual entry. Preserve package labels verbatim and explicitly enter quantity/unit;\nnever guess amounts from a label. Confirm generic ingredient matches before aggregation. A match groups\nshopping amounts only, establishing no nutrition/allergy/health facts. Package counts add explicit sources\nwith immutable product snapshots; later product edits cannot change them.\n\nCamera starts only on request in a supported secure browser with native `BarcodeDetector` and permission.\nFrames stay local; tracks stop after detection, stopping, dialog closure/navigation or document hiding.\nManual entry works without camera support or network. Optional Open Food Facts lookup needs per-request\nconsent and discloses barcode, server IP and program user agent. No household names, recipes, quantities\nor frames leave the instance. Fixed HTTPS, no redirects, timeouts, 64 KiB responses and local throttling\nbound requests. Review source, `ODbL` 1.0, retrieved/modified dates and original provider label before saving.\nPackage quantity/matching remain explicit. Candidates expire after 24 hours; only the latest twenty stay.\n[Provider API](https://openfoodfacts.github.io/openfoodfacts-server/api/) and\n[barcode normalization](https://openfoodfacts.github.io/openfoodfacts-server/api/ref-barcode-normalization/).\n\n## API and recovery\nGET /api/household/shopping returns revisioned items, sources, shops/history and products. POST takes\n`{expected_revision,command:{operation,...}}`. Rust and strict `TypeScript` contracts define operations;\nquantities are decimal strings. POST /api/household/shopping/lookup requires a writer and\n`{expected_revision,barcode,consent:true}`. A lookup reserves a revision before requesting the provider;\nafter failure the client refreshes that revision while retaining the draft. Stale writes return 409.\nNever automatically replay a mutation after an unknown outcome: reload/review to avoid duplicate sources.\nNextcloud uses existing household proxy authentication, roles and CSRF marker.\n\nThe private mode-0600 household/shopping.json is atomically replaced under the household writer lock.\nLimits: 4 MiB, 1,000 sources, 5,000 purchases, 100 shops, 200 sessions and 1,000 product records. Capacity\nerrors are explicit. Reset learning history before its limit fills. Source/receipt archival needs a future\naccepted migration; never delete the ledger to bypass a limit. Corrupt data or .shopping.pending fails\nstartup closed. Stop the writer and restore a verified complete backup before restarting.\nNew metadata fields default on older shopping files; older binaries may reject them. Rollback restores\nthe matching complete backup. Back up membership, recipes, profiles, plans, images, ingredients and\nshopping together. Never run host/container writers simultaneously. Test fixtures are archived only\nwith ownership/content evidence; customer households are never selected by age or UI position.\n\nRecipe contributions buy measured base inputs. An explicit homemade output (`prepared_part`, F33)\nis displayed in the recipe but excluded from purchases; its preparation's measured ingredients\ncount once. This also applies to meal-plan synchronization and rescaling. Availability is never\ninferred globally from the ingredient name.\n\n## Household stock and offers\n\nUse **Inventory** or **Use stock** to review reservations before reducing purchases. Covered-by-stock\nentries remain separate from purchase receipts. Recording purchased stock and completed cooking is\nexplicit; booked receipts must be reversed before unchecking. Offers retain frozen package/price/source\nfacts and recheck current dietary constraints. See the [inventory guide](../page_docs_guides_inventory_md/index.html).\n"]
pub mod page_docs_guides_shopping_md {}

#[doc = "# Bootstrap provenance\n\nRetrieved 2026-11-07:\n- [curvyo/curvyo](https://github.com/curvyo/curvyo/tree/9ca3ebba86e511cd24559424f13fa2183caeea57):\n  AGENTS.md/CLAUDE.md conventions, specs/README.md lifecycle, docs/adr format and AGPL text.\n  Mealshop rewrites project-specific guidance; no Curvyo product modules or agent setup copied.\n- [Th3Link/homeautomation](https://github.com/Th3Link/homeautomation/tree/4c65a45d2046d795aaef5b79e3c311ae1f0a1847):\n  CI quality gate, nextest/coverage, cargo-msrv, debug/release build artifacts, cargo-sbom,\n  cargo-deny/audit, self-hosted Renovate and rustdoc/Pages publication patterns.\n  ESP/QEMU/firmware jobs do not apply. Metrics measure host server binary bytes, not ESP memory.\n  No upstream advisory exceptions or hardware-specific Renovate exclusions are carried over.\n\nThe initial customer request authorizes bootstrap and direct main publication. Future feature\nspecs remain Draft; they are not completed just because their documentation exists.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_provenance_md {}

#[doc = "# Product requirements\n\nOrigin: customer vision, 2026-11-07. Mealshop is a working name.\nThe [feature index](../page_specs_readme_md/index.html) maps F01–F32 one-to-one to acceptance contracts.\n\n## Cross-cutting requirements\n- N01: Rust/Axum/Tokio backend and Flutter client; standalone and free Nextcloud `ExApp` deployment.\n- N02: Selectable Git/file, PostgreSQL, SQLite or MariaDB persistence, with portable versioned data.\n- N03: Precise quantities, configurable presentation, reproducible nutrition and explicit unknowns.\n- N04: Household isolation, dietary exclusions above recommendations, consented calendar/learning use.\n- N05: AGPL-3.0-only complete software; paid official app, self-build allowed, free/paid hosted tiers.\n- N06: Imported recipes share their source URL only, including via collections, MCP and federation.\n- N07: Multi-day processes and reminders survive restart and explain delivery limitations.\n- N08: Spec-driven development, ADRs and CI with Renovate, builds, docs/Pages, audit, SBOM, metrics and MSRV.\n\n- N09: English is the coded/canonical base with stable language-independent IDs and separate translations. German/English i18n from the outset: UI, content names/search, exact locale-aware quantity input and number/date/plural formatting; preserve source language when translations are absent.\n- N10: Vue 3 product web clients for Nextcloud and standalone hosting. The existing framework-free web surface is a pragmatic demo only; significant UX/UI work belongs in the product workflow.\n\n## Scope and unresolved decisions\nM0 delivered the bootstrap. F02 adds a read-only, versioned nutrition starter catalogue and\nstateless demo accepted in PR #4. Its USDA snapshots are CC0; long-term provider coverage/refresh, identity/authentication,\ncalendar provider, database migration/sync details, payment provider, attestation feasibility,\nnotification transport, federation protocol and concrete paid tiers remain open. No customer\nprice, health guarantee or provider commitment is inferred from the vision.\n\n## Traceability\nN01 → F01/F18; N02 → F18; N03 → F02/F03/F04; N04 → F09/F10/F11;\nN05 → F19; N06 → F05/F16/F17/F20; N07 → F12/F13/F14; N08 → F01.\nEach feature plan must link its tests to its numbered acceptance criteria before Done.\n\nN09 → F21 (all client features); N10 → F22/F18. F02 is Done following customer merge of PR #4.\n\nF23 expands the kitchen reference catalogue from the customer ingredient list with exact BLS/USDA\nsource mappings; unresolved product profiles remain explicitly unknown.\n\nF23 is accepted through PR #5. F04/F18 now starts local recipe authoring and immutable file\nrevisions with pinned nutrition inputs; authenticated ownership, scaling and other adapters\nremain future work. See [recipe guide](../page_docs_guides_recipes_md/index.html).\n\nF25 adds household ingredient groups/subtypes, exact declared nutrients with explicit inheritance,\nand separate dietary/origin declarations; see its acceptance contract. N03 → F25, N04 → F25.\n\nF26 refines native authoring, recipe-owned preparation parts, optional constituents and explicit\nsorbitol intolerance declarations. N03/N04/N09/N10 → F26. Full catalogue grouping follows separately.\n\nF27 organizes every bundled ingredient through a complete exact-ID mapping, after F26 verification.\nGroups provide discovery and optional explicit household declarations, without shared source nutrition.\n\nF28 reviews all shipped native views and improves draft protection, profile editing, discovery,\nerror recovery and review focus. N03/N04/N09/N10 → F28.\n\nF06/F07/F08 deliver protected shopping provenance, optional household shop ordering and manual/native\nbarcode product capture. N03/N04/N09/N10 apply. F21/F22 close only the delivered Nextcloud scope\nby explicit customer decision. F29/F30 retain the complete original cross-client acceptance contracts\nunchanged; standalone/Flutter localization, versioned content translations and standalone shell remain open.\nN09 → F21/F29; N10 → F22/F30/F18.\n\nF12/F13 deliver native Nextcloud guided cooking and reviewed multi-day baking with durable UTC deadlines,\nIANA/DST resolution and personal `AppAPI` reminders. N03/N04/N07/N09/N10 apply; accepted delivery is not\nguaranteed device delivery. See [cooking guide](../page_docs_guides_cooking_md/index.html) and ADR 0016.\n\nF31 records the customer-approved unified Nextcloud meal/preparation calendar, mealprep allocations, multipart authoring and typed ingredient-line extensions for future device consumption. N03/N07/N09/N10 apply; see specs/0031-unified-preparation. Implementation status is tracked there.\n\nF32 refines household shopping discovery, item tiles, exact quantity entry and check-off/undo within\nthe native Nextcloud shell, following the supplied Bring interaction reference. N03/N04/N09/N10 apply.\n\nF33 refines recipe reading/editing against the approved screens: compact always-open composition,\noptional plus actions and explicit homemade outputs with measured base inputs. N03/N04/N07/N09/N10\napply. See [F33](../page_specs_0033_compact_recipe_preparation_specification_md/index.html) and ADR 0018.\n\n### F34 — Source-backed nutrition ranges\nThe customer authorizes comparative estimates for missing composition, separate from original\nsource facts. Freeze selected reference observations and matching rationale, distinguish reported\nsupplements from estimated/group/declared ranges, aggregate exact bounds and show native warning\nindicators with complete/partial totals and provenance. Do not infer constituents, allergy safety,\ndensity, nutrient definitions or population confidence. The customer explicitly authorized private\nhousehold ingredient review in memory only, without household audit copies or record logging.\nSee [F34](../page_specs_0034_evidence_nutrition_ranges_specification_md/index.html) and ADR 0019.\n\n\n## F05 Nextcloud delivery boundary\nRecipes remain household-owned. Private collections preserve ordered recipe/revision references;\nsource owners manage named-account grants, addressed invitations to household owners and direct\ngrants to another household they own. Current recipient membership gates every shared read.\nImported recipes share only their retained original HTTPS source URL, including historical revisions\nand collection entries. Revocation prevents later server access, without claiming to remove copies.\nSee [F05](../page_specs_0005_sharing_collections_specification_md/index.html) and\n[the sharing guide](../page_docs_guides_sharing_md/index.html). Payments, public links, federation and other clients remain\noutside this Nextcloud delivery.\n\n## F11 Nextcloud delivery boundary\nExplicit manual or verified completed-session history, household-reported ratings, owner-managed\nlocal learning epochs and explained whole-pool suggestions reuse existing household authorization\nand dietary/time/quantity checks. Reset removes learned state without silently replaying retained\nratings. Model changes have a versioned synthetic baseline comparison; real-world effectiveness\nis not inferred from offline proxies. See [F11](../page_specs_0011_recommendation_feedback_specification_md/index.html).\n\nF17 delivers disabled-by-default per-account MCP grants within current Nextcloud household roles,\nsource-only imported projections, canonical nonpersistent recipe previews and atomically audited,\nidempotent confirmed creation. The supplied stdio bridge uses existing Nextcloud app passwords;\nhosted OAuth and per-client delegated credentials are outside this authentication slice.\nN03/N04/N06/N09/N10 apply. See [MCP guide](../page_docs_guides_mcp_md/index.html) and ADR 0022.\n\nF16 delivers customer-operated public HTTPS recipe retrieval, bounded structured previews, explicit\nnative ingredient/quantity/source review and manual private fallback. DNS/peer/redirect/robots bounds\nprotect the fetch surface. Atomic first-revision import receipts retain original fields separately\nfrom reviewed values and preserve source-only sharing through later edits, collections and MCP.\nN03/N04/N06/N09/N10 apply; see [import guide](../page_docs_guides_recipe_import_md/index.html) and ADR 0023.\n\n## F15 Nextcloud delivery boundary\n\nF15 delivers exact household lots, reviewed reservations, explicit purchase/completed-cooking stock\nbookings, durable idempotency/reversals and manual shop offers with current dietary checks. Plans and\nshopping check-off never consume physical stock. No offer provider, scraping or automatic purchasing\nis implied. N03/N04/N09/N10 apply; see [inventory guide](../page_docs_guides_inventory_md/index.html) and ADR 0024.\n\n## F14 Nextcloud delivery boundary\n\nF14 implements source-linked beer methods plus independently researched cider, white/red wine and\ntraditional mead processes, immutable recipes with exact liquid quantities, reviewed\nindependent batches/equipment reservations, recipe-specific pre-brew reminders and original manual/\ninstrument readings with append-only corrections on the shared durable scheduler. BIAB is all-grain;\nstrategy/layout/heat/sparging are distinct, including the requested named and DIY systems. No fake food\nservings, unconfigured live connector, automatic equipment control or non-beer chemistry is implied.\nSeparate typed fermentation methods and phase orders satisfy the original AC 5 independent-validation\nguard; no grain-processing assumptions are reused for these drinks. N03/N07/N09/N10 apply; see\n[brewing guide](../page_docs_guides_brewing_md/index.html), research and ADR 0025. Acceptance evidence is tracked in F14.\n\n## F35 Flutter delivery planning\n\nThe customer supplied a mobile target specification on 2026-11-13 and requested fewer, larger PRs,\nwith a smartphone-usable result from the first implementation delivery. F35 imports the unchanged\nreference pack, audits the current server/web contracts and proposes five complete phone releases.\nThis planning PR needs customer review/merge and explicit Go before implementation. Flutter remains\nunimplemented; F18/F29/F30 and their original acceptance criteria retain their existing statuses.\nN01/N03/N04/N06–N10 apply. See [F35](../page_specs_0035_flutter_mobile_client_specification_md/index.html) and\n[the plan](../page_specs_0035_flutter_mobile_client_plan_md/index.html).\n"]
pub mod page_docs_requirements_md {}

#[doc = "# Roadmap\n\nThis is a proposed delivery order, not a promise of dates. Dependencies can change after review.\n\n| Milestone | Outcome | Features |\n|---|---|---|\n| M0 | Runnable server, specs, docs and delivery pipeline | F01 |\n| M1 | Recipe/nutrition foundation, household constraints, first storage/deployment slice, localization and product web clients | F02–F04, F09, F18, F21–F23, F29–F30 |\n| M2 | Shared recipes, family plans, shopping, guided cooking, incremental preference feedback | F05–F07, F10–F12 |\n| M3 | Barcode, long dough processes, inventory/offers, imports, MCP | F08, F13, F15–F17 |\n| M4 | Brewing/fermentation and commercial operations | F14, F19 |\n| M5 | Opt-in federation | F20 |\n\nM1 starts with local recipe revision files and pinned BLS/USDA nutrition sources.\nThe authenticated identity model, Git/SQL adapters and Flutter interaction remain to be specified. F18 spans several milestones: a first backend\nis not completion of the four-backend/Nextcloud/client acceptance contract. Online learning\nstarts with inspectable rules and feedback; production ML follows measured benefit.\n\nPR #4 is accepted. Its browser surface is a demo only. F21 starts with pragmatic demo localization;\nF22 specifies Vue 3 shells for Nextcloud and standalone hosting before product UI implementation.\n\nPR #5 is accepted (F23 Done; initial F21 slice accepted). F04/F18 now starts local recipe\nauthoring and file persistence; proportional scaling and production integrations remain open.\n\n## Delivered Nextcloud scope\nF06/F07/F08 provide native household shopping, optional shop ordering and barcode products.\nThe customer approved closing F21/F22 for the delivered Nextcloud client. F29/F30 retain their\noriginal complete cross-client acceptance contracts for unfinished content/standalone/Flutter work.\nF18 remains In progress for its other deployment/storage/client requirements.\n\n## Proposed phone delivery sequence (F35)\n\nThis is a plan for customer approval, not delivered app functionality or a dated commitment.\nEach implementation PR contains its necessary Flutter, Rust, compatibility and device evidence:\n\n1. P1: real Nextcloud connection and independent shared shopping, including safe offline replay.\n2. P2: household/profile tour, recipe/ingredient/shared library and optional activity.\n3. P3: reviewed whole-week planning, physical mealprep allocations and inventory/offers.\n4. P4: native guided cooking, baking/fermentation timers, reminders and feedback.\n5. P5: phone camera, share-sheet URL/barcode/OCR review and final portability audit.\n\n[Detailed dependencies, acceptance and install gates](../page_specs_0035_flutter_mobile_client_plan_md/index.html).\nThe customer must merge the planning PR and give a separate Go. The initial phone platform still needs\nconfirmation; Android APK is a proposal. Standalone identity, other persistence adapters and hosted\ncommerce keep their own contracts rather than delaying the first Nextcloud phone journey.\n"]
