#[doc = "# F10 family planning acceptance\n\n- [x] Protected persisted date/meal slots with pinned recipe revisions, attendees and servings (AC 1).\n- [x] Manual selection and constraint-compatible reviewed suggestions with rejected reasons (AC 1, 5).\n- [x] Consented read-only calendar snapshot import, visible source and manual override (AC 2).\n- [x] Active/elapsed/advance estimates and explicit unknown/batch-size timing uncertainty (AC 3).\n- [x] Preview/apply replacement/removal with scaled nutrition and shopping requirement deltas (AC 4).\n- [x] Profile/plan revision conflicts, renewed review markers, source revision retention (AC 4–5).\n- [x] Acceptance tests for constraints, consent, overrides, time, conflicts, restart and failures.\n- [x] Browser acceptance and full local repository gate; current-commit CI evidence in the PR.\n\nTests in tests/planning.rs map directly to the five acceptance criteria. Current local scope,\ncalendar import format and the distinction from live providers/F06 are documented in the guide\nand ADR 0011. Source uncertainty is retained; unknown data never becomes safe automatically.\n\nStatus: Done for the documented local/import-based F10 contract after local and browser acceptance.\nSee [validation](../page_specs_0010_meal_planning_validation_md/index.html). No live provider or F06 shopping execution is implied.\n"]
pub mod page_specs_0010_meal_planning_plan_md {}

#[doc = "# Family meal planning and calendar attendance\n\nStatus: Done\nPriority: Must\nOrigin: Customer\nMilestone: M2\n\n## User value\nChoose feasible meals for the family and adapt when plans change.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n1. Given a day/meal and attendees, a planner can select recipes manually or request constraint-compatible suggestions.\n2. Given consented calendar access, attendance is proposed with a visible source and can be overridden without editing the external calendar.\n3. Given per-day preparation windows, suggestions distinguish active work, total elapsed time and advance preparation.\n4. Given a last-minute attendance/time change, the user can choose an alternative, rescale servings and preview shopping-list changes before applying them.\n5. Given no feasible meal, the planner explains the conflicting constraints instead of silently violating intolerances.\n\n## Out of scope\nAutonomous calendar writes or purchases.\n\n## UX notes\nSee ux-notes.md.\n\n## Links\nRequirement: [F10](../page_docs_requirements_md/index.html). Delivery tasks: [plan.md](../page_specs_0010_meal_planning_plan_md/index.html). Decisions: [adrs.md](../page_specs_0010_meal_planning_adrs_md/index.html).\n\n## Implementation contract\nLocal protected planning uses F09 operator/reader roles and immutable recipe revisions. Calendar\nattendance is supplied through a consented read-only snapshot import with source/date/event and\nmapped household IDs; no specific live provider is implied. Preparation is estimated from ordered\nrecipe steps; scaled batch-time uncertainty is shown for review. Preview/apply revisions prevent\nstale profile/plan decisions, and known hard/time conflicts cannot be acknowledged away.\nShopping changes are plan-derived mass requirements, not completion of F06 purchase workflows.\nUnknown allergen data remains review-required. Hosted provider adapters and process scheduling\nbelong to their own features; F10 does not autonomously write external calendars or make purchases.\n"]
pub mod page_specs_0010_meal_planning_specification_md {}

#[doc = "# Pragmatic family-planning demo\n\nUse household credentials already present in the demo. Load family/plans explicitly; choose\nparticipants, date/meal, pinned recipe and preparation budgets. Both manual selection and a\nbounded multi-select candidate list support suggestions. Show blocked reasons, current profile\nnotes, timing uncertainty, source/attendance overrides, scaled nutrition and shopping deltas.\nA separate review checkbox and Apply action commit a feasible preview; changed fields invalidate\nit. Read-only users can preview but server authorization rejects mutation. Errors keep inputs.\nSaved slots can be loaded, replaced or removed after an explicit removal preview.\n\nCalendar input is a bounded JSON attendance snapshot, with source/date/event/proposed member IDs.\nConsent is separate from import and manual attendance override is explicit. No external writes.\nUnknown timing/data yields conflicts or dietary review, never an inferred safe result. Strings\nuse English-base keyed resources with German equivalents; this remains a disposable demo.\n"]
pub mod page_specs_0010_meal_planning_ux_notes_md {}

#[doc = "# F10 acceptance evidence\n\nStatus: Done for the documented local family-planning and read-only attendance-import contract.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n1. Protected manual recipe selection and bounded suggestions use pinned revisions, selected\n   attendees and F09 review. Known hard exclusions reject candidates; dislikes only rank them.\n2. Consented calendar snapshots retain source/event/date/proposal. Tests verify missing consent\n   rejection, source display and explicit manual attendance override without any external write.\n   The actual supported adapter is JSON snapshot import, not a live provider connection.\n3. Step timing distinguishes active, sequential elapsed and advance lead. Tests cover insufficient\n   active/elapsed windows and unknown step timing. UI makes original-batch timing uncertainty\n   explicit when portions change; no inferred parallelism or batch-time multiplier is claimed.\n4. Preview/apply supports alternatives, changed attendees/servings and removals. Tests verify\n   signed grocery deltas, derived persisted shopping requirements, revision conflict rejection,\n   explicit dietary acknowledgement, pinned historical recipe inputs and restart round trips.\n5. Suggestions return rejected conflict reasons and no-feasible explanations; both API and\n   browser tests verify hard constraints cannot be bypassed by acknowledgement or soft dislikes.\n\n## Automated results\n`cargo nextest run --workspace --locked`: 40 tests passed, none skipped, including four family\nplanning journeys. Additional focused planning/household runs passed after final arithmetic\nerror handling cleanup. Six Node locale tests and JS syntax checks passed. Catalogue import,\nnutrition source, conversion and allergen verifiers passed. Rustfmt, clippy with warnings denied,\nrelease build, licenses, cargo-audit, strict rustdoc and MSRV 1.85.0 passed. Current-commit CI\nlinks and outcomes are recorded in the PR.\n\nStorage tests simulate interrupted and corrupt plan snapshots; startup fails closed and releases\nlocks on failure. Reads/export remain protected; readers cannot apply. Existing F09 access,\nprofile export/removal/conflict/restart and F03/F04 nutrition/quantity tests remain green.\n\n## Browser results\nWith fictional profiles (one milk exclusion) and oat/milk recipes, suggestions retained the oat\nrecipe and rejected milk. The preview showed current member notes, active 600 s / elapsed\n2400 s / advance 1200 s and +200 g oats for four servings. Explicit acknowledgement/apply saved\nrevision 1 and 200 g derived demand; reloading preserved the pinned meal. Setting available\nactive time to zero produced no feasible meal and distinct timing/dietary conflict explanations.\n\n## Scope and limitations\nCalendar access is a user-consented, read-only attendance snapshot import with already mapped\nhousehold IDs. Live polling, OAuth, ICS recurrence/timezone expansion, external calendar writes,\npurchases, inventory and F06's full shopping workflow remain separate work. Review never\nestablishes allergen safety; data gaps, cross-contact and batch-time changes remain explicit.\nCapacity: 100 meal slots / 1 MiB local snapshot and 50 candidate revisions per suggestion call.\n"]
pub mod page_specs_0010_meal_planning_validation_md {}

#[doc = "# Decisions for Cooking history and online preference learning\n\n- [ADR 0005](../page_docs_adr_0005_privacy_and_learning_md/index.html)\n\n## Feature-local decisions\n2026-11-07: Acceptance criteria capture the customer vision; unspecified implementation choices remain proposals. No future feature is implemented by this bootstrap.\n\n- [ADR 0021](../page_docs_adr_0021_completed_history_and_local_preferences_md/index.html): explicit completed\n  sources, household-reported ratings, opt-in epochs, incremental aggregates and constrained ranking.\n"]
pub mod page_specs_0011_recommendation_feedback_adrs_md {}

#[doc = "# F11 model-change evaluation\n\nThe versioned fixture is [recommendations-v1.json](https://github.com/Th3Link/mealshop/blob/main/tests/fixtures/recommendations-v1.json).\nIt contains four **synthetic** held-out choices with separately dated training observations and\nheld-out 1–5 rating labels. It is hand-authored test evidence, not customer data or a user study.\nThe evaluator uses the production incremental aggregate and ranking functions.\n\nRun `cargo test --locked --lib feedback::evaluation -- --nocapture` to reproduce the comparison.\nChanging an algorithm version requires updating the explicit fixture contract and reviewing all four\nmetrics. A constraint violation fails the test independently of any preference improvement.\n\n| Metric | `rules-v1` baseline | `history-v1` learned rule |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Selected hard-constraint violations | 0 | 0 |\n| Held-out acceptance proxy (first choice equals labelled choice) | 0 / 4 | 4 / 4 |\n| Distinct first choices across the four cases | 1 | 2 |\n| Held-out rating-utility proxy (rating points / choices) | 4 / 4 | 20 / 4 |\n\nThese fractions retain their denominators and the 1–5 rating scale. They do not estimate a real\nacceptance rate, prediction confidence, human satisfaction or causal benefit. The fixture deliberately\nchecks learnable preferences; it cannot establish generalization or demographic fairness. Real-world\neffectiveness remains unknown until sufficient observed evidence and an appropriate study exist.\n\nThe offline eligible set includes a strongly rated blocked candidate that is excluded before ranking.\nSeparate authenticated API regressions exercise real current household exclusions against high positive\nratings, opt-out, source revisions and ordinary planning. Existing dietary regressions cover allergen\nand intolerance declarations, frozen/current composition and hard-review enforcement. Cold-start,\nsparse history, discovery beyond the first 20 recipes, whole-pool pagination and reset are independently\ntested. Thus preference gains cannot waive the constraint gate, and the synthetic metric is not used\nto justify weakened safety checks or a mandatory ML framework.\n"]
pub mod page_specs_0011_recommendation_feedback_evaluation_md {}

#[doc = "# F11 delivery plan\n\n## Build order\n1. Completed history and incremental aggregates: validated model → atomic store → protected API → native recording/editing.\n2. Planner ranking: canonical feasibility checks → whole-pool baseline/learned ordering → native reasons/review.\n3. Privacy and evaluation: owner scope/reset/deletion → deterministic offline benchmark → live role/restart/browser verification.\n\nThese are verifiable parts of the existing F11 contract, not new standalone products or adapters.\n\n## Tasks\n- [x] Read current requirements, F11/ADR 0005 and actual cooking/planning/household boundaries.\n- [x] Record the conservative delivery contract and native flows before implementation.\n- [x] Implement completed/manual history, validated incremental updates and durable restart.\n- [x] Integrate household roles, trusted actors and source validation with API regressions.\n- [x] Deliver and verify native history entry/list/edit/delete and completed-session entry points.\n- [x] Reuse canonical feasibility checks and add versioned whole-pool explainable ranking.\n- [x] Implement owner-only opt-in/scope/reset and separate history deletion.\n- [x] Compare baseline/learned rules with labelled offline acceptance/variety/rating proxies.\n- [x] Verify real Nextcloud roles, exact values, responsive accessibility, restart and synthetic cleanup.\n- [x] Run required repository/client/container gates and prepare the single-PR delivery with acceptance evidence.\n\nThe PR's live exact-head CI is the remaining remote gate before handoff; its result is recorded on\nthe PR rather than inferred from this committed checklist. See [validation.md](../page_specs_0011_recommendation_feedback_validation_md/index.html).\n\n## Acceptance trace\nAC 1: explicit completed/manual history and immutable source tests plus native editor workflow.\nAC 2: incremental correction/deletion/count tests and explained suggestion integration.\nAC 3: cold-start/discovery tests and visible unreviewed dietary states.\nAC 4: reproducible versioned synthetic baseline comparison; no human-effectiveness claims.\nAC 5: role/scope/epoch/reset/deletion tests and constraint checks after opt-out.\n"]
pub mod page_specs_0011_recommendation_feedback_plan_md {}

#[doc = "# Cooking history and online preference learning\n\nStatus: Done\nPriority: Should\nOrigin: Customer\nMilestone: M2\n\n## User value\nImprove suggestions from real cooking and family feedback.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n1. Given a completed cook, history records recipe revision, attendees, date, actual effort and individual/household rating without counting merely planned meals.\n2. Given new ratings or repeated cooking, suggestions update incrementally and explain relevant history, constraints and confidence.\n3. Given cold-start users, transparent rules provide usable suggestions; new recipes remain discoverable rather than being excluded by sparse feedback.\n4. Given model changes, evaluation compares constraint violations, acceptance, variety and user satisfaction against the baseline.\n5. Given a reset or opt-out, learned preference state can be removed and suggestions fall back to rules; hard exclusions remain enforced.\n\n## Out of scope\nMandatory cloud training or a chosen ML framework before evaluation.\n\n## UX notes\nUI flows, accessibility, empty/error states and user review points must be specified before Ready.\n\n## Links\nRequirement: [F11](../page_docs_requirements_md/index.html). Delivery tasks: [plan.md](../page_specs_0011_recommendation_feedback_plan_md/index.html). Decisions: [adrs.md](../page_specs_0011_recommendation_feedback_adrs_md/index.html).\n\n## Nextcloud delivery contract\nThe customer requested F11 after the merged F05 delivery. Use the existing protected household,\nrecipe-revision, cooking-session and planning boundaries. This contract enters Ready for the\nrequested implementation under the customer's autonomous-work instruction, then In progress.\nNo standalone/Flutter shell, remote training provider or new crate is introduced. The delivered\nscope and reproducible acceptance evidence are recorded in [validation.md](../page_specs_0011_recommendation_feedback_validation_md/index.html).\n\n- A history entry is an explicitly confirmed completed cook, never an automatically imported plan.\n  It references a saved recipe/revision, servings, actual civil date, actual attendees, optional\n  reported active effort in exact seconds, optional household rating and optional attendee ratings\n  (1–5). Unknown effort/rating stays null. A completed session can seed an entry only after all\n  its stages are completed. Manual recording supports cooking performed outside the timers.\n- Pin the recipe title/language/revision and actual source on creation. Corrections retain their\n  recorded actor/time and record the updating actor/time. Session-derived entries cannot duplicate\n  a source session or change its recipe; historical entries survive session removal. Writers can\n  correct/delete history, readers can browse. Stale writes fail without losing the client draft.\n- Learning is off by default. Only the household owner manages the learning scope; household\n  ratings/cooking frequency and optionally selected attendees' ratings stay inside that household.\n  Ratings are household-reported for dietary profiles, not privately authenticated personal votes.\n  All existing household readers can see the history. Explain that visibility before recording.\n- Enabling/changing scope starts a fresh learning epoch. Only explicitly saved/corrected cooks in\n  the current epoch contribute; no opt-in or restart silently processes old ratings. Opt-out/reset\n  disables learning, erases aggregates and starts a new epoch. Raw history has separate confirmed\n  single-entry/all-history deletion; reset must not silently delete it. No analytics requests leave\n  the server and no health/rating data appears in application logs or public fixtures.\n- Incrementally update bounded recipe/attendee counts and rating sums on save/correction/deletion.\n  Validate persisted aggregates against current-epoch records at restart. Do not treat missing\n  ratings as zero or timer estimates/elapsed wall time as measured effort.\n- Reuse the planner's current dietary/time/quantity checks before ranking. Explicit exclusions,\n  allergens, intolerance triggers, compositional closure and current plus frozen declarations are\n  hard filters. Unknown allergy coverage continues to require review. Learning cannot make an\n  infeasible recipe eligible. Recommendation selection retains the existing explicit plan review.\n- The baseline orders feasible recipes by declared dislikes and stable recipe identity, with a\n  deterministic discovery position for a recently added recipe. An opt-in\n  versioned integer rule uses smoothed recorded ratings, actual cooking frequency and recent-cook\n  variety; confidence describes evidence counts, not a probability or allergy/health assurance.\n  Keep recipes without current learning evidence visible and reserve an early discovery position\n  within equal dislike groups. Recipe-identity feedback can include older revisions; disclose their\n  contribution count. The variety window uses the seven reported civil days ending on the plan day.\n  Return reasons, sample counts, model/history versions and baseline position. Evaluate the whole\n  bounded pool once and return at most 20 previews per page, rather than ranking separate client batches.\n- Model-change evaluation is an offline, deterministic, versioned synthetic holdout benchmark\n  against the baseline: hard violations, held-out acceptance, distinct recommendations and held-out\n  rating utility. Acceptance/utility are explicitly labelled offline proxies, not measured human\n  satisfaction or causal gains. Keep real-world effectiveness unknown until observed evidence exists.\n  Constraint failures block the benchmark regardless of preference gains. No opaque ML framework\n  is selected before this evidence. Publish no evaluation dataset derived from private households.\n\n## Implementation and verification\nUse `src/feedback/` for the bounded history/learning model, storage and protected routes; extend\n`src/planning.rs` through shared candidate checks, and reuse completed cooking sources. Native\nVue views/contracts live in `clients/nextcloud/src/`; integration tests in `tests/feedback.rs`,\nalgorithm/storage regressions in the feature module, browser coverage under `clients/nextcloud/e2e/`.\nUse typed tagged variants, exact integer counters/durations, stable keyed EN/DE messages and native\nNextcloud controls. Existing recipe files, grants, plans, authentication and CSRF remain intact.\n\nCommands: `cargo nextest run --workspace --locked`; `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings`;\n`npm --prefix clients/nextcloud test`; `npm --prefix clients/nextcloud run build`; all repository gates.\nTest planned/active/cancelled exclusion, completed/manual sources, duplicate/stale writes, scope/reset,\ncorrections/deletion, restart/corruption, household/role isolation, cold start/discovery, immutable\nreferences, strong positive ratings against hard constraints and reproducible baseline evaluation.\nLive checks use new ledger-recorded synthetic households only; never inspect/copy retained private\nhousehold directories for feature testing or cleanup. `AGENTS.md` remains unchanged.\n"]
pub mod page_specs_0011_recommendation_feedback_specification_md {}

#[doc = "# Native cooking history and learning\n\nUse household navigation History for actual completed cooks and an owner-only Learning settings\nsection within it. A compact dated list shows recipe, servings, actual attendees, reported effort\nand household/individual ratings without decorative dashboards. Link back to the exact recipe\nrevision. Record a cook from history (manual) or from a completed cooking session, keeping the\ncompletion confirmation and household visibility clear. Never turn a planned meal into history.\n\nThe editor uses visible labels, an existing-recipe selector, actual-date input, attendee selectors,\noptional effort and native labelled 1–5 selects with Not rated. Session-derived recipe/servings are\nlocked to the actual session; no guessed effort is prefilled. Explicit save, cancel, dirty protection,\nconfirmed deletion, loading/error/conflict states and focus restoration follow existing views.\n\nLearning is an explicit owner-controlled opt-in. Explain who can see ratings, which profile ratings\nare used, that changing scope starts fresh, and the separate effects of reset versus history deletion.\nUse a clear current state and ordinary controls; never present a generic enable-all consent banner.\n\nPlanning suggestions retain Review and the current dietary warning/review flow. Show compact reason\nlines with actual sample counts and whether a recipe is a discovery choice; use words alongside icons.\nRatings affect order only after current hard constraints. Do not label a recipe safe, imply clinical\nconfidence or hide unknowns. Keep scope, profile and history revisions tied to one server response.\n\nUse Nextcloud Vue components, theme tokens and existing typography; Taste guides alignment/spacing\nand UI/UX Pro Max guides form feedback/confirmation. No landing-page assets, palettes, fonts, resets\nor new UI framework. Verify EN/DE, light/dark, keyboard, 1440/390/320 px, axe and screenshots in the real\nNextcloud instance, with only owned synthetic fixtures.\n"]
pub mod page_specs_0011_recommendation_feedback_ux_notes_md {}

#[doc = "# F11 acceptance evidence\n\n## Contract and automated checks\n`cargo nextest run --workspace --locked`: **126 passed, 0 skipped**.\n`npm --prefix clients/nextcloud test`: **28 passed**.\n\n| Acceptance | Observable evidence |\n|---|---|\n| 1: actual completed cooks | Explicit manual confirmation, actual attendees/date/timezone, optional exact reported effort and 1–5 ratings; active/cancelled sessions rejected; completed-session recipe/servings pinned; duplicate/stale writes rejected; original/correcting actors retained; historical recipe reading and correction after session removal |\n| 2: incremental, explained suggestions | Contribution subtraction/replacement/deletion, selected profile scopes, persisted counts/sums/dates/revisions, current-epoch reconstruction on restart; coherent whole-pool ordering and 20-preview pages; reasons, support counts and earlier-revision evidence in native planning |\n| 3: cold start and discovery | Rule fallback, explicit absent/limited/repeated history, declared-dislike ordering, early discovery within equal-dislike groups, 25-candidate pagination regression; canonical feasibility precedes ranking |\n| 4: model comparison | Versioned synthetic holdout compares hard violations, held-out acceptance, distinct first choices and rating utility against `rules-v1`; see [evaluation](../page_specs_0011_recommendation_feedback_evaluation_md/index.html); these are offline proxies, not measured human satisfaction or causal gains |\n| 5: reset and opt-out | Off by default, owner-only scope/reset/bulk-clear, new epochs without replay, same-scope saves preserve contributions, separate retained-history deletion, readers cannot write, recipe grantees gain no history access, removed members denied, strongest positive ratings cannot override exclusions before/after opt-out |\n\nFocused tests are in `tests/feedback.rs`, `src/feedback/`, `src/exapp/feedback_tests.rs` and\n`clients/nextcloud/tests/feedback-api.test.ts`. Fixtures are synthetic and contain no household audit data.\nThe fixture comparison gives 0 hard violations in both modes, acceptance hits 0/4 versus 4/4,\ndistinct first choices 1 versus 2 and rating points 4/4 versus 20/4. These intentionally learnable\nexamples do not establish real-world satisfaction, fairness or generalization.\n\n## Repository and client gates\nAll 17 required catalogue, nutrition evidence, conversions, allergens, localization, formatting,\nClippy, Rust test, licence/advisory, strict Vue build, contract and npm audit gates passed. Strict\nrustdoc and demo syntax checks also pass; generated documentation is checked after status updates.\n\n```sh\npython3 scripts/generate_docs.py --check\npython3 scripts/import_catalogue.py --check\npython3 scripts/verify_catalogue.py\npython3 scripts/import_nutrition_estimates.py --check\npython3 scripts/verify_nutrition_estimates.py\npython3 scripts/verify_conversions.py\npython3 scripts/verify_allergens.py\nnode --check src/web/demo.mjs\nnode --test tests/i18n.test.mjs\ncargo fmt --all --check\ncargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings\ncargo nextest run --workspace --locked\ncargo deny check licenses\ncargo audit\nnpm --prefix clients/nextcloud run format:check\nnpm --prefix clients/nextcloud test\nnpm --prefix clients/nextcloud run build\nnpm --prefix clients/nextcloud audit --audit-level=moderate\nRUSTDOCFLAGS='-D warnings' cargo doc --workspace --no-deps --locked\n```\n\nThe production client transforms 873 modules and produces approximately 168 kB CSS (24 kB gzip)\nand 1.48 MB JS (451 kB gzip). These are build output sizes, not load-time measurements.\nNo crate/runtime dependency, remote training service, global CSS reset, font or theme palette was added.\nExisting upstream Zod annotation notices do not affect strict type checking.\n\n## Native browser evidence\n`clients/nextcloud/e2e/feedback.spec.ts` exercises completed manual/session recording, exact effort,\nfuture-date rejection, corrections, retained conflicting drafts, explicit reload/discard, pinned recipe\nrevisions, owner opt-in/reset, no automatic plans-as-cooks, two-account reader restrictions, discovery,\npositive/negative ratings, hard exclusions and the normal acknowledged plan review.\n\nForm, history/detail, learning and suggestion states are checked in EN/DE, system light/dark,\n1440/390/320 px with no page overflow and scoped WCAG 2.2 AA axe checks. Native checkbox actions use\nthe keyboard. Screenshot inspection refined native field labels, compact definition-list spacing and\nlearning controls; results receive focus after loading. These are scoped automated checks, not blanket\naccessibility certification. Screenshots remain local ignored artifacts of owned synthetic data.\n\n## Installed container verification\nThe complete installed Playwright sweep passed **26/26 (9.4 minutes)**. After the final scoped\nalignment changes, all three F11 scenarios passed again on the final image (**3/3, 1.4 minutes**).\nThat image is `mealshop.local/mealshop:cooking-history-feedback`,\n`sha256:16877aa292e05b95ecd2110320f1c0dbedf73daff73d0099d9d2cb8b742f4165`, built from\nimplementation commit `f728682`. Later acceptance/status documentation changes do not change the\nfunctional server/client implementation. The final image passes\n`python3 scripts/test_exapp_container.py --image mealshop.local/mealshop:cooking-history-feedback`\nin both isolated rootless TCP and Unix-socket modes, including F11 history/learning/constrained\nsuggestions and existing lifecycle, authentication, CSRF, sharing, shopping and cooking checks.\n\nIt is installed in the existing Nextcloud 35.0.2 test deployment with its existing `AppAPI`\nregistration, secret, internal URL and data volume. Only one application writer runs. Public JS/CSS\nresponses match the running container's assets, and the expected image reports healthy.\n\nA separately ledger-recorded synthetic fixture verifies an actual installed container restart:\nhistory list/detail, exact 125-second effort, actor provenance, policy/epoch/counts and explained\nsuggestion responses remain identical. Opt-out erases aggregates while retaining raw history;\nre-enabling leaves contribution counts at zero instead of replaying that history.\n\nAfter testing, only ledger-recorded synthetic households are validated and archived with the single\nwriter stopped. Retained household directories are neither read nor copied by cleanup. Retained\nmembership/library entries and monotonic household identities are preserved; no active ledger-owned\nfixture remains. No broad original-file checksum audit is claimed. `AGENTS.md` is unchanged.\nThe final container is restarted and healthy after cleanup. Exact-head remote CI is tracked in the PR.\n"]
pub mod page_specs_0011_recommendation_feedback_validation_md {}

