#[doc = "# Brewing-method and equipment research\n\nDate: 2026-11-13. Origin: the customer's explicit request to research BIAB, Grainfather, Brew Monk,\nSpeidel Braumeister, DIY all-grain, extract, partial mash and related variants before F14 implementation.\nThis document separates source facts from Mealshop's proposed representation. No manufacturer cloud\nintegration, controller compatibility, universal recipe, efficiency or safety result is inferred.\n\n## Process families\n\n| Family | What changes in the process | Consequence for a usable template | Primary source |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Malt extract (LME/DME) | Concentrated malt extract supplies the wort's fermentable material; the homebrew process need not recreate its original mash. | Dissolve/add extract, recipe-specific boil/additions, cool and ferment; retain liquid/dry extract identity. | [AHA: extract manufacture](https://www.homebrewersassociation.org/how-to-brew/how-malt-extract-is-made/) |\n| Extract with specialty-grain steeping | Grain steeping adds recipe character; it is distinct from relying on a conversion mash. | Add a separately named steep/remove-grain phase before extract additions. Do not call it partial mash. | [AHA: working with extract recipes](https://www.homebrewersassociation.org/how-to-brew/working-with-extract-recipes/) |\n| Partial mash / mini mash | Some fermentables come from grain mashing; extract supplies another portion. | Include a real mash and grain separation as well as explicit extract additions; keep both ingredient sources. | [AHA: partial mash](https://homebrewersassociation.org/tutorials/partial-mash-homebrewing/partial-mash-homebrewing/) |\n| All-grain | The recipe uses a grain mash to produce wort before boiling and fermentation. | Include mash, lautering, optional sparge and collection; equipment determines transfers. | [AHA: batch-sparge all-grain](https://homebrewersassociation.org/tutorials/all-grain-batch-sparge-homebrewing/all-grain-batch-sparge-homebrewing/) |\n| Brew in a bag (BIAB) | An all-grain implementation using a removable bag as the grain separator, commonly in one vessel. | Mash in the bag, lift/drain, then boil in the kettle. Optional rinse is an explicit variant, not an invisible mandatory step. | [AHA: BIAB](https://www.homebrewersassociation.org/wp-content/uploads/How-To-Brew-in-a-Bag.pdf) |\n\nA concentrated/partial-volume boil with later top-up is a separate volume choice from partial **mash**.\nExtract additions may occur at more than one stage. Conversion between extract and grain requires\nrecipe and equipment assumptions; it is not a universal substitution factor. These distinctions are\nsupported by [AHA's extract discussion](https://www.homebrewersassociation.org/how-to-brew/working-with-extract-recipes/).\nPre-hopped kits are also not assumed to require the same hop-boil sequence as unhopped extract;\nkit-specific instructions remain necessary rather than inventing a universal no-boil recipe.\n\n## Equipment families and the named systems\n\n| Layout | Observed workflow distinction | Equipment/profile data Mealshop needs | Source |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Bag + kettle (DIY BIAB) | The bag separates grain; there need not be a separate mash/lauter vessel. | Kettle and bag identity, user-declared working capacity, lift/drain phase, chosen water/boil plan. | [AHA BIAB](https://www.homebrewersassociation.org/wp-content/uploads/How-To-Brew-in-a-Bag.pdf) |\n| Grainfather G30 example | A removable grain basket, top-return recirculation, basket lifting/draining and separately described sparging. | Actual model/generation, vessel/pump resource, basket-removal phase, optional sparge-water vessel and actual chiller. | [Manufacturer G30 English manual](https://help.grainfather.com/hc/en-us/article_attachments/15548590179345), pp. 7–10; [manual index](https://help.grainfather.com/hc/en-us/articles/360011864078-Instructions-G30) |\n| Brew Monk B40/B50/B70 example | Malt pipe/filter plates, circulation pipe and pump with adjustable return; hardware and operating modes have their own instructions. | Actual model, vessel/pump resource, malt-pipe handling and independently declared settings. | [Brouwland manufacturer/distributor manual](https://brouwland.com/brldocs/057.702.xx_BrewMonk_Manual.pdf), English section pp. 18–25 |\n| Speidel Braumeister PLUS example | Wort rises through the malt pipe and overflows; pump pauses and malt-pipe removal belong to this system's process. | Distinct upward-flow profile, actual model, malt-pipe release/removal and available cooling equipment. | [Speidel original English manual](https://www.speidels-braumeister.de/en/service/instruction-manuals/original-instruction-manual-speidels-braumeister-plus-10-20-50-100-litres.html), §§6.4–6.6, manual dated September 2020 |\n| Cooler mash/lauter tun + kettle | A separate insulated mash/lauter vessel retains grain; wort is collected into the boiling vessel. | Mash tun, kettle and, if used, sparge-water vessel; transfers and actual heating plan. | [AHA all-grain tutorial](https://homebrewersassociation.org/tutorials/all-grain-batch-sparge-homebrewing/all-grain-batch-sparge-homebrewing/) |\n| Two-vessel recirculating setup | Vessel count alone does not define the heat/recirculation path. Two-vessel kettle-RIMS is one concrete implementation. | Separate resource IDs and explicit circulation/heat method instead of assuming a three-vessel layout. | [Blichmann BrewEasy](https://www.blichmannengineering.com/product/breweasy-compact-all-grain-brewing-system/) |\n| Three-vessel DIY | Hot-liquor tank, mash/lauter tun and boil kettle have distinct roles. | Separate reservations and connections; the same phase can need several resources. | [The Electric Brewery's own three-vessel design](https://shop.theelectricbrewery.com/pages/kettles-overview) |\n| HERMS | Recirculating wort receives heat through a heat exchanger in the hot-liquor tank. | Mash vessel, pump and HLT/heat-exchanger resources; recipe-specific rest/ramp entries. | [Builder's HLT/HERMS design](https://shop.theelectricbrewery.com/pages/hot-liquor-tank) |\n| RIMS | Recirculating wort passes a direct heat source in the loop. | Explicit loop/pump/heater resource identities and the actual instrument's documentation. | [Blichmann controller manual, sensor placement](https://www.blichmannengineering.com/wp-content/uploads/UpdatedManuals/Electric_TOP_240V_Controller_Manual_V3_0.pdf); [builder's HERMS/RIMS comparison](https://shop.theelectricbrewery.com/pages/faq-herms-vs-rims) |\n\nBrand names are examples of equipment families, not separate fermentation chemistries or claims of\nremote control support. G30 generations and other Grainfather models have separate manuals. The app\nmust not copy one model's capacities, plumbing or chiller assumptions onto every device with the same\nbrand. DIY profiles need editable resources; a brand preset cannot describe every custom build.\n\n## Independent choices\n\n- **Mash programme:** single infusion, multiple rests/step mash or decoction. Decoction removes a\n  portion to another vessel and returns it; that transfer/resource must be representable. This is\n  independent of whether the wort is later batch- or fly-sparged. [AHA: decoction](https://www.homebrewersassociation.org/zymurgy-article/decoction-mashing-history-and-modern-approaches/)\n- **Lautering/sparging:** no sparge; one or more batch rinses after drainage; or continuous/fly sparge\n  while collecting wort. Preserve the chosen water volumes and repeated phases. Do not claim that a\n  particular efficiency always follows from a technique. [AHA: sparging comparison](https://www.homebrewersassociation.org/how-to-brew/fly-sparing-vs-batch-sparging/)\n- **Heating:** insulated/manual, direct heat, HERMS or RIMS. Record the user's actual configuration;\n  the software schedules actions and records readings, not electrical or pump control.\n- **Boil/volume:** full volume versus concentrated boil/top-up, with extract and hop additions attached\n  to explicit phases. No automatic water, bitterness or extract conversions are inferred.\n- **Cooling and fermentation:** independently assigned equipment and editable phases. Elapsed time is\n  an observation/reminder, not a declaration that fermentation or packaging is complete.\n\n## Implementation interpretation\n\nThe following is Mealshop's design derived from the researched distinctions, not a manufacturer's API:\n\n1. Store fermentable strategy, vessel layout, heating and sparging as independent typed choices.\n   Offer understandable starter templates for the named systems and DIY layouts without a combinatorial\n   list of nearly identical recipes. BIAB remains identifiable within all-grain.\n2. Keep user brew recipes and immutable batch snapshots separate from food-recipe servings/nutrition.\n   A batch has an exact target volume and its own measured ingredients, phases and equipment bindings.\n3. Reuse the durable timer/timezone/reminder engine. Preview actual dates, including an optional explicit\n   yeast-preparation reminder before brew day; never prescribe one universal yeast handling schedule.\n4. Record raw readings with original units, measurement timestamp, manual/device attribution and known\n   or unknown calibration. Do not silently equate SG, Plato and Brix or temperature-correct them.\n5. Check actual equipment reservations across batches and keep their operation/reminder identities\n   independent. Editing one batch must not reset another's timers or original readings.\n6. Cider, white wine, red wine and traditional mead have independently reviewed templates below.\n   Their typed processes contain no grain/layout/heat/sparge classifications.\n\n## Source use and limits\n\nSources were read online on 2026-11-13. URLs and the identified manual/model are retained above;\nmanufacturer content remains copyrighted. The implementation uses original phase labels/brief prompts\nand links, not reproduced manuals, diagrams, recipe text or example temperature/time programmes.\nOlder manuals' legal, nutritional and troubleshooting claims are not adopted as current application\nrules. Model capacities and recipes must remain explicitly supplied/reviewed facts. No comparative\nproduct recommendation or claim that one brewing method is superior is made here.\n\n## Measurement and yeast handling references\n\nOriginal instrument calibration is part of the observation, not an application-wide correction.\nThe [iSpindel calibration documentation](https://www.ispindel.de/docs/Calibration_en.html) describes\ninstrument-specific calibration; [Tilt's manufacturer FAQ](https://tilthydrometer.com/pages/faqs)\ndescribes its device readings and calibration. The model therefore retains the raw unit, source,\ncalibration declaration and separate measurement/entry times, without assuming a configured connector.\n[Fermentis' product-specific usage guidance](https://fermentis.com/en/easy-2-use/) is another reason\nnot to impose a universal day-before yeast treatment. F14 offers an explicitly authored recipe reminder,\nresolved by local calendar day, instead. These are primary-reference-informed implementation decisions;\nno numeric beer chemistry, device credential contract or food-safety conclusion is inferred.\n\n## Independent validation of cider, wine and mead\n\nPrimary references independently reviewed on 2026-11-13; the resulting labels/prompts are Mealshop's\noriginal implementation decisions. Validation here means source review of the process distinctions,\nnot certification or a guarantee that a user's recipe is safe or fermentation complete.\n\n| Template and boundary | Source-supported distinction | Primary reference and version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Cider from prepared apple juice | Juice preparation, yeast, observations, transfer and maturation; no grain conversion. Whole-fruit milling/pressing is outside this juice starter template. | [AHA, How to Make Cider](https://homebrewersassociation.org/tutorials/how-to-make-cider/how-to-make-cider/), 2018-02-15 |\n| Traditional mead without boiling | Honey dissolved in water, yeast, recipe-specific nutrition/observations, transfer and maturation. No malt conversion or mandatory wort boil. | [AHA, How to Make Mead](https://homebrewersassociation.org/tutorials/how-to-make-mead/how-to-make-mead/), 2018-02-07 |\n| White wine from grapes | Press/separate solids **before** juice fermentation; then transfer and maturation. | [MoreWine, Guide to White Winemaking](https://morewinemaking.com/web_files/intranet.morebeer.com/files/wwhiw.pdf), copyright 2009, chapters 2–4, 6–7 |\n| Red wine with skin fermentation | Crush/prepare grapes, ferment on skins with recipe-specific cap management, then press, transfer and mature. | [MoreWine, Guide to Red Winemaking](https://morewinemaking.com/web_files/intranet.morebeer.com/files/wredw.pdf), copyright 2008, chapters 2–4, 7–9 |\n\nNo universal temperature, elapsed fermentation duration, yeast nutrition, acid/sulphite dose, stabilization,\nmalolactic treatment, priming or packaging readiness is inferred. Additional repeated observation/addition\nphases and source instructions can be authored explicitly in the recipe. Readings retain original SG,\nPlato/Brix or temperature units; conversion, alcohol and chemistry calculators are not introduced.\nThese four templates reuse only neutral scheduling, equipment reservation and provenance mechanisms.\nBackend validation rejects grain-method fields masquerading as non-beer processes; beverage identity\nsurvives batch snapshots, restart and idempotent replay. Old beer/food wire formats remain unchanged.\n"]
pub mod page_specs_0014_brewing_fermentation_research_md {}

#[doc = "# Brewing and fermentation workflows\n\nStatus: Done\nPriority: Could\nOrigin: Customer\nMilestone: M4\n\n## User value\nUse configurable processes and equipment for beer, then cider, wine and mead.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n1. Given beer brewing, separate process templates support brew-in-a-bag, all-grain and extract with equipment-specific steps and measurements.\n2. Given a brew day, a recipe may schedule a yeast-out-of-fridge reminder the day before and further phase-specific timers.\n3. Given readings from a hydrometer/temperature device or manual input, units, calibration, timestamp and source are retained.\n4. Given several simultaneous batches, their equipment reservations, stages and reminders remain independent.\n5. Given cider/wine/mead, additional templates are introduced only after domain validation; beer assumptions do not silently carry over.\n\n## Out of scope\nAutomatic heater/pump control, food-safety guarantees or unvalidated non-beer chemistry.\n\n## UX notes\nSee [ux-notes.md](../page_specs_0014_brewing_fermentation_ux_notes_md/index.html) for native layout, review, recovery and accessibility.\n\n## Links\nRequirement: [F14](../page_docs_requirements_md/index.html). Delivery tasks: [plan.md](../page_specs_0014_brewing_fermentation_plan_md/index.html). Decisions: [adrs.md](../page_specs_0014_brewing_fermentation_adrs_md/index.html).\n\n## Customer research clarification and delivery contract\n\nThe customer requested internet research on BIAB, Grainfather, Brew Monk, Speidel Braumeister, DIY\nall-grain, extract and partial mash on 2026-11-13. [research.md](../page_specs_0014_brewing_fermentation_research_md/index.html) retains primary sources\nand separates those facts from implementation choices. BIAB is represented within all-grain;\nfermentable strategy, vessel layout, heating and sparging are distinct.\n\nThe native delivery includes beer, cider, wine and mead recipes with immutable revisions and exact target liquid\nvolume/entered ingredients, source-linked editable phase templates, explicit equipment bindings,\nreviewed independent batches and phase reminders. Readings retain raw units, measurement/entry\ntimestamps, manual/instrument attribution and known or unknown calibration; corrections preserve\nthe original. Manual instrument transcription is supported without claiming a live connector.\n\nUse the existing durable scheduler and atomic household file with an honest food/brew source boundary.\nNo food servings are repurposed as litres, no fake food recipe is created and no automatic stock\nconsumption or chemistry calculation is inferred. Entered yeast instructions/times remain recipe-\nspecific. Independently reviewed cider-juice, white-wine, red-wine and traditional-mead templates\nuse their own process classifications; see the primary-source validation in research.md.\n\nDecision: [ADR 0025](../page_docs_adr_0025_native_brewing_processes_md/index.html).\n\n## Acceptance evidence\n\nSee [validation.md](../page_specs_0014_brewing_fermentation_validation_md/index.html) for all five criteria, source review, real Nextcloud/browser/restart\nchecks, identified defects and their passing repeats, privacy-preserving cleanup and publication gates.\n"]
pub mod page_specs_0014_brewing_fermentation_specification_md {}

#[doc = "# Native brewing workflow\n\nUse the existing Nextcloud shell, components, theme tokens, MDI icons, responsive typography and keyed\nEN/DE messages. UX-UI Pro Max informs hierarchy and recovery; Taste informs compact alignment and clear\nactions, under the established native design hierarchy. No marketing layout, extra fonts or UI framework.\n\nA dedicated Brewing navigation entry separates batches, brew recipes and equipment. The empty state\nstarts with a source-linked process template. Explain method versus equipment without making users\nchoose between overlapping labels such as \"all-grain\" and \"BIAB\". Templates name the source/model and\nprovide editable phase structure, with no invented universal rest temperatures or fermentation duration.\n\nRecipe editing keeps batch volume, measured ingredients, method and equipment together. Phases stay\nopen and show the main timing/resource fields compactly; optional details use explicit plus actions.\nUsers can add/edit phases, their explicit dependencies, actual extract/hop additions and measurements without leaving context.\nSave an immutable revision; changing a recipe must not rewrite a running batch's recipe snapshot.\n\nStarting a batch chooses a saved revision, brew-day civil time, optional explicit yeast-preparation\nreminder and concrete equipment. Review resolved dates/timezones, uncertainty, resource conflicts and\nmissing calibration/values before confirmation. Unknown is not zero and a brand is not a verified\ncapacity. Conflicts retain the draft and permit corrected dates/resources; no silent scheduling override.\n\nBatch detail shows independent phase timers, equipment and original recipe, personal reminder delivery\nstate and exact readings. Elapsed time does not mark fermentation or packaging complete. Actual running/\npaused phases retain equipment until explicitly completed or cancelled. Pausing one batch does not pause\nanother. Cancellation and corrections require explicit confirmation and preserve original observations.\n\nReading entry is compact: phase, raw value/unit, measurement time, manual or named instrument attribution\nand known/unknown calibration. Recording a device transcription must not suggest a live connection.\nDisplay original units and calibration; never silently convert SG/Plato/Brix, correct alcohol influence\nor infer completion from readings. A reasoned correction preserves the earlier record.\n\nReaders inspect without editing, operating timers or subscribing. Protect dirty drafts, retain rejected\nsaves, reject stale revisions and retry ambiguous confirmed requests without duplication. Ignore late\nresponses after navigation. Offline views remain visibly stale and read-only until reconnect.\n\nVerify keyboard focus, labels/errors/status announcements, responsive overflow and axe WCAG 2.2 AA\nin real Nextcloud owner/reader flows, EN/DE, light/dark and 1440/390/320 px. Use only ledger-owned\nsynthetic households and stop the sole writer before targeted cleanup; never inspect retained folders.\n\n## Validated final refinements\n\nGroup starter templates by beverage. Cider, white/red wine and mead show their own process name, with\nbeer-method controls reserved for beer. All phases stay open. Saving a recipe or batch navigates by the\nreceipt's exact result ID, including duplicate titles and uncertain-response retries.\n\nAt narrow widths, stack fact labels above values to keep long German words and exact quantities legible.\nKeep tab targets distinct. Validate text bounds as well as page overflow and axe in both languages,\nboth themes and 1440/390/320 px. Retain all accessibility rules and wait for native theme transitions\nbefore measuring contrast. Screenshots complement the automated checks.\n"]
pub mod page_specs_0014_brewing_fermentation_ux_notes_md {}

#[doc = "# F14 acceptance evidence\n\nDate: 2026-11-13. Functional implementation: `0a9a270`, with final scoped CSS refinement, on\n`story/brewing-and-fermentation`, PR #37\nagainst `main` (`fe642d9`). Only explicitly recorded synthetic fixtures were used. No retained household\ndirectory was inspected, copied, hashed or reset during this feature's validation.\n\n## Contract coverage\n\n| Criterion | Delivered evidence |\n| --- | --- |\n| AC 1: distinct brewing methods and equipment | Twelve primary-source-linked beer templates preserve extract/steeping/partial-mash/all-grain distinctions, BIAB and named/DIY equipment. Exact liquid/ingredient quantities, declared phase temperatures/volumes, editable expanded phases and immutable recipe revisions are covered by catalogue, HTTP and native authoring tests. |\n| AC 2: pre-brew and phase reminders | Recipe-specific calendar-day preparation, DST's 23-hour interval, phase deadlines, explicit manual completion and the existing private opt-in delivery worker are tested. A 40-day active fermentation deadline survives an installed-container restart. |\n| AC 3: original measurements | Raw six-place SG/Brix/temperature/volume strings, original unit, measured civil/absolute time, authenticated entry actor/time, manual/transcribed/reported attribution, known/unknown calibration and reasoned append-only corrections survive replay/restart. No conversion or configured live device connector is inferred. |\n| AC 4: independent batches and reservations | Atomic reviewed reservations, exact phase-volume capacity checks, frozen equipment snapshots, concurrent equipment edits, long-running/paused physical holds and cross-beverage resource conflicts are tested. Explicit completion releases the resource; an elapsed observation timer does not. |\n| AC 5: independent fermentation validation | [Primary references](../page_specs_0014_brewing_fermentation_research_md/index.html#independent-validation-of-cider-wine-and-mead) independently distinguish prepared-juice cider, white grape wine, red wine fermented on skins and traditional no-boil mead. Their typed processes omit beer-method fields. Incorrect beverage/method/template combinations and grain-mash phases are rejected; all four workflows pass real native authoring, batch and original-reading checks. Source review is not recipe certification or a safety guarantee. |\n\n## Local quality gate\n\nAll commands below passed on the final functional implementation: **181 Rust tests**, **36 native client\ncontracts**, strict TypeScript/Vite, strict rustdoc, and unchanged dependency manifests/lockfiles.\nThe mutation experiment inverted the beverage/process guard, observed the relevant integration test fail,\nrestored the guard and then ran the complete passing gate.\n\n```sh\npython3 scripts/generate_docs.py --check\npython3 scripts/import_catalogue.py --check\npython3 scripts/verify_catalogue.py\npython3 scripts/import_nutrition_estimates.py --check\npython3 scripts/verify_nutrition_estimates.py\npython3 scripts/verify_conversions.py\npython3 scripts/verify_allergens.py\nnode --check src/web/demo.mjs\nnode --test tests/i18n.test.mjs\ncargo fmt --all --check\ncargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings\ncargo nextest run --workspace --locked\ncargo deny check licenses\ncargo audit\nnpm --prefix clients/nextcloud run format:check\nnpm --prefix clients/nextcloud test\nnpm --prefix clients/nextcloud run build\nnpm --prefix clients/nextcloud audit --audit-level=moderate\nnode scripts/generate_mcp_schema.mjs --check\npython3 -m unittest discover -s tests -p test_mcp_stdio.py\ncargo build --locked\nnode scripts/test_mcp_client.mjs\ncargo doc --workspace --no-deps --locked\n```\n\n## Real installation and browser checks\n\nNextcloud **35.0.2**, `AppAPI` **35.0.0**, Chromium, existing embedded and household proxy paths.\nInstalled implementation image:\n`sha256:d7437c3e86c7ee93a92701ea6e30219424c8fd397c0903e5193aa2975242bd75`.\nAuthenticated public asset responses match the container hashes: JS **1,660,580 bytes**, CSS\n**174,759 bytes**. The production build reports JS **498.94 kB gzip**, CSS **25.00 kB gzip**;\nthese are total artifact sizes, not a runtime performance benchmark. No dependencies were added.\n\nRootless, read-only, dropped-capability container acceptance passes over **TCP and Unix sockets** with\nlifecycle/auth/CSRF, existing food workflows, imports/sharing/MCP/inventory and the new source-linked\nbrew/fermentation workflows. Fresh and replayed mutations return the exact receipt result ID.\n\nThe actual installed container was restarted with five independently active beverage workflows on\nimage `79d72ff…`; the final CSS-only image has byte-identical server and JavaScript artifacts. Original\n40-day deadlines, exact liquid quantity/capacity, reported device calibration, original/corrected beer\nreadings, four original Brix readings and old-revision receipts/result IDs survived without duplication.\nA conflicting future equipment reservation remains rejected. The separate food reference and three\nfood servings remain unchanged. The synthetic household's persisted file is mode `0600`.\n\nBrowser runs use one worker and ledger-owned homes. No tests are skipped. Batches are separated by\nowned-fixture cleanup to respect the household limit. Initial findings and their targeted reruns are\nretained rather than claiming an uninterrupted all-green invocation:\n\n| Run | Image / scope | Result |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| A | Earlier complete implementation `2ae33b9…`: authoring, brewing, catalogue, compact recipes, cooking, daily workflows, ingredient groups, Nextcloud, nutrition, theme, preparation and usability | 19/21 passed. The new duplicate-title case and a cooking theme-transition audit failed; both were addressed below. |\n| Targeted repeat | Installed `79d72ff…`: all five brewing cases plus the complete cooking case | **6/6 passed**. All four drink templates deliberately reuse the same recipe title; each correct immutable recipe, phase count, batch type and original Brix reading is verified after reload. |\n| B | Installed `79d72ff…`: feedback/history, MCP, sharing and shopping | Eight cases passed before the browser process received SIGTERM; the four remaining cases passed separately on CSS image `10d8512…`: **12/12 distinct cases passed**. |\n| C | CSS image `10d8512…`: inventory and recipe import | **8/8 passed**. |\n| Final visual repeat | Final stacked CSS image `d7437c3…`: complete native authoring, empty states and four fermentation templates | **3/3 passed**, including text bounds checks in all 48 scene/language/theme/width combinations. |\n\nThe duplicate-title finding was a product defect: selecting a saved recipe by title could open an older\nsame-named recipe. Both fresh and replayed API responses now return their durable `result_id`; recipe\nand batch navigation use that exact ID. The cooking audit was sampling a native theme transition;\nit now waits for font/layout frames and finite animations, retaining all contrast rules. The existing\ninventory purchase test waits for the canonical purchase acknowledgement before navigation, rather\nthan an optimistic checkbox update. Manual screenshot review also found long German fact labels overlapping values at 320 px. Scoped\nnormal wrapping, stacked mobile facts and native tab spacing fix this; the visual audit now checks text bounds as well as\napplication overflow and axe. Credential-bearing network tracing is disabled; only synthetic\nscreenshots/error contexts are retained locally.\n\nAll F14 recipe, batch, empty-equipment and red-wine views pass axe's WCAG 2/2.1/2.2 A/AA rules in\n**EN/DE × light/dark × 1440/390/320 px**, with no application horizontal overflow. Synthetic screenshots\nwere inspected at desktop and narrow dark layouts. Automated checks support the accessibility evidence;\nthey do not establish full manual WCAG conformance. Keyboard, owner/reader, offline, stale-write,\nuncertain-save and empty-state flows are exercised through the native UI.\n\n## Final publication and cleanup\n\nAll **41 distinct browser scenarios** passed across the recorded runs, with targeted repeats after each\nfinding. The final scoped CSS has an additional passing three-case visual repeat. Final fixture cleanup\nleft **zero active ledger-owned households**, preserving every non-ledger household and allocation counter;\nretained household directories were not inspected. The exact final image remains healthy.\n\nThe final change after the functional commit contains only the tested scoped CSS, its text-bounds audit,\nand documentation/generated rustdoc metadata. All mandatory gates are rerun before push. All eleven CI\nchecks must pass on the final PR head before review handoff; the PR's live checks are authoritative.\nThe installed image and artifact identities remain explicit above rather than implying every earlier\nbrowser invocation used the same image.\n"]
pub mod page_specs_0014_brewing_fermentation_validation_md {}

